<graph edgedefault="undirected"><node id="Statisch Bestimmte Stabsysteme">
  <data key="d0">Statisch Bestimmte Stabsysteme</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Statically determinate bar systems in which bar forces, stresses, strains, displacements, and length changes can be determined sequentially from equilibrium conditions, elasticity law, and kinematics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784894368</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statisch Unbestimmte Stabsysteme">
  <data key="d0">Statisch Unbestimmte Stabsysteme</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Statically indeterminate bar systems in which equilibrium conditions, elasticity law, and kinematics must be considered simultaneously; temperature changes typically cause thermal stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Statically indeterminate bar systems, a topic covered in the German engineering mechanics lecture document.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fed5dfab36680bc0a576b0ed9cc7e5a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hookesches Gesetz">
  <data key="d0">Hookesches Gesetz</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hooke's Law, a material model relating normal stress to strain via the elastic modulus, expressed as sigma equals E times epsilon.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784894368</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normalspannung">
  <data key="d0">Normalspannung</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Normal stress in a cross-section perpendicular to the bar axis, calculated as sigma equals N divided by A.&lt;SEP&gt;The stress component acting perpendicular to the cross-section, which can be positive (tensile stress) or negative (compressive stress).&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental stress concept related to normal stress.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress component acting normal to the cutting surface, causing tension or pressure on an infinitesimal volume element.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67c14bdfc0522f2a0b7438b9c789bafe&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784900562</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dehnung">
  <data key="d0">Dehnung</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dehnung is the German term for strain, representing a foundational concept in the mechanics of materials and deformation mechanics. Serving as an umbrella topic for kinematic quantities, it objectively describes the geometric changes and relative deformation of an elastic body. In the context of technical literature and structural analysis, the study of Dehnung is typically introduced immediately following a discussion of stress.

Technically, Dehnung is defined as the ratio of the change in length to the original length of a material. Mathematically denoted by epsilon (ε), it is formulated by examining how displacement changes across a differential length within a rod element, expressed as epsilon equals du divided by dx (where epsilon is much less than 1). In practical applications, such as material testing, this relative deformation is calculated from the displacement measured by an extensometer. 

In educational and textbook contexts, Dehnung is a key technical topic in the study of structural bars, notably serving as a foundational pillar of "Chapter 10" and specifically covered in "section 10.2." This instructional section details the technical strain definition and examines local strain, with a particular focus on structural bars that have variable cross-sections. Whether analyzed theoretically in a rod element or referenced in the surrounding textual context of a diagram, the term is consistently used synonymously with local or technical strain to quantify material deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b0e3f83f75ba130c72c201a9b9da1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5281233f3c19ef2742208835bb41bd76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23f9a8f34af690fb6d5cb0ea12f409a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba3dc6bb30208edf4354dd20376fa9f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cebf917adc3d92ce2d5e155d10154396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40c9b6d63b8c22a4bba35a9a76979235&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896865</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Längenänderung">
  <data key="d0">Längenänderung</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Change in length of a bar, calculated via the integral of normal force over tensile stiffness plus thermal expansion over the length of the bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784894368</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gleichgewichtsbedingung">
  <data key="d0">Gleichgewichtsbedingung</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Equilibrium conditions used at nodes or on the undeformed system to determine bar forces and internal forces.&lt;SEP&gt;The equilibrium condition, one of three fundamental equation systems used for analyzing axially-loaded structural members.&lt;SEP&gt;The equilibrium condition relating internal forces and external distributed loads in structural bars, visually represented by the diagram under section 10.4 Einzelstab.&lt;SEP&gt;German term for equilibrium condition, referring to the x-direction force balance requirement for the infinitesimal element in plane stress state</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45f0f3e4c4e1775f77972c4268710cbb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastizitätsgesetz">
  <data key="d0">Elastizitätsgesetz</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Elasticity law relating stress and strain in structural analysis of bar systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The constitutive law equation type based on Hooke's model with modulus of elasticity E.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e2db8e0cc453f4ff50201dc37d1fa0c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematik">
  <data key="d0">Kinematik</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Kinematics describing geometric displacement relationships, used together with equilibrium and elasticity law especially for statically indeterminate systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The study of the geometry of deformation, using kinematic quantities like displacement and strain.&lt;SEP&gt;Kinematics, a section introducing displacement u(x) and strain ε as kinematic quantities describing deformation geometry.&lt;SEP&gt;The study of motion and deformation, conceptually bridging basic geometry with the topic of strain.&lt;SEP&gt;The kinematics equations, one of three fundamental equation systems describing geometric relationships in structural member analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;One of three foundational equation types, defining the kinematic relationship between displacement u(x) and strain ε(x).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23f9a8f34af690fb6d5cb0ea12f409a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40c9b6d63b8c22a4bba35a9a76979235&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e2db8e0cc453f4ff50201dc37d1fa0c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dehnsteifigkeit">
  <data key="d0">Dehnsteifigkeit</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tensile stiffness denoted EA, representing a bar's resistance to axial deformation; both bars in the example have equal tensile stiffness.&lt;SEP&gt;The axial stiffness (EA), defined as the product of Young's modulus and cross-sectional area, derived from combining the constitutive law with equilibrium considerations.&lt;SEP&gt;A term meaning "tensile stiffness", representing a special case in the analysis of rods.&lt;SEP&gt;Tensile stiffness, a specific elastostatics topic covered in the referenced textbook.&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for axial stiffness, mentioned in the text in relation to EA1 and EA2.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f2ecbe17c739fbd7bdf88a5cb116b3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7401c00a8316a2124dcb1cf703b5f16d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Punkt C">
  <data key="d0">Punkt C</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A node in a statically determinate bar system at which displacement is sought under loading conditions.</data>
