<node id="Equilibrium Condition">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Equilibrium Condition, also known by its German term Gleichgewichtsbedingung, is the first of three fundamental equation types required for structural analysis and solving bar problems. Serving as a governing principle that represents the static balance of forces on a structural element, it is essential for determining the stresses and deformations of a single bar. Specifically, the Equilibrium Condition establishes a relationship between the internal normal force and external loads acting upon the structure.

In the analysis of a single bar, the Equilibrium Condition is utilized to yield the internal axial force, commonly denoted as N. For instances involving a constant axial force, this relationship is expressed as N = F, which serves as a fundamental step in deriving the elongation formula for the structural element. When analyzing bars subjected to varying loads, the Equilibrium Condition is expressed as a differential equation, N'(x) = -n(x). This principle dictates that the spatial derivative of the internal normal force is equal to the negative of the applied distributed load.

Furthermore, the Equilibrium Condition encompasses discrete force balance equations applied at specific structural connections, such as node K. An example of such a nodal force balance is represented by the equation S₁ cos α + S₂ + S₃ cos α - F = 0. These specific algebraic conditions, which can also yield simplified relationships such as B - C = 0 (concluding that B = C), are derived directly from the static analysis of the structural system. Ultimately, these equilibrium equations are used in conjunction with compatibility conditions to fully solve and describe the structural system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-738c698a563cc3405e3c1de5794fa318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f72457639c5767731980d0c54a4fe069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-413d03b5c0b6ff01a4da279c609d09f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-635877c2ca4bd8652a8de3e6ef23d5f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematics">
  <data key="d0">Kinematics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Kinematics, also referred to as Kinematik, is a fundamental branch of engineering mechanics that studies the motion of points and bodies without explicit reference to the forces that cause them. Covered extensively in technical mechanics lectures and foundational engineering texts such as Technische Mechanik 2, the field broadly addresses the motion of objects. In the specialized context of solid mechanics and structural analysis, kinematics narrows its focus to the geometry of deformation. It specifically investigates the motion of deformation and the displacement field, serving as a critical conceptual step that precedes and connects directly to constitutive laws.

In structural mechanics, kinematics constitutes the second of three fundamental equation types required to determine the stresses and deformations of a single bar (Einzelstab). As a governing principle in bar element analysis, it geometrically relates axial displacement to strain. This relationship is mathematically defined by the derivative of displacement with respect to position, expressed through formulas such as ε=du/dx and the corresponding elongation formula Δl=∫εdx. By dealing with the geometric derivation of strain from the displacement field, kinematics provides the essential framework for deriving structural elongations.

Furthermore, kinematics establishes the geometric compatibility conditions necessary for evaluating complex structural systems. It is recognized as one of the three core conditions that must be considered simultaneously when analyzing statically indeterminate bar systems. In practical applications, kinematics defines the geometric relationships between different structural movements, such as linking vertical displacement and horizontal displacement to overall bar elongations. For instance, it provides the analytical framework required to project the vertical displacement of a specific point, such as node K, onto inclined bars, thereby contributing essential variables to the governing structural formulas.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6780202a7476f0626124683cb4683f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-738c698a563cc3405e3c1de5794fa318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-039628e7ccab30fb4810eab64b589dc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17dfe04089064853421a9f1af5f1cb77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad1fe24f4724db937ef65aa2cd2c2fab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba3dc6bb30208edf4354dd20376fa9f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3cdf7e6c0e0814f6340adcf0330c36f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9966746921724686a393e234327d9e41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-055d89277cf2e87034384489ad8dc567&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-151ad67dcab298d7226da28fe7afb340&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b35bef5073db0c5a31b6dd2e83c0ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab59a548a45f0f75802088fe89a02c30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-413d03b5c0b6ff01a4da279c609d09f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-635877c2ca4bd8652a8de3e6ef23d5f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1a7b2afc01b89dc35586063ca901c7f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896884</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elasticity Model">
  <data key="d0">Elasticity Model</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Elasticity Model, also known by its German terms Elastizitätsgesetz or Elastizitätsmodell, and commonly referred to as the constitutive law, is a theoretical model and governing equation type used to define material behavior. It primarily dictates the relationship between stress and strain, frequently utilizing Hooke's law to characterize how materials respond to applied forces.

In the context of structural mechanics, particularly regarding the analysis of tension and compression in bars, the Elasticity Model serves as the third fundamental equation type necessary for evaluating a single bar (Einzelstab). It is an essential component of a three-equation framework—operating alongside kinematics and equilibrium—that is strictly required to solve statically indeterminate problems, calculate axial stiffness, and determine overall stresses and deformations in structural components.

Mathematically, this model is one of the three fundamental relationships used to derive the elongation formula for a bar. This derivation is accomplished by combining the normal stress equation ($\sigma=N/A$) with the strain equation ($\varepsilon=\sigma/E$). Because of its foundational role in mechanics, learning to properly apply the Elasticity Model is a key educational objective for students, particularly within dedicated curriculum sections such as Section 10.4 Einzelstab.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6780202a7476f0626124683cb4683f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-738c698a563cc3405e3c1de5794fa318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-039628e7ccab30fb4810eab64b589dc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0dae0b4038721550e833d3e9407ccd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-635877c2ca4bd8652a8de3e6ef23d5f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Stiffness">
  <data key="d0">Axial Stiffness</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Axial stiffness, also known as axial rigidity or by its German equivalent Dehnsteifigkeit, is a fundamental mechanical property that quantifies a structural member's resistance to axial deformation. It is universally denoted as EA and is mathematically calculated as the product of a material's Young's modulus, also referred to as the elastic modulus (E), and its cross-sectional area (A). By combining these two factors, axial stiffness effectively represents the integrated material and geometric stiffness of a bar's cross-section.

As a critical parameter in structural mechanics, axial stiffness is predominantly utilized to analyze uniaxial bar elements, rods, and structural frameworks. It plays an essential role in the final bar deformation equation, allowing engineers to accurately determine the extent to which a structural member will deform under an applied load. In complex structural assessments, such as when evaluating a compound rod, the specific axial stiffness (EA) of each individual segment is taken into consideration. Furthermore, in specific analytical models and problem setups like a two-bar system, the axial rigidity (EA) of the respective components is sometimes assumed to be equal to streamline calculations and comparative analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad1fe24f4724db937ef65aa2cd2c2fab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdbe00f1344b6e8391f5aa4709a1d925&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a093ad0a488172322e66eed43c540981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f862d3f8b712d95184fc656859d1e6cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c71f393772a794391e49730d47a8bda4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-635877c2ca4bd8652a8de3e6ef23d5f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Determinate Problem">
  <data key="d0">Statically Determinate Problem</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Problem type where the normal force N(x) is determinable using equilibrium conditions alone&lt;SEP&gt;A structural problem where the internal normal force can be found from equilibrium conditions alone.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Indeterminate Problem">
  <data key="d0">Statically Indeterminate Problem</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Problem type where the normal force N(x) cannot be determined by equilibrium conditions alone; requires equilibrium, kinematics, and elasticity law&lt;SEP&gt;A structural problem that requires solving equilibrium, kinematics, and the elasticity model simultaneously.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Strain">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Thermal Strain is the component of total strain caused by temperature variations, a concept also referred to as Wärmedehnung. Mathematically, this strain component is denoted as ε_T and is defined as the product of the coefficient of linear thermal expansion (α_T) and the temperature change (ΔT). This formulation effectively captures the free thermal expansion experienced by a material as a result of temperature fluctuations.

In the context of engineering and structural mechanics, Thermal Strain is a critical factor that must be incorporated into structural analysis when evaluating cases involving thermal loading. The structural impact of Thermal Strain depends heavily on the determinacy of the system. Specifically, it produces no additional stresses in statically determinate problems, as these structures are free to expand or contract. However, in statically indeterminate problems where movement is constrained, Thermal Strain induces additional internal stresses that must be carefully accounted for during analysis and design.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-738c698a563cc3405e3c1de5794fa318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic Strain">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Plastische Dehnung ε_pl that remains after complete unloading when stress exceeds the yield stress σ_F&lt;SEP&gt;Denoted by ε_pl, the permanent strain that occurs when yield stress is exceeded.&lt;SEP&gt;The permanent, non-recoverable deformation remaining in the material after unloading, marked as $\varepsilon_{pl}$.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Superposition">
  <data key="d0">Superposition</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Alternative solution method for statically indeterminate problems combining displacements from different systems: u_c = u_c^(0) + u_c^(1)&lt;SEP&gt;A method used in elastostatics where the solution combines a statically determinate system with a corrective system.&lt;SEP&gt;An alternative solution method used for analyzing statically determinate bar systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71dad38576055c6e013578d06064a913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896964</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Force">
  <data key="d0">Normal Force</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Normal Force, also referred to as Normalkraft, is the internal axial force or resultant that acts along the longitudinal axis of a structural member, such as a bar or a rod. Denoted typically as N or N(x) to represent its value at a specific position x, this force can manifest as either tensile or compressive. It is a critical component in structural mechanics, essential for determining the internal stresses and deformations within the material. At the endpoints of a bar segment, this internal axial force may also be represented as F1 and F2 within equilibrium equations.

The Normal Force is commonly determined using the method of sections, known in German as the Schnittverfahren. In statically determinate structures, the normal force N(x) is directly calculable using equilibrium conditions related to external loads. For instance, it can remain constant along a rod under the equilibrium condition B − C = 0, where it acts as a support reaction. Mathematically, at a given position x, the internal normal force is represented by the equation E·A·u', meaning it equals the rod's entire weight G at the position x=0 and drops to zero at the end of the rod's length x=l. Conversely, in statically indeterminate problems where equilibrium conditions alone are insufficient, the Normal Force is determined using constitutive relations that account for both displacement and thermal strain.

In specific analytical scenarios, particularly those focusing on thermal expansion, the Normal Force is treated as a distinct mechanical force that interacts with thermal effects. Within certain specialized thermal expansion equations and unconstrained thermal expansion analyses, this mechanical force is either entirely excluded from the calculations or explicitly assumed to be zero (N = 0).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a6dab57c43d26f48e0a5a1f5535c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71dad38576055c6e013578d06064a913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-635877c2ca4bd8652a8de3e6ef23d5f9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896900</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Determinate Bar Systems">
  <data key="d0">Statically Determinate Bar Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural systems where bar forces are determined from equilibrium conditions&lt;SEP&gt;A type of structural system where internal forces and reactions can be determined solely from the equations of static equilibrium.&lt;SEP&gt;A topic in engineering mechanics involving systems where bar length changes and displacements are analyzed, discussed in the cited textbook.&lt;SEP&gt;Bar systems (German: statisch bestimmte Stabsysteme) whose support reactions and internal forces can be determined solely from equilibrium conditions, discussed on this page.&lt;SEP&gt;An engineering mechanics topic discussed in Section 10.5, positioned near the cited footer content on page 18.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71dad38576055c6e013578d06064a913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdbe00f1344b6e8391f5aa4709a1d925&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f2ecbe17c739fbd7bdf88a5cb116b3a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Behavior">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Behavior</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Material behavior where the material takes its original length after complete unloading, occurring when stress is below the yield stress&lt;SEP&gt;Material response classification applicable when the absolute value of stress σ is less than yield stress σ_F</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic Behavior">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Behavior</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Material behavior where plastic strain remains after complete unloading, occurring when stress exceeds the yield stress&lt;SEP&gt;Material response classification applicable when the absolute value of stress σ is greater than yield stress σ_F</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yield Stress">
  <data key="d0">Yield Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Yield Stress, commonly denoted as σ_F, is a critical stress limit that separates elastic and plastic material behavior. It represents the specific stress value at which a material transitions from a linear-elastic regime to one of permanent plastic deformation. Once this stress threshold is exceeded, the material begins to deform plastically, meaning the linear-elastic behavior no longer applies.

In technical and engineering contexts, Yield Stress serves as a foundational concept within material models. It dictates that the proportional limit stress must remain below the σ_F value to maintain structural integrity. As detailed in associated technical literature, including discussions in the sections preceding page 12, this threshold effectively categorizes material behavior into distinct elastic and plastic regimes.

From a practical standpoint, Yield Stress is an essential parameter used in engineering design verification. By ensuring that applied loads do not surpass this stress limit, engineers can guarantee that a material maintains its overall elastic behavior, thereby avoiding permanent plastic strain and ensuring the safety and reliability of the design.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-055d89277cf2e87034384489ad8dc567&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-783555881f4ab73d4d04ea548b9fae1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-151ad67dcab298d7226da28fe7afb340&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e6d370949ced2e82caca9443dede62a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-635877c2ca4bd8652a8de3e6ef23d5f9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897033</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
