</node>
<node id="Line Force">
  <data key="d0">Line Force</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A distributed load (Linienkraft) acting along the length of a bar, such as self-weight</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784894368</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Normal Stress, also known by its German equivalent Normalspannung, is a fundamental mechanical concept originally formalized by Cauchy. It represents the intensity of internal forces acting perpendicular to a specified surface, sectional plane, or the faces of an infinitesimal material element. As a component resulting from the decomposition of the stress vector, it is mathematically expressed as σ_N = t·n. The theoretical principles of Normal Stress are typically introduced in foundational mechanics coursework, providing the necessary basis for structural analysis and the calculation of mechanical strain.

Denoted by the Greek letter sigma (σ), Normal Stress comprises the components located on the main diagonal of the symmetric 3x3 stress tensor (σ_xx, σ_yy, and σ_zz). These components are often denoted using simplified notation as σ_x, σ_y, and σ_z. When acting in positive coordinate directions on positive faces, they represent tensile normal stresses, indicating tension and often depicted in diagrams by outward-pointing arrows (such as thick red arrows labeled σ_0). 

Mathematically, Normal Stress is calculated by dividing the normal force by the cross-sectional area of a structural component, generally defined by the equation σ = N/A = F/A. When analyzing how these forces vary along the length of an object, the formula is expressed specifically as σ(x) = N(x)/A(x), with values varying by partial derivatives across infinitesimal distances dx and dy. The resulting value indicates the nature of the mechanical load: positive values denote tensile stress, where the material is being stretched or pulled apart, while negative values signify compressive stress, where the material is being squeezed or pushed together. In practical engineering applications, Normal Stress is crucial for evaluating stress distributions in straight bars, uniaxially loaded members, and inclined planes, such as calculating an unknown normal stress resultant on an inclined hypotenuse face in worked examples. Specific problem values, such as σ_x = 50 MPa and σ_y = -20 MPa, are commonly analyzed. Additionally, in a pure shear stress state, the normal stress components (σx and σy) are equal to zero.

Normal Stress is highly involved in coordinate transformation equations—such as those transforming from an x,y to an η,ξ-system—derived using trigonometric identities. In graphical analysis, it is the direct stress component represented by sigma (σ) along the horizontal axis of the Cartesian coordinate system in Mohr's Circle diagram. The extremal values of Normal Stress occur in the principal directions, eventually becoming the principal stresses. This principal angle orientation is found when the derivative of the stress component (σξ) with respect to the cutting angle (φ) is set to zero, yielding the tangent equation. Furthermore, Normal Stress acting perpendicular to a plane generally does not vanish at the planes of maximum or extremal shear stress, where it is mathematically given by the equation σ_n = (σ_1 + σ_2)/2.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47064af1c6b2b2b7fb34a9c6b98111d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ec7d98bfb03bb42a21ae886e54257cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f7107d9e481e8d7b78079a1c4ff9229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65c0da2a284b623237194f0ea10b2b73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fed5dfab36680bc0a576b0ed9cc7e5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-842c0517bb20a54922d1a75c277c68f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6513413e0a80f005888472d94d385e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-341774a38a3b4fd47c6656391af81dd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-011c3fc433230965d7a734e4052f7689&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b2a42c115ed62e72e715daa89e2370&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-271a171e393b79c95418cb0645e75d72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95ef86ae323cfba68764c2acb2d46785&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74378ee7045b50cecf2c625c47685477&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-765ad24fd0f91713e3dc722570316ee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98ff466f481e3ef48f03b583574686cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2662124ebaa998fdaa13ffecb3add380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60e545266c1919041c313cfba39a94c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb64a0edb671186691ad9f74cd07ae9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb9e40b1a13627933f97ce043e59f768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8be23ae96862171a1f9cb78cb1cfcb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6589bf554e5066ac0fa2960ba8d673c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553477d678ccfb56c56a99c6a713a1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a7d19da473048f7577c45363a2702e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d97fb385691a70a5c314b2a77a107e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b3409eb9a461816c78110bf52f37f07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadc704f7f5e7980d43e7d870873727e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65f35adb2f34cbe81a9755002339ad40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f29928f565c2e69cfd84b7a3af24ffe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b18872f44af51f12eed1b7bb9c974c8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a03cb4a8bcb2904e74a15376b18fddec</data>
