<node id="Strain">
  <data key="d0">Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strain, also referred to by its German equivalent Dehnung, is a fundamental dimensionless kinematic quantity in continuum mechanics and engineering mechanics, denoted by the symbol epsilon ($\varepsilon$). As a longitudinal deformation measure representing relative elongation, it is generally defined as the ratio of the change in length to the material's original length. In a more specific pointwise formulation, the local strain field $\varepsilon(x)$ is defined as the spatial derivative of the displacement field, expressed as $\varepsilon = du/dx$ or $\varepsilon = u'(x)$. Strain is a sign-definite quantity where positive values indicate elongation and negative values indicate compression. 

The concept of Strain is intrinsically linked to both displacement and stress, serving as a foundational principle for logical flow in structural analysis and single bar evaluation. As a distinct kinematic quantity separate from stress, it relates to displacement through kinematics, constituting one aspect of the second fundamental equation type used for solving bar problems. Furthermore, its constitutive relationship with stress is explored within the context of Material Law/Model, where the deformation is affected by both stress and thermal changes. Most commonly, mechanical deformation is determined using Hooke's Law in conjunction with normal stress, calculating strain by dividing normal stress ($\sigma$) by the modulus of elasticity ($E$), yielding the formula $\varepsilon = \sigma/E$. This deformation, which can include both elastic and plastic components in a structural element, is empirically determined through tensile testing.

As a core topic in structural mechanics, Strain is typically introduced immediately following the discussion of stress. It is covered extensively in mechanical engineering coursework, such as Technische Mechanik 2, where its behavior is analyzed in conjunction with location-dependent cross-sectional areas. The concept is systematically detailed throughout lecture documents, specifically noted within Section 10.7 and summary sections preceding page 27.&lt;SEP&gt;A topic within elastostatics typically covered in the textbook Technische Mechanik 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6780202a7476f0626124683cb4683f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-738c698a563cc3405e3c1de5794fa318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf7cfa532d0224ec6532c6354495df59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e31c68f0ed98d537493847b61aa6024c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5281233f3c19ef2742208835bb41bd76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ec7d98bfb03bb42a21ae886e54257cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7acbcd6b20eaf7e62181f096df8a5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ae0372192096a8671eab765c6a11a90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7191762e3c837260ad5bf3f1bc50f25a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ad3e7d061ebaf26776716772bd7daeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-783555881f4ab73d4d04ea548b9fae1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fed5dfab36680bc0a576b0ed9cc7e5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7f9fd51bacb1b5261081565de649fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f862d3f8b712d95184fc656859d1e6cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-413d03b5c0b6ff01a4da279c609d09f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f44965262ad3a19924f66261ac5694c5</data>
