<node id="Zugstab">
  <data key="d0">Zugstab</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A bar subjected to tensile loading, resulting in a positive normal force and positive normal stress.&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental stress concept related to tension rods or tie rods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67c14bdfc0522f2a0b7438b9c789bafe</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896978</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Druckstab">
  <data key="d0">Druckstab</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A bar subjected to compressive loading, resulting in a negative normal force and negative normal stress.&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental stress concept related to compression rods or members.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67c14bdfc0522f2a0b7438b9c789bafe</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896978</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tangente">
  <data key="d0">Tangente</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-424242c0995ba02f22fe1c1864ca5f4b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d2">For small displacements, the circular arc can be replaced by the tangent as a geometric approximation.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784894369</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Juxtaposition of Historic Stone Architecture and Modern Glass Facade (image)">
  <data key="d0">Juxtaposition of Historic Stone Architecture and Modern Glass Facade (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image captures the coexistence of a 19th-century style stone building and a contemporary glass-clad structure. In the context of a lecture on 'Elastostatik' (Elastostatics) and material mechanics by Prof. Münstermann, the image serves as a visual anchor, potentially symbolizing the evolution of construction materials—from massive masonry to high-performance glass and steel systems—while illustrating principles of load-bearing and structural design discussed in the course.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-80a36b09d5e96cd8be0fbbcf40e15538</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="RWTH Aachen University Institutional Footer (footer)">
  <data key="d0">RWTH Aachen University Institutional Footer (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">Institutional branding footer identifying RWTH Aachen University as the source of the lecture document on elastostatics in materials engineering. Contains the department/institute abbreviation 'ibF' alongside the university name. This footer provides academic provenance and authentication for the course materials presented by Prof. Münstermann in Chapter 10 on tension and compression in structural bars.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9691eaa3b3e1ab3547916750f810ae73</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Conceptual Overview of Elastostatics Topics (image)">
  <data key="d0">Conceptual Overview of Elastostatics Topics (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image acts as a thematic introduction to Chapter 10 of Prof. Sebastian Münstermann's lecture on Elastostatics. It visually categorizes the essential components of analyzing elastic structures under load: kinematic relationships, stress (Spannung), strain (Dehnung), and elasticity models, specifically within the context of bar elements. The 'LEARN' background underscores the instructional purpose of the material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-48b0e3f83f75ba130c72c201a9b9da1d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Lernziele (Learning Objectives) (header)">
  <data key="d0">Lernziele (Learning Objectives) (header)</data>
  <data key="d1">header</data>
  <data key="d2">Section header introducing learning objectives for Chapter 10 on tension and compression in bars (Zug und Druck in Stäben). Positioned between the chapter title page and section 10.1 on stress (Spannung), it defines the pedagogical goals covering internal force determination, normal stress calculation, and the section method applied to elastic bar structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0dae0b4038721550e833d3e9407ccd22</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 2 - Section 10.1 Spannung (Stress) (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 2 - Section 10.1 Spannung (Stress) (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '2' identifies the second slide in Chapter 10 of the Elastostatics lecture. This page introduces Section 10.1 on Stress (Spannung), presenting fundamental concepts: bar axis definition, internal force determination via cutting method, Cauchy's normal stress concept, and sign conventions for tensile vs. compressive stress. Foundational content for stress analysis in bars.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47064af1c6b2b2b7fb34a9c6b98111d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Schematic Diagram of a Tensile Bar and Stress Analysis Plane (image)">
  <data key="d0">Schematic Diagram of a Tensile Bar and Stress Analysis Plane (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image provides a visual representation of a prismatic bar subjected to axial tensile forces (F). It serves as the primary illustration for the definition of normal stress (σ = F/A) within the context of elastostatics. The diagram highlights the cross-sectional area (A) and the location of an arbitrary cut plane (s-s), which are essential for determining the uniform distribution of internal stresses according to the elementary theory of tension members discussed in the accompanying text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eae0b855416df86edb6af7ac8d0407fb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram of Internal Forces and Normal Stress in a Tensile Bar (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram of Internal Forces and Normal Stress in a Tensile Bar (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This technical illustration visually defines the relationship between external axial force (F), internal normal force (N), and normal stress (σ) in a straight bar. The lower figure shows the equilibrium of internal forces at a cross-section, while the upper figure details the distribution of these forces as uniform normal stresses. It directly supports the textbook's explanation of 'Technische Spannung' (Technical Stress) and the behavior of a 'Zugstab' (Tensile Bar) in elastostatics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a81d55be35cb5a2d86799183a37d1295</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Equation for Uniaxial Loading (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Equation for Uniaxial Loading (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The fundamental normal stress formula σ = N/A = F/A for straight bars under axial loading, derived from Cauchy's stress principle. It calculates uniform stress distribution assuming constant cross-section, relating internal normal force or external load to cross-sectional area. Essential for tension/compression member analysis, though limited near stress concentrations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Bibliographic Source Citation Footer (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Bibliographic Source Citation Footer (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">This footer provides the source attribution 'Quelle: Gross, Hauger, Schröder, Wall (2017), Technische Mechanik 2, Springer Vieweg' for content on page 25 discussing Mohr's Stress Circle (Mohrscher Spannungskreis). It cites a standard German engineering mechanics textbook, establishing academic provenance for the lecture material covering stress analysis special cases and supporting the broader discussion of continuum mechanics and stress state calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1daacc45ca5900ab841904f26a5061a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10de6334f28b94ed6f4ea9af906f66c0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Non-uniform Stress Distribution in Axially Loaded Bars (image)">
  <data key="d0">Non-uniform Stress Distribution in Axially Loaded Bars (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a visual explanation of stress concentration phenomena in tension bars. It contrasts the theoretical model of a bar under load (a) with the actual, non-uniform stress distributions found at bar ends (b) and near geometric notches (c). By showing how stress vectors ($\\sigma$) vary across the cross-section, the image supports the textual discussion on why elementary stress theories fail at discontinuities and highlights the presence of stress peaks ('Spannungsspitzen') that must be considered in structural design.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f7107d9e481e8d7b78079a1c4ff9229</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress in an Axially-Loaded Bar (Cauchy Stress) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress in an Axially-Loaded Bar (Cauchy Stress) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the technical normal stress σ(x) as the ratio of internal normal force N(x) to cross-sectional area A(x) at position x along a bar. It is the foundational stress formula for axially-loaded members, used for tensile/compressive stress analysis and cross-section dimensioning, with the assumption of uniform stress distribution. It generalizes to bars with weakly varying cross-sections but does not capture stress concentrations at notches or bar ends.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e52c13f9f7327cff72c363dce5291e4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Section 10.1 Spannung (Stress) - Practical Applications (header)">
  <data key="d0">Section 10.1 Spannung (Stress) - Practical Applications (header)</data>
  <data key="d1">header</data>
  <data key="d2">Numbered section header (10.1) in a German mechanics textbook covering stress analysis. This instance on page 4 continues the topic from theoretical foundations (page 2) to practical cross-section dimensioning, required area calculations, and buckling considerations for compression members. Serves as organizational anchor connecting stress theory to engineering design practice.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65c0da2a284b623237194f0ea10b2b73</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 7 - Spannung Chapter Page Number (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 7 - Spannung Chapter Page Number (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '7' serving as a navigational reference within Chapter 10.1 'Spannung' (Stress) of a German technical mechanics document. Located at page index 3, it falls between stress theory fundamentals (internal forces, normal stress) and practical cross-section design content, aiding document navigation for stress analysis topics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67c14bdfc0522f2a0b7438b9c789bafe</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Bibliographic Source Citation (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Bibliographic Source Citation (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A standard academic footer providing source attribution for the page's content. It cites the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017), published by Springer Vieweg. This establishes the provenance for the technical mechanics diagrams and concepts discussed in the surrounding sections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4ad3e7d061ebaf26776716772bd7daeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e808a43e592e78037efdbadafb6d692e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd72d98943d7e0523296223aa7032d98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f04350734b994ec6a731daabee6c318c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be68185d5f9be3b57dbcc2d1f774fbb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f868d6a430faab3f01aae01801aa90be</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Allowable Stress Design Criterion (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Allowable Stress Design Criterion (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The inequality |σ| ≤ σ_{zul} is the fundamental stress-based design condition ensuring that the magnitude of normal stress (tensile or compressive) in a structural member does not exceed the material's allowable stress. It serves as the primary constraint for cross-section dimensioning and is essential for safe structural design, particularly when stress concentrations from geometric features must be considered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Required Cross-Sectional Area Design Formula (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Required Cross-Sectional Area Design Formula (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A dimensioning equation calculating the minimum required cross-sectional area ($A_{erf}$) as the ratio of absolute normal force ($|N|$) to allowable stress ($\sigma_{zul}$). This fundamental design constraint in structural engineering ensures actual stresses remain below permissible material limits, applicable where uniform stress distributions prevail per elementary tension/compression bar theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2a6dab57c43d26f48e0a5a1f5535c85</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 8 - Stress Analysis Chapter (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 8 - Stress Analysis Chapter (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '8' marking page 5 of a German engineering textbook covering stress analysis (Spannung). Located within Chapter 10.1, this page addresses stress distributions in bars with variable cross-sections, stress concentrations at notches, and includes a conical bar example demonstrating normal stress calculations under axial compression loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a375b4397dc3d85d100d03de64a86ab7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Source Citation Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source Citation Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A bibliographic source attribution footer citing the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017, Springer Vieweg) as the origin of the slide content. It provides academic provenance for the stress analysis material presented, establishing the lecture content's foundation in established mechanics literature.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd50f7cb97e77515d873719c81dafca9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e05e8f931eaf44276170a34134cf8c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c527b23d18394e188c355b1b1185acec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7acbcd6b20eaf7e62181f096df8a5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61ee301f89076e345159fdaef391ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec30ad8072c74dbe906914b85d9353a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80068bee1bee4c2ec1a3ff3d5d6d4f71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9d212905be253e446a02b9ce4b7ba85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83ac1dafe6c2621a4fb3a412b3493553&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0139ebf1ce9f9e12ac7fedf1b939a2f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0f20e6bdef6f836f08f3407dd794183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac4ea6191b5ef268551e8e517a153c48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Conical Compression Bar Stress Analysis Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Conical Compression Bar Stress Analysis Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image provides the geometric and loading parameters for a mechanics problem concerning a tapered (conical) bar under axial compression. It defines the bar's length (l), end radii (r0 and 2r0), and the applied compressive force (F). By introducing a variable radius r(x) along a longitudinal axis x, the diagram facilitates the derivation of the normal stress distribution σ(x) = N/A(x) as discussed in the context of elastostatic stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b523f7f38322578a875dcfe57c5cdd91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Radius Profile of Conical Bar (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Linear Radius Profile of Conical Bar (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the linearly varying radius r(x) of a conical bar under axial compression, transitioning from r₀ to 2r₀ over length l. This geometric relationship is essential for computing position-dependent cross-sectional area and stress distribution σ(x) = N/A(x) in non-prismatic structural members, illustrating fundamental concepts of stress analysis in variable cross-section components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Conical Compression Rod Normal Stress Distribution (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Conical Compression Rod Normal Stress Distribution (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Calculates the position-dependent normal stress σ(x) in a conical compression rod with circular cross-section under axial load F. The radius varies linearly from r₀ to 2r₀ over length l, yielding σ(x) = -F/[π·r₀²·(1+x/l)²]. Demonstrates non-uniform stress distribution in non-prismatic members under constant axial load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cf7cfa532d0224ec6532c6354495df59</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Boundary Stress Ratio of a Conical Bar (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Boundary Stress Ratio of a Conical Bar (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the relationship between the normal stresses at the ends of an axially loaded conical bar. It states that the stress at the smaller end ($x=0$) is four times the stress at the larger end ($x=l$), demonstrating the inverse proportionality between stress and cross-sectional area under a constant axial load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 9 (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 9 (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '9' serves as a navigational marker for the document page containing a worked example of normal stress in a conical rod and the transition to the topic of strain (Dehnung).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5281233f3c19ef2742208835bb41bd76</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Source Citation Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 Reference (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source Citation Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 Reference (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A bibliographic source attribution footer citing Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017), Technische Mechanik 2, Springer Vieweg. Located at the bottom of page 6, it attributes the preceding content on stress (Spannung) and strain (Dehnung) to this standard German mechanics textbook, establishing academic provenance for the lecture material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7ec7d98bfb03bb42a21ae886e54257cb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram of Tensile Deformation of a Prismatic Bar (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram of Tensile Deformation of a Prismatic Bar (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the fundamental concept of tensile strain (Dehnung) for a prismatic bar with a constant cross-sectional area ($A = \\text{konst.}$). It contrasts the 'Ausgangslage' (initial state) of length $l$ with the 'Nach der Verformung' (deformed state) resulting from applied tensile forces $F$. The visual explicitly defines the elongation $\\Delta l$, providing a geometric basis for the technical strain formula $\\varepsilon = \\Delta l / l$ discussed in the accompanying text on elastostatics and the kinematics of deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Technical Strain Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Technical Strain Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the dimensionless technical strain ($\varepsilon$) as the ratio of the change in length ($\Delta l$) to the original length ($l$). It is a fundamental kinematic relation in mechanics used to quantify deformation in structural members.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 10 - Stress, Strain, and Kinematics Fundamentals (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 10 - Stress, Strain, and Kinematics Fundamentals (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 10 of a German mechanics textbook covering section 10.2 (Dehnung/Strain) and introducing Kinematik. Content includes strain definition ε=Δl/l, local strain for variable cross-sections ε(x)=du/dx, displacement integration, and identification of displacement and strain as kinematic quantities. Connects stress analysis from section 10.1 to deformation kinematics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-23f9a8f34af690fb6d5cb0ea12f409a1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Diagram of Differential Bar Element Deformation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Diagram of Differential Bar Element Deformation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a visual derivation of local strain in a deformed bar. By comparing an 'undeformierter Stab' (undeformed bar) with a 'deformierter Stab' (deformed bar), it illustrates how a differential element of initial length '$dx$' undergoes displacement. The shifts of the element's faces are labeled '$u$' and '$u+du$', respectively. This figure is essential for understanding the kinematic relationship where strain ε is defined as the derivative of displacement $u$ with respect to position $x$, a core concept in the study of elastostatics and tension/compression in structural members.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ba3dc6bb30208edf4354dd20376fa9f6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Rod Element Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Differential Rod Element Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates an infinitesimal element of length dx, which is a core concept in the study of strain (Dehnung) and deformation in solid mechanics. It represents the differential segment used to derive the relationship between local displacement u(x) and local strain ε(x), as described in the context of elastostatics and tensile/compressive loading of rods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cebf917adc3d92ce2d5e155d10154396</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="One-Dimensional Strain-Displacement Relation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">One-Dimensional Strain-Displacement Relation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines local strain ε(x) as the spatial derivative of displacement u(x), equivalent to the engineering strain Δl/l for uniform deformation. This dimensionless kinematic quantity measures deformation intensity under small strain assumptions, serving as the geometric foundation connecting displacement fields to constitutive material models in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5ae0372192096a8671eab765c6a11a90</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Section 10.2 Dehnung (Strain) (header)">
  <data key="d0">Section 10.2 Dehnung (Strain) (header)</data>
  <data key="d1">header</data>
  <data key="d2">This header introduces Section 10.2 on 'Dehnung' (Strain). It organizes the subsequent definitions of technical strain, positive/negative deformation, and local strain as a function of displacement. This establishes the kinematic foundation required to understand material behavior and stress-strain relationships discussed later in the document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40c9b6d63b8c22a4bba35a9a76979235</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 11 - Engineering Mechanics Document (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 11 - Engineering Mechanics Document (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '11' marking the position within a German engineering mechanics textbook covering strain, kinematics, and material laws. Located at page index 7, it follows discussions of deformation concepts and precedes material on stress-strain diagrams and constitutive models. Serves as navigational reference for the academic document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a647d5317d835efced8e4c2f0cdae43</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Source Citation Footer - Gross et al. Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source Citation Footer - Gross et al. Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A bibliographic source citation attributing page content to 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017, Springer Vieweg). This German engineering mechanics textbook serves as the foundational reference for material covering constitutive laws, stress-strain relationships, and experimental determination of material properties through tensile/compression testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7191762e3c837260ad5bf3f1bc50f25a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Definition of Mechanical Normal Stress (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Definition of Mechanical Normal Stress (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines normal stress σ as force F divided by cross-sectional area A. This foundational relation connects external loading to internal material stress, a Kraftgröße paired with strain ε (a kinematische Größe). It underpins the stress-strain diagram and Hooke's Law, enabling material characterization and elastic/plastic classification central to this strength-of-materials lecture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Definition of Engineering Strain (Dehnung) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Definition of Engineering Strain (Dehnung) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines strain ε as the ratio of length change Δl to original length l, serving as the fundamental kinematic relationship in one-dimensional bar mechanics. It forms the geometric basis for Hooke's Law and elastic-plastic material modeling within the stress-strain framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Schematic Diagram of a Material Testing Apparatus (image)">
  <data key="d0">Schematic Diagram of a Material Testing Apparatus (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This schematic depicts a standard experimental setup for performing tensile or compression tests on a material specimen ('Probe'). It identifies key components including the load cell ('Lastzelle'), the extensometer ('Längungsmesser'), and the movable crosshead ('bewegliches Querhaupt'). The illustration serves as a practical reference for the theoretical discussion on kinematics, stress-strain relationships, and Hooke's Law presented in the surrounding text regarding elastostatics in bars.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress-Strain Diagram Illustrating Engineering vs. True Stress and Plastic Deformation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Stress-Strain Diagram Illustrating Engineering vs. True Stress and Plastic Deformation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram visualizes the tensile behavior of a ductile material, plotting Stress ($\\sigma$) against Strain ($\\varepsilon$). It distinguishes between engineering stress (red curve) and true stress (dashed black curve), highlighting the effects of necking ('Einschnürung'). Key features include the yield point ($\\sigma_F$), strain hardening ('Verfestigung'), and the unloading path ('Entlastung') which results in permanent plastic strain ($\\varepsilon_{pl}$). The included specimen schematic provides geometric context for the tensile test variables ($F, A, l$). This figure serves as a practical illustration of the material laws and experimental data discussed in the 'Elastostatik' chapter.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="10.3 Stoffgesetz/-modell (Constitutive Law/Model) (header)">
  <data key="d0">10.3 Stoffgesetz/-modell (Constitutive Law/Model) (header)</data>
  <data key="d1">header</data>
  <data key="d2">Introduces constitutive laws defining the physical relationship between stress and strain. It bridges kinematics and kinetics, detailing elastic vs. plastic behavior, Hooke's Law for linear elasticity, and the determination of material properties like Young's Modulus via experimental stress-strain diagrams.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-055d89277cf2e87034384489ad8dc567</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Test Principle Figure Citation (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Test Principle Figure Citation (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure caption citing Gottstein (p. 209) as the source for an illustration of the tensile test principle (Prinzip Zugversuch). Located within the constitutive model section (Stoffgesetz), it provides academic attribution for the visual depiction of the primary experimental method used to determine stress-strain relationships and material properties discussed in the surrounding content.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-783555881f4ab73d4d04ea548b9fae1b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 12 (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 12 (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '12' serves as a navigational marker in a mechanics lecture. It anchors the beginning of section 11.2.1, which introduces coordinate transformation for the plane stress state, building directly upon the foundational concepts of the stress tensor established on page 10.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-87396546f57986339f19784a816ba8db</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Notation/Reference Footer Fragment (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Notation/Reference Footer Fragment (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A corrupted footer element on page 9 of a German mechanics lecture, containing subscripted notation followed by a colon. Located at page bottom [bbox: 733,951]. The fragment likely represents a mathematical variable, constant, or reference label related to the page's discussion of elastic/plastic material behavior, yield stress (σ_F), proportional limit (σ_p), and Hooke's law. Encoding issues prevent precise identification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9e6d370949ced2e82caca9443dede62a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="True Cross-Sectional Area Reference (Wahre Querschnittsfläche) (footer)">
  <data key="d0">True Cross-Sectional Area Reference (Wahre Querschnittsfläche) (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">Footer annotation on page 8 referencing 'True cross-sectional area' (Wahre Querschnittsfläche). This term connects to the mechanics lecture discussing stress-strain relationships, material laws, and Hooke's model. It distinguishes actual deformed cross-section from nominal area, critical for calculating true stress versus engineering stress in tensile/compressive testing scenarios described in surrounding content.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a438fa813ee176d3e3d64cd5a7e2dba</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Source Citation: Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source Citation: Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A bibliographic footer providing source attribution for the surrounding lecture material. It cites the 2017 textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall, published by Springer Vieweg. The contextual content focuses on engineering mechanics, specifically stress tensors and plane stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3eaae8681c23616569341d164404cba8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b35bef5073db0c5a31b6dd2e83c0ddb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Generalized Hooke's Law (Tensorial Constitutive Equation for Linear Elasticity) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Generalized Hooke's Law (Tensorial Constitutive Equation for Linear Elasticity) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Tensorial form of Hooke's Law σ = E·ε, the constitutive model defining linear-elastic material behavior by linearly mapping the strain tensor to the stress tensor via the fourth-rank elasticity tensor. Central to Section 10.3 on material laws, it underpins the three-equation framework (equilibrium, kinematics, constitutive law) for structural analysis and reduces to the scalar form σ = E·ε for the uniaxial rod element in Section 10.4.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a093ad0a488172322e66eed43c540981</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Law for Linear-Elastic Uniaxial Loading (Inverse Form) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Law for Linear-Elastic Uniaxial Loading (Inverse Form) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation expresses Hooke's Law in inverse form, relating strain ε to stress σ through Young's modulus E. It is valid within the linear-elastic regime (σ ≤ σ_p) and constitutes the constitutive material model (Stoffgesetz) for analyzing single rod structures under axial loading, forming one of three fundamental equation systems in structural mechanics analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress-Strain Diagram illustrating Elastic-Plastic Material Behavior (image)">
  <data key="d0">Stress-Strain Diagram illustrating Elastic-Plastic Material Behavior (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image is a stress-strain diagram (σ-ε Diagram) that visualizes the results of a tensile test (Zugversuch). It depicts the relationship between stress and strain, highlighting the linear-elastic region (Hooke's Law), the yield point (σ_F), and the plastic deformation region. Crucially, it shows the unloading path ('Entlastung') resulting in permanent plastic strain (ε_pl) and contrasts engineering stress (σ = F/A) with true stress (σ_w = F/A_w). This serves as a graphical representation of the 'Stoffgesetz' (material law) concepts discussed in the accompanying text regarding material modeling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73a8a17ee36ec3a25041cb22c3b85cd5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 13 - Structural Mechanics Lecture (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 13 - Structural Mechanics Lecture (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '13' from a German engineering mechanics document covering axial loading topics. Located on page_idx 9, this page falls within sections discussing material constitutive laws (Stoffgesetz), elastic-plastic behavior, and single bar analysis (Einzelstab) including equilibrium, kinematics, and elasticity equations for structural analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ab59a548a45f0f75802088fe89a02c30</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Textbook Source Citation (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Textbook Source Citation (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">This footer serves as a bibliographic citation, attributing the surrounding academic content to the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017, Springer Vieweg). It authenticates the engineering mechanics concepts discussed, such as material laws and stress analysis, providing a verifiable source for knowledge retrieval.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3118530638f20d2b532ee5ac5df3f9a0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Bar Element Equilibrium Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Differential Bar Element Equilibrium Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image is a free-body diagram of an infinitesimal bar segment used to illustrate the equilibrium conditions for an individual bar (Einzelstab) under axial loading. It shows the balance between internal normal forces ($N$ and $N+dN$) and a distributed axial load ($n \, dx$). This visualization supports the theoretical framework in section 10.4 for determining stresses and deformations by establishing the fundamental differential equation of equilibrium for one-dimensional structural members.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-45f0f3e4c4e1775f77972c4268710cbb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Rod Element (Differential Element of a Bar) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Rod Element (Differential Element of a Bar) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Depicts an infinitesimal rod element of length dx at position x along a bar's axis, used as the fundamental geometric basis for deriving equilibrium, kinematic, and constitutive differential equations in one-dimensional structural analysis of axially loaded members.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6e2db8e0cc453f4ff50201dc37d1fa0c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Axial Equilibrium Equation for a Bar Element (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Axial Equilibrium Equation for a Bar Element (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation expresses static force equilibrium in the axial direction for an infinitesimal bar element (dx) under distributed loading. It balances the internal normal force N on one face with N+dN on the other, plus the line load contribution n·dx. Simplifying yields dN/dx = −n, the foundational differential equilibrium relation for one-dimensional bar analysis within the framework of equilibrium, kinematics, and constitutive law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f862d3f8b712d95184fc656859d1e6cd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Equilibrium Equation for Axially-Loaded Bar (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Differential Equilibrium Equation for Axially-Loaded Bar (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">First-order ordinary differential equation governing static equilibrium of a bar under axial loading. Relates the spatial gradient of internal normal force N(x) to the distributed line load n. Serves as the equilibrium condition among three equation types (equilibrium, kinematics, constitutive law) for analyzing single bars in structural mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-413d03b5c0b6ff01a4da279c609d09f9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Bar Equilibrium Equation with Distributed Load (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Bar Equilibrium Equation with Distributed Load (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation expresses the axial force equilibrium for a single bar segment under distributed loading, relating the normal forces at both ends to the resultant line load. It is the first of three governing equations for bar analysis in linear elasticity, establishing the basis for stress and deformation calculations in structural mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Strain-Displacement Relation for Axial Deformation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Strain-Displacement Relation for Axial Deformation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation ε(x) = du/dx is the one-dimensional strain-displacement relation defining longitudinal strain as the spatial derivative of axial displacement. It serves as the kinematic foundation within the three-equation framework (equilibrium, kinematics, constitutive law) for analyzing single bars under axial loading. Combined with Hooke's law and equilibrium conditions, it enables complete determination of stresses, strains, and displacements in linear-elastic structural members.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-635877c2ca4bd8652a8de3e6ef23d5f9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermoelastic Strain Equation (Duhamel-Neumann Law, 1D) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Thermoelastic Strain Equation (Duhamel-Neumann Law, 1D) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A one-dimensional thermoelastic constitutive law expressing total strain as the superposition of elastic mechanical strain (σ/E) and free thermal strain (α_T·ΔT). It extends Hooke's law to account for temperature effects in bar structures, enabling analysis of thermally-induced stresses and deformations in statically indeterminate systems within the linear-elastic regime.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermoelastic Bar Strain-Displacement Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Thermoelastic Bar Strain-Displacement Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A first-order differential equation relating axial strain (du/dx) of a bar to mechanical loading (N/EA) and thermal expansion (α_T·ΔT). It is the constitutive model for linear-elastic bar analysis under combined thermo-mechanical loading, forming the third pillar of single-bar analysis alongside equilibrium and kinematic equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Equation for Axially Loaded Bar (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Equation for Axially Loaded Bar (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines normal stress σ as internal axial force N divided by cross-sectional area A. Serves as the constitutive link between equilibrium analysis and material behavior in single-bar analysis, enabling comparison with material limits (σ_p, σ_F) and forming the basis for deformation calculations via Hooke's law within linear-elastic design.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 15 (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 15 (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '15' serves as a pagination marker for a document page covering 'Einzelstab' (single rod) mechanics. It locates the discussion on rod cross-section displacements and constant tensile stiffness conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7401c00a8316a2124dcb1cf703b5f16d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Source Citation Footer (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source Citation Footer (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A bibliographic footer attributing the page content to the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017), published by Springer Vieweg. It serves as the source reference for the concepts and figures related to plane stress and coordinate transformation discussed on the page.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dead11f22d1654614fa266e394b63218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1a7b2afc01b89dc35586063ca901c7f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Rod Elongation Equation with Thermal Effects (Stabverlängerungsgleichung) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Rod Elongation Equation with Thermal Effects (Stabverlängerungsgleichung) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A composite equation relating axial strain, displacement, and total elongation of a rod, incorporating both mechanical and thermal strain contributions. It integrates kinematics ($\varepsilon = du/dx$), integral elongation, and the constitutive law ($\varepsilon = N/EA + \alpha_T \Delta T$), serving as the foundational relationship for analyzing deformation in statically determinate and indeterminate structural rod problems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Bar Elongation Equation with Mechanical and Thermal Loading (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Bar Elongation Equation with Mechanical and Thermal Loading (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Calculates total axial elongation of a single prismatic bar as the superposition of mechanical deformation (Hooke's Law, Fl/EA) and free thermal expansion (αT·ΔT·l). Serves as the integrated constitutive-kinematic relation for the special case of constant stiffness, point load, and uniform temperature change in structural mechanics, distinguishing behavior between statically determinate and indeterminate systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Zero-Force Boundary Condition for Single Bar Analysis (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Zero-Force Boundary Condition for Single Bar Analysis (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Specifies a boundary condition where external axial force F equals zero on a bar cross-section (Einzelstab). Part of the equilibrium-kinematics-elasticity framework for structural analysis. Under this condition, normal force N(x)=0, affecting stress σ and strain ε distributions. Relevant for analyzing free ends, thermal expansion cases, and statically determinate structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c6780202a7476f0626124683cb4683f6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Elongation Formula for Prismatic Bar (Hooke's Law Application) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Axial Elongation Formula for Prismatic Bar (Hooke's Law Application) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the axial deformation (Δl) of a prismatic bar under tensile or compressive loading, combining equilibrium, kinematic, and constitutive relationships. It is the fundamental formula for statically determinate single-member analysis in structural mechanics, where EA represents the Dehnsteifigkeit (tensile stiffness).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-738c698a563cc3405e3c1de5794fa318</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Expansion Equation for a Bar (Wärmeausdehnungsgesetz) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Expansion Equation for a Bar (Wärmeausdehnungsgesetz) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the longitudinal thermal deformation of a prismatic bar subjected to a uniform temperature change. It is fundamental to the kinematic analysis in section 10.4, distinguishing between statically determinate problems (where thermal effects cause only deformation) and statically indeterminate problems (where thermal effects induce additional stresses due to constraints).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 16 - Einzelstab Analysis Section (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 16 - Einzelstab Analysis Section (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 16 within section 10.4 Einzelstab (Single Bar) of an engineering mechanics textbook. The page covers statically determinate and indeterminate bar problems, equilibrium conditions, kinematics, elasticity law, strain stiffness, normal force calculations, displacement determination, and thermal strain effects. Serves as structural reference for academic content navigation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039628e7ccab30fb4810eab64b589dc9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Source Citation Footer (Technische Mechanik 2) (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source Citation Footer (Technische Mechanik 2) (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A bibliographic source citation attributing page content to 'Technische Mechanik 2' (2017) by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall, published by Springer Vieweg. The footer establishes academic provenance for surrounding material on Mohr's Stress Circle (Mohrscher Spannungskreis), confirming the content originates from an established German engineering mechanics textbook rather than original authorship.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6e0e1d2a168869746758dfc06077ca50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17dfe04089064853421a9f1af5f1cb77</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="10.4 Einzelstab (header)">
  <data key="d0">10.4 Einzelstab (header)</data>
  <data key="d1">header</data>
  <data key="d2">Section header for 'Single Bar' analysis (Einzelstab) in mechanics of materials. It categorizes the evaluation of axially loaded bars into statically determinate and indeterminate problems, detailing concepts like normal force, displacement, thermal expansion, and governing differential equations across pages 11-13.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e31c68f0ed98d537493847b61aa6024c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 17 (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 17 (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">A navigational footer element indicating page 17 of the document. It structures a section on structural mechanics analyzing 'Einzelstab' (single rods), specifically contextualized within discussions of statically indeterminate problems and differential equations for displacement. The offset from the internal page index suggests prior front matter.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9fcf46285e4f22dfa1724a1fa4063d3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Governing Differential Equation for Axial Rod Displacement with Thermo-Mechanical Loading (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Governing Differential Equation for Axial Rod Displacement with Thermo-Mechanical Loading (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Second-order ODE governing axial displacement u(x) of a rod under distributed load and thermal effects. Combines equilibrium, kinematics, and thermo-elastic constitutive law. Applicable to both statically determinate and indeterminate problems; central to bar element analysis and FEM foundations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Strain-Displacement Relation with Thermal Effects (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Axial Strain-Displacement Relation with Thermal Effects (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This governing differential equation for a single rod (Einzelstab) relates axial displacement derivative to mechanical strain (N/EA) and thermal strain (α_T·ΔT). It forms the basis for computing displacement fields in both statically determinate and indeterminate structural problems, incorporating material elasticity and thermal expansion effects through superposition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Equilibrium Equation for Axially Loaded Bar (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Differential Equilibrium Equation for Axially Loaded Bar (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A first-order ODE governing the equilibrium of a one-dimensional bar under distributed axial loading. It relates the spatial derivative of the normal force N(x) to the distributed load n, serving as the fundamental starting point for deriving displacement, stress, and strain distributions in structural mechanics analysis of rods and bars.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Governing Differential Equation for Axially Loaded Bar (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Governing Differential Equation for Axially Loaded Bar (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This second-order ODE describes axial displacement of a bar under distributed load (self-weight). Combining equilibrium, kinematics, and Hooke's law with constant EA, it enables computation of displacement, strain, and stress distributions. It serves as the foundational equation for statically determinate bar problems in structural mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 18 - Einzelstab Example (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 18 - Einzelstab Example (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Printed page number '18' (page_idx 13) in a German mechanics textbook, located in Chapter 10.4 on single bar analysis. The page contains a worked example of a statically determinate hanging bar under self-weight, covering boundary conditions, integration constants, and determination of displacement and stress distributions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cc79c4ab764f023bc17eed0dd0b44b48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Schematic diagram of a hanging bar under self-weight (image)">
  <data key="d0">Schematic diagram of a hanging bar under self-weight (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image is a technical illustration of a vertical bar of length 'l' and weight 'G' fixed at its upper end. It provides the geometric and loading parameters for a structural mechanics problem involving a statically determinate bar under its own weight, used to derive differential equations for displacement and analyze internal stresses and deformations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17b87724a453e2805522671a76c088ec</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Free-body diagram of a hanging bar segment under self-weight (image)">
  <data key="d0">Free-body diagram of a hanging bar segment under self-weight (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the mechanical equilibrium of a segment of a vertically hanging bar subjected to its own weight ('Eigengewicht'). It defines the relationship between the internal normal force $N(x)$ and the partial weight $G^*$ as a function of the longitudinal coordinate $x$. This visual aid supports the derivation of stress distribution and total elongation for a statically determinate bar with constant cross-sectional area, as described in the accompanying technical documentation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c2ec62b2ae01b69aeae754d5bb1c9dcb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Equation for Axial Displacement of a Hanging Bar under Self-Weight (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Differential Equation for Axial Displacement of a Hanging Bar under Self-Weight (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The governing second-order ODE $EA \cdot u'' = -G/l$ for a statically determinate prismatic bar hanging under its own weight. It combines axial equilibrium with the linear-elastic constitutive law, yielding a linearly varying normal force distribution with maximum tension at the fixed support and zero at the free end.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1dfca5c7dbae86836330061b0f49bc01</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Force-Displacement Equation for Hanging Rod under Self-Weight (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Force-Displacement Equation for Hanging Rod under Self-Weight (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">First-order ODE describing the axial normal force distribution $N(x) = EA \cdot u^{\prime}$ in a statically determinate hanging rod subjected to its own weight. It expresses the internal force as a linear function of position, with integration constant $C_1 = G$ determined by the free-end boundary condition $u^{\prime}(l) = 0$. Foundational for elastostatic bar analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="General Solution for Longitudinal Displacement of a Statically Determinate Rod (equation)">
  <data key="d0">General Solution for Longitudinal Displacement of a Statically Determinate Rod (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation represents the general integral solution for the longitudinal displacement of a linear-elastic rod under its own weight. It correlates axial rigidity and displacement to a parabolic position function, incorporating integration constants resolved via static boundary conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field for Hanging Bar Under Self-Weight (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field for Hanging Bar Under Self-Weight (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Closed-form solution for the axial displacement u(x) of a statically determinate hanging bar of constant cross-section under its own self-weight. Derived from the governing ODE EA·u''=-G/l with boundary conditions u(0)=0 and u'(l)=0. Foundational example within the Einzelstab (single bar) framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Force Distribution in Hanging Rod under Self-Weight (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Axial Force Distribution in Hanging Rod under Self-Weight (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation describes the linear axial force distribution N(x) = G(1 - x/l) in a statically determinate hanging rod under self-weight. It combines the constitutive relation N = EA·u'(x) with equilibrium, showing maximum internal force G at the fixed support (x=0) and zero force at the free end (x=l).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Elongation-Displacement Relation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Elongation-Displacement Relation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the total elongation Δl of a rod as the difference between axial displacements at its endpoints, Δl = u(l) − u(0). Serves as the kinematic bridge between the displacement field and global deformation, essential for boundary condition enforcement and compatibility conditions in both statically determinate and indeterminate structural analysis under mechanical and thermal loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement of Hanging Rod under Self-Weight (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement of Hanging Rod under Self-Weight (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation gives the axial displacement at the free end of a uniform hanging rod under self-weight (Eigengewicht). The displacement equals half the product of total weight and length divided by axial stiffness. The factor 1/2 reflects the linearly varying internal force distribution from the fixed support to the free end.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Stress Distribution in a Hanging Rod under Self-Weight (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Axial Stress Distribution in a Hanging Rod under Self-Weight (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation gives the linear stress distribution σ(x) = (G/A)(1 - x/l) for a statically determinate hanging rod under its own weight. It derives from σ = N/A with the internal force varying linearly from G at the fixed support to zero at the free end, obtained by solving the governing differential equation with appropriate boundary conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 19 - Section 10.4 Einzelstab (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 19 - Section 10.4 Einzelstab (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 19 within section 10.4 'Einzelstab' of a German mechanics textbook. Positioned between examples of statically determinate (hanging rod under self-weight) and statically indeterminate (clamped rod with thermal expansion) systems. Serves as navigation aid for locating rod analysis content including differential equations, boundary conditions, and support reaction calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59e8bd68bf620257999ef2c55d39d49c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Schematic of a Statically Indeterminate Two-Segment Bar under Thermal Load (image)">
  <data key="d0">Schematic of a Statically Indeterminate Two-Segment Bar under Thermal Load (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image illustrates a classic elastostatics problem: a bar with varying cross-sections (segments 1 and 2) fixed at both ends (supports B and C). Diagram 'a' shows the physical setup where segment 1 is heated by $\\Delta T$. Diagram 'b' shows the corresponding reaction forces $B$ and $C$ required to satisfy equilibrium and compatibility conditions for this statically indeterminate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccf2b44f3719aa488a2e1148c9ed36db</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Normal Force in a Statically Indeterminate Thermally-Loaded Bar (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Internal Normal Force in a Statically Indeterminate Thermally-Loaded Bar (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation relates the constant internal normal force N in a doubly-clamped bar to the support reactions B and C. The result N = -B = -C follows from axial equilibrium (B = C) and establishes that the bar is uniformly in compression due to constrained thermal expansion. It is a key intermediate result in solving the statically indeterminate problem using the force method with compatibility condition Δl = 0.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermomechanical Elongation Compatibility Equations for a Two-Segment Statically Indeterminate Rod (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Thermomechanical Elongation Compatibility Equations for a Two-Segment Statically Indeterminate Rod (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the elongation of two serial rod segments in a both-ends-fixed (statically indeterminate) system, where segment 1 experiences both mechanical strain (Nl/EA₁) and thermal strain (αT·ΔT·l), while segment 2 experiences only mechanical strain (Nl/EA₂). Together with the compatibility condition Δl₁ + Δl₂ = 0, these equations enable determination of the unknown support reactions under combined thermal and mechanical loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal-Mechanical Compatibility Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Thermal-Mechanical Compatibility Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A compatibility condition for a doubly clamped, statically indeterminate rod. It balances the mechanical deformations of two different cross-sections against the thermal expansion of the heated section, equating their sum to zero to solve for the internal axial force.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abc3cadf3a789dbb882aa778366ab3b3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Normal Force in Statically Indeterminate Two-Segment Bar (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Normal Force in Statically Indeterminate Two-Segment Bar (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Calculates the compressive normal force N induced by uniform heating ΔT of segment 1 in a both-end-fixed (statically indeterminate) bar with two cross-sections A₁ and A₂. Derived from the compatibility condition that total elongation equals zero. The negative sign reflects constraint of thermal expansion, producing compressive stress without external loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c38a79e5e8255bb32751c63ad9a1aca1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 20 - Statically Indeterminate Rod with Thermal Loading (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 20 - Statically Indeterminate Rod with Thermal Loading (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 20 of a German engineering mechanics textbook, located within Section 10.4 Einzelstab. The page contains the statically indeterminate example of a clamped-clamped rod (beidseitig eingespannter Stab) subject to uniform heating (ΔT). Content covers equilibrium, compatibility conditions, and the superposition method for solving support reactions due to thermal loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-06a907de5d496ddcf9ab58b307c8f8ed</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Source citation footer for Technische Mechanik 2 textbook (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source citation footer for Technische Mechanik 2 textbook (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A bibliographic citation footer attributing page content to 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017), published by Springer Vieweg. This German-language textbook serves as a source for the lecture material on pages discussing statically indeterminate systems and thermal expansion analysis of bars using superposition methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f99cae1296009661e572167acf5fe19</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermally Loaded Statically Indeterminate Bar System (image)">
  <data key="d0">Thermally Loaded Statically Indeterminate Bar System (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image illustrates a classic statically indeterminate mechanics problem featuring a two-segment bar (① and ②) fixed between two rigid supports (B and C). Diagram 'a' establishes the physical setup where segment 1 undergoes a temperature change ΔT. Diagram 'b' visualizes the resulting compressive reaction forces at the supports. This figure serves as a visual aid for section 10.4 ('Einzelstab'), demonstrating how to solve for unknown support reactions when thermal expansion is physically constrained.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df979c16e8c52bf800fb84c3a4a4bdea</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Superposition Method Diagram for Statically Indeterminate Heated Bar (image)">
  <data key="d0">Superposition Method Diagram for Statically Indeterminate Heated Bar (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the superposition principle used to analyze a statically indeterminate bar fixed at both ends (points B and C) undergoing thermal expansion ($\\Delta T$). It decomposes the problem into a '</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bbd27ba75933ee3cba54b55605c1c8dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Expansion in Statically Determinate Base System (State 0) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Expansion in Statically Determinate Base System (State 0) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation describes the unconstrained thermal elongation of a bar in a statically determinate reference system, serving as the first step in a superposition method for solving statically indeterminate thermoelastic problems. It relates thermal expansion displacement to material properties (α_T), temperature change (ΔT), and bar length (l), enabling the determination of support reactions in constrained systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-542bf3a4290d267cd5257b52563c594f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Superposition State (1) Displacement Equation for Statically Indeterminate Rod (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Superposition State (1) Displacement Equation for Statically Indeterminate Rod (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the displacement at point C in the correction state (1) of a superposition solution for a thermally-loaded, doubly-clamped rod with variable cross-section. It sums the elastic deformations of two segments under constraint force F_x, using Hooke's Law. Combined with state (0) thermal expansion, it enforces the zero-displacement boundary condition, enabling determination of support reactions in statically indeterminate systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e6428916bed03d77d29954942400a6f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermoelastic Compatibility Condition for Clamped Two-Segment Rod (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Thermoelastic Compatibility Condition for Clamped Two-Segment Rod (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This compatibility equation governs a statically indeterminate, double-clamped rod with two segments (areas A₁, A₂) where only segment 1 is heated by ΔT. Setting total elongation to zero, it balances thermal expansion against elastic compression from support reactions, enabling determination of the indeterminate reaction force F_x and illustrating thermoelastic coupling in redundant structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c71f393772a794391e49730d47a8bda4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Reaction Force in a Doubly-Clamped Two-Segment Rod (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Reaction Force in a Doubly-Clamped Two-Segment Rod (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Computes the support reaction forces B = C in a statically indeterminate, doubly-clamped rod with two segments (cross-sections A₁, A₂) when segment 1 is heated by ΔT. Derived from equilibrium and zero-displacement compatibility, the induced normal force equals EA₁A₂α_TΔT/(A₁+A₂), representing constrained thermal expansion stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed4c24e6b357be46b6c720d32cdb5f62</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="10.4 Einzelstab (Single Rod Analysis) (header)">
  <data key="d0">10.4 Einzelstab (Single Rod Analysis) (header)</data>
  <data key="d1">header</data>
  <data key="d2">Section header introducing analysis of individual rods under thermal and mechanical loading in a mechanics textbook. Covers statically indeterminate systems with examples of both-sides clamped rods under uniform heating, presenting both direct (equilibrium + kinematics + boundary conditions) and superposition-based solution methods. Establishes foundational techniques extended in subsequent section 10.5 on statically determinate multi-rod systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2866a3f417ff78b075338fa558392e29</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 21 - Statisch bestimmte Stabsysteme Section (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 21 - Statisch bestimmte Stabsysteme Section (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number marker (21) for a page in a German structural mechanics textbook covering statically determinate bar systems. Located within Sections 10.4-10.5 discussing single bar analysis, thermal expansion, equilibrium conditions, and displacement calculations at node points. Serves as navigation reference for content on bar force determination and deformation analysis methodology.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64ae8f78050d6e3fb28375ca609b6390</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Two-Bar Truss System Schematic (image)">
  <data key="d0">Two-Bar Truss System Schematic (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This schematic depicts a statically determinate two-bar truss system consisting of a horizontal bar (1) and an inclined bar (2) meeting at joint C. A vertical force F is applied at joint C. This diagram is part of an example problem in elastostatics aimed at calculating the displacement of point C, utilizing principles of equilibrium and small-displacement theory where circular arcs of displacement are approximated by tangents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94aa1e1d6b738ff40ac94957c9a7084a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Free-body diagram of node C in a two-bar truss system (image)">
  <data key="d0">Free-body diagram of node C in a two-bar truss system (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image is a force vector diagram used to analyze the static equilibrium at node C of a two-bar structure. It shows the relationship between horizontal force $S_1$, inclined force $S_2$ (at angle $\\alpha$), and vertical load $F$. The diagram serves as the basis for calculating internal bar forces and displacements as part of the example problem discussed in Section 10.5 regarding statically determinate bar systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6c113cfbbfa4f8142d716b5538931026</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium Conditions at Node C (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium Conditions at Node C (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation represents the resolution of forces at a concurrent joint in a statically determinate system. By balancing horizontal and vertical force components, it solves for the internal axial forces (S1, S2) in the truss members. These forces are essential for subsequent calculations of stress, strain, and nodal displacement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Bar Elongation Equations for Statically Determinate Two-Bar System (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Bar Elongation Equations for Statically Determinate Two-Bar System (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Computes axial elongations Δl₁ and Δl₂ of two bars in a statically determinate system using Hooke's Law (Δl = Sl/EA). Bar forces derived from nodal equilibrium at point C under load F yield compression in bar 1 and tension in bar 2, with trigonometric terms reflecting the inclined geometry. Results enable subsequent displacement calculation at node C.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fe61c354100bb43a98ebc62d57cf28c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 22 (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 22 (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '22' indicates the beginning of section 11.2.3, 'Mohrscher Spannungskreis' (Mohr's Stress Circle). This page introduces the graphical representation of stress states, providing the geometric definition and a step-by-step procedure for constructing the circle using known normal and shear stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-269e2be218f17e48feb7ecb2c0ddf3f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Quellenangabe Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Quellenangabe Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">Source citation footer attributing content to Gross, Hauger, Schröder, Wall (2017), 'Technische Mechanik 2', Springer Vieweg. Located on page discussing statically determinate bar systems, superposition methods, and displacement calculations at node C. Provides academic provenance for engineering mechanics instructional material covering equilibrium conditions, thermal expansion, and small-deformation approximations in structural analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-71dad38576055c6e013578d06064a913</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Two-bar statically determinate truss system diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Two-bar statically determinate truss system diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image displays a technical diagram of a two-bar truss system (bars 1 and 2) fixed to a vertical wall at points A and B and meeting at joint C. It shows an external vertical force F applied at C, along with geometric parameters l (horizontal length) and α (angle). According to the surrounding context, this figure illustrates an example problem in elastostatics for calculating the displacement of node C and internal bar forces in a statically determinate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed3c14262e18f66ed415c1b72900af48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Diagram for a Statically Determinate Two-Bar System (image)">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Diagram for a Statically Determinate Two-Bar System (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a visual solution for the displacement of a joint (C to C') in a two-bar system, a core concept in elastostatics. It graphically represents how the individual axial elongations ($\\Delta l$) of the members combine to produce a resultant displacement vector. This illustration directly supports the educational material on statically determinate bar systems, specifically demonstrating the geometric application of the small-displacement theory to find the final position of a loaded node.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6681170cb4ff75b4d310df8342f3aad9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Force Equilibrium and Displacement Diagram for a Two-Bar System (image)">
  <data key="d0">Force Equilibrium and Displacement Diagram for a Two-Bar System (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image provides a complete visual solution for a statically determinate two-bar truss problem. The top diagram establishes the force equilibrium at node C under an external load F. The bottom diagram ('d') illustrates the 'Verschiebungsplan' (displacement plan), showing how to graphically determine the total displacement of node C (components u and v) based on the calculated axial elongations ($\\Delta l_1, \\Delta l_2$) of the members, utilizing the small-displacement assumption where arcs are replaced by tangents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5b9805139d42f6eef23df04f62f07811</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Displacement of Node C in Statically Determinate Two-Bar System (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Displacement of Node C in Statically Determinate Two-Bar System (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Calculates horizontal displacement u of node C in a statically determinate bar system with a horizontal and an inclined bar (angle α) under vertical load F. The displacement equals the absolute shortening of bar 1, derived via equilibrium (S₁ = F/tan α), Hooke's law, and small-displacement kinematics. The result scales as Fl/(EA·tan α).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc78afbb216db05f32ee167a8643581f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Displacement of Node K in Statically Indeterminate Three-Bar System (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Displacement of Node K in Statically Indeterminate Three-Bar System (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Calculates the vertical displacement $v$ of node K in a statically indeterminate three-bar truss system. Derived by simultaneously applying equilibrium, Hooke's law, and kinematic compatibility. The result shows displacement depends on load F, stiffness ratio EA₁/EA₂, and the characteristic cos³α factor arising from geometric coupling between inclined and vertical members.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad1fe24f4724db937ef65aa2cd2c2fab</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 23 - Statically Determinate Bar Systems Displacement Analysis (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 23 - Statically Determinate Bar Systems Displacement Analysis (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '23' located at bottom-left of page_idx 18. This page continues Section 10.5 on statically determinate bar systems, presenting methods for calculating point displacements using small-deformation assumptions and tangent approximations. It provides the geometric framework for determining horizontal and vertical displacement components in two-bar systems with equal axial stiffness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bdbe00f1344b6e8391f5aa4709a1d925</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Source Citation - Technische Mechanik 2 (Gross et al., 2017) (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source Citation - Technische Mechanik 2 (Gross et al., 2017) (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A bibliographic source citation attributing the page's content on Hauptspannungen (principal stresses) to the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017), published by Springer Vieweg. The footer establishes academic provenance for the lecture material covering principal axis systems, extremal shear stress values, and stress transformation theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f56ba60dee95d9f5127479d5f80c141c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f2ecbe17c739fbd7bdf88a5cb116b3a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Analysis of a Statically Indeterminate Three-Bar Truss System (image)">
  <data key="d0">Analysis of a Statically Indeterminate Three-Bar Truss System (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This technical illustration details the step-by-step analysis of a symmetric, statically indeterminate three-bar truss. Figure (a) establishes the geometry and loading conditions; figure (b) depicts the equilibrium of forces at the central node; and figure (c) provides the geometric compatibility (displacement plan) necessary to solve for unknown internal forces and nodal displacements in structural mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Equilibrium Condition at Node K (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Equilibrium Condition at Node K (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Horizontal force balance at node K in a symmetric statically indeterminate three-bar system, establishing S₁ = S₃ due to geometric symmetry. This reduces the system's indeterminacy and, combined with vertical equilibrium and compatibility conditions, enables determination of nodal displacement and all bar forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2d49057dad0c3acc1a3ce1afb3e687a5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Hooke's Law for Axial Deformation in a Statically Indeterminate System (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Hooke's Law for Axial Deformation in a Statically Indeterminate System (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation applies Hooke's Law to calculate the elongation of individual bars in a statically indeterminate structural system based on their internal forces, lengths, and axial rigidities. It serves as the necessary constitutive (material) link between the system's static equilibrium and geometric kinematics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fe1aa2ab22c57b58f370cf064cf7ce98</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Compatibility Equation for Statically Indeterminate Bar System (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Compatibility Equation for Statically Indeterminate Bar System (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Geometric compatibility condition relating elongations of bars 1 and 2 in a statically indeterminate three-bar system. Essential for solving the system alongside equilibrium and constitutive equations, enabling calculation of bar forces and node displacement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f72457639c5767731980d0c54a4fe069</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 24 - Statically Indeterminate Bar Systems (Solution Derivation) (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 24 - Statically Indeterminate Bar Systems (Solution Derivation) (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '24' on document page_idx 19, located within Section 10.6 on statically indeterminate bar systems. This page bridges the problem setup (equilibrium conditions and displacement plan) with the final solution (bar forces and displacements) for a three-bar system loaded at knot K.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c5d7eea8f9e0a901768256832130203b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Academic Source Citation Footer (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Academic Source Citation Footer (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">This footer provides bibliographic attribution for the educational slide content. It cites the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017), published by Springer Vieweg. It links the surrounding technical analysis of statically indeterminate bar systems to its foundational literature, serving as an academic reference point for students.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f3cdf7e6c0e0814f6340adcf0330c36f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Schematic of a Three-Bar Statically Indeterminate System (image)">
  <data key="d0">Schematic of a Three-Bar Statically Indeterminate System (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image provides the foundational geometric and loading model for a classic engineering problem concerning statically indeterminate structures. It depicts a symmetrical three-bar assembly (Bars 1, 2, and 3) subjected to a vertical force F at node K. As described in the surrounding text (Section 10.6), this diagram serves as the basis for calculating internal bar forces (S1, S2, S3) and the resulting displacement of node K by combining principles of equilibrium, kinematics, and Hooke's law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1db4055b5d566c7d454787a7ff1515b7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium and Kinematic Diagrams for a Symmetric Three-Bar System (image)">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium and Kinematic Diagrams for a Symmetric Three-Bar System (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image provides the essential visual framework for solving a statically indeterminate mechanics problem involving a symmetric three-bar truss. Diagram (b) defines the static equilibrium at node K under an external load F, while diagram (c) illustrates the kinematic constraints relating the vertical displacement of node K to the axial elongations of the individual bars. These diagrams directly support the derivation of internal forces and displacements discussed in section 10.6 regarding statically indeterminate bar systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cdfa6405eb28772fd593fe970821beaf</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Stabkraft S₂ for Statically Indeterminate Three-Bar System (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Stabkraft S₂ for Statically Indeterminate Three-Bar System (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Closed-form expression for the axial force in the central bar of a symmetric, singly statically indeterminate three-bar truss under vertical loading at the common node. Derived by coupling equilibrium, Hooke's law, and displacement compatibility. Demonstrates that force distribution in indeterminate systems depends on relative member stiffnesses ($EA_1/EA_2$) and geometry ($\alpha$).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edab2d4bca40b7e16cfc072b04cebe0c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Indeterminate Bar Forces Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Statically Indeterminate Bar Forces Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This formula calculates the axial forces in the diagonal bars of a symmetric, statically indeterminate three-bar system. It results from combining nodal equilibrium, elastic material laws (Hooke's Law), and geometric compatibility, demonstrating how an external load $F$ distributes according to the relative axial stiffnesses ($EA_1/EA_2$) and the geometry ($\alpha$) of the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9966746921724686a393e234327d9e41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 25 - Statically Indeterminate Bar System Example Solution (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 25 - Statically Indeterminate Bar System Example Solution (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 25 identifies the continuation page of Section 10.6's worked example on a statically indeterminate three-bar system. Contains critical solution steps: compatibility condition substitution, calculation of S₂, vertical displacement Δl₂, and forces S₁,₃. Connects the equilibrium/kinematics setup (p.19) to the chapter summary (p.21).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3891c97c9b24173fa3943bf0d59b8fa9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Expansion Equation for Bars (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Expansion Equation for Bars (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the thermal elongation of a bar subjected to uniform temperature change ΔT. Derived from the general elongation formula by setting N=0, it is fundamental for analyzing statically indeterminate systems where thermal effects induce stresses. The context emphasizes that temperature changes cause Wärmespannungen in constrained systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 26 - Mechanics Lecture (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 26 - Mechanics Lecture (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '26' displayed on page_idx 21 of German engineering mechanics lecture materials covering statically indeterminate bar systems (Stabsysteme). Located between summary content on stress, strain, Hooke's law, and the closing acknowledgment slide. Serves as navigational reference in lecture notes or presentation deck on structural analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5fed5dfab36680bc0a576b0ed9cc7e5a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784895160</data>
</node>
