<node id="Stress Design Criterion (|σ| ≤ σ_zul)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Design Criterion (|σ| ≤ σ_zul)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The fundamental stress design criterion (Spannungsnachweis) in structural mechanics and strength of materials, expressed as |σ| ≤ σ_zul, where the absolute value ensures the condition applies to both tensile and compressive stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress (σ)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress (σ)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress (Normalspannung) acting on a cross-section, computed as σ(x) = N(x)/A(x), where N(x) is the internal normal force and A(x) is the cross-sectional area.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanical quantity representing normal stress acting on a cross-section of a structural member under axial loading, with units [MPa = N/mm²], expressing an intensive quantity as the ratio of extensive force to geometric area&lt;SEP&gt;A type of stress represented by the horizontal axis in the Cartesian coordinate system of Mohr's Circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ca440e5727deb504109d3cda9b3642d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Allowable Stress (σ_zul)">
  <data key="d0">Allowable Stress (σ_zul)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The allowable stress (zulässige Spannung), a material-specific limit defined by engineering codes that incorporates safety factors against yielding, fracture, or other failure modes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Normal Force (N(x))">
  <data key="d0">Internal Normal Force (N(x))</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The internal normal force used in the computation of normal stress, denoted N(x); in the conical bar example, N(x) = -F produces compression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Sectional Area (A(x))">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Sectional Area (A(x))</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The cross-sectional area used in the computation of normal stress, denoted A(x); the required area A_erf must be chosen so that maximum stress magnitude remains below σ_zul.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Stress">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of normal stress (Zugspannung) that is designated as positive according to the sign convention used in the lecture material.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Zugspannung, the type of stress indicated by positive values of normal stress in tension/compression members.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of normal stress (Zugspannung) that occurs when a material is subjected to pulling forces, causing elongation. Introduced as a theoretical concept within Section 10.1.&lt;SEP&gt;Positive normal stress (Zugspannung) that the absolute value formulation of the stress design criterion accounts for.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47064af1c6b2b2b7fb34a9c6b98111d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65c0da2a284b623237194f0ea10b2b73</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compressive Stress">
  <data key="d0">Compressive Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of normal stress (Druckspannung) that is designated as negative according to the sign convention used in the lecture material.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Druckspannung, the type of stress indicated by negative values of normal stress in tension/compression members.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of normal stress (Druckspannung) that occurs when a material is subjected to compressive or pushing forces. Introduced as a theoretical concept within Section 10.1.&lt;SEP&gt;Negative normal stress (Druckspannung) that the absolute value formulation of the stress design criterion accounts for; slender compression members may fail by buckling before reaching σ_zul.&lt;SEP&gt;The negative stress value (Druckspannung) indicating compression in a structural member, represented by the negative sign in stress equations</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47064af1c6b2b2b7fb34a9c6b98111d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf7cfa532d0224ec6532c6354495df59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65c0da2a284b623237194f0ea10b2b73</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Section Dimensioning">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Section Dimensioning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A practical engineering method (Querschnittsbemessung) for determining appropriate cross-sectional dimensions of structural components under loading conditions. Covered as practical application content in Section 10.1.&lt;SEP&gt;A practical engineering procedure for sizing cross-sections of structural members, addressed on Page 4 of the textbook.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of determining the required cross-sectional area (Querschnittsbemessung) such that the maximum stress magnitude anywhere in the component remains below σ_zul.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67c14bdfc0522f2a0b7438b9c789bafe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65c0da2a284b623237194f0ea10b2b73</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Concentrations">
  <data key="d0">Stress Concentrations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A technical mechanics concept related to notches, also referred to as 'Spannungsspitzen'.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Spannungsspitzen, non-uniform stress distributions that occur at bar ends, notches, and geometric discontinuities, requiring advanced methods beyond the elementary formula.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress phenomena occurring at notches, discussed within the stress theory content.&lt;SEP&gt;Localized stress peaks (Spannungsspitzen) caused by geometric discontinuities such as notches (Kerben) or bar ends (Stabenden), which can locally exceed predictions from elementary bar theory.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of conceptual diagram presented in the main body of the document relating to localized high stress.&lt;SEP&gt;The localized high stresses occurring at geometric discontinuities such as notches (Kerben) and bar ends (Stabenden).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a6dab57c43d26f48e0a5a1f5535c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67c14bdfc0522f2a0b7438b9c789bafe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10de6334f28b94ed6f4ea9af906f66c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd72d98943d7e0523296223aa7032d98</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Buckling">
  <data key="d0">Buckling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A failure mode (Knicken) for slender compression members that may occur before the allowable stress σ_zul is reached.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure mode (Knicken) specific to slender compression members, which may fail before reaching material stress limits.&lt;SEP&gt;A potential failure mode for slender compression members that may occur before reaching critical stress, considered in stability analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a6dab57c43d26f48e0a5a1f5535c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Von Mises">
  <data key="d0">Von Mises</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A more complex failure theory used in machine design and structural engineering, built upon the foundational stress-based design criterion.&lt;SEP&gt;A material failure criterion used in structural analysis and based on stress tensor formulations&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion applied to the stress state for structural analysis and failure prediction of plates, beams, and machine components.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure theory fundamental to determining critical loading directions in engineering mechanics.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion used in structural analysis and material strength assessment, applied using principal stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;A material failure criterion referenced in the context of stress transformation and elastic deformation analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion that predicts no yielding of a material under pure hydrostatic stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1626d6e3dffee746e5efebf5c0f44ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebe13321a6d5df3c82e760adc093a99c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d8ef0753f64ed72eb451c29b8108d08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tresca">
  <data key="d0">Tresca</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A more complex failure theory used in machine design and structural engineering, built upon the foundational stress-based design criterion.&lt;SEP&gt;A material failure criterion used in structural analysis and based on stress tensor formulations&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion applied to the stress state for structural analysis and failure prediction of plates, beams, and machine components.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure theory fundamental to determining critical loading directions in engineering mechanics.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion used in structural analysis and material strength assessment, applied using principal stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;A material failure criterion referenced in the context of stress transformation and elastic deformation analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1626d6e3dffee746e5efebf5c0f44ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebe13321a6d5df3c82e760adc093a99c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f</data>
