<node id="Mathematical Equation">
  <data key="d0">Mathematical Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The entity "Mathematical Equation" encompasses a diverse array of mathematical formulations, primarily utilized within the contexts of structural analysis, solid mechanics, and stress transformation. Legitimate applications of these equations include a system of two-dimensional static equilibrium equations for a concurrent force system at a structural node, which details vertical and horizontal force balances to provide closed-form algebraic solutions for internal forces S1 and S2. In the context of principal stress transformation, it also includes equations that verify normalization, demonstrating that any ratio of identical quantities squared will yield unity. Additionally, "Mathematical Equation" covers discrete finite difference step equations used to satisfy equilibrium conditions in solid mechanics by evaluating the difference quotients of spatial gradients of stresses. Other valid representations include numerical arithmetic expressions composed of products and divisions of rational numbers, as well as shorthand notations such as the division of ordered tuples (e.g., representing trigonometric values in stress analysis). 

However, a significant portion of the descriptions attributed to "Mathematical Equation" consists of mathematically inconsistent formulas and garbled text, likely resulting from Optical Character Recognition (OCR) artifacts or extraction errors. Several of these equations present invalid mathematical identities, such as an expression incorrectly relating common quadratic factors by equating 1/2 to 1/4, and an inconsistent equality evaluating to 30.25 on one side and approximately 0.929 on the other. Other corrupted outputs include a mathematically incoherent chain of equalities involving rational numbers and percentages, and an inconsistent arithmetic chain reducing to zero. Further extraction artifacts manifest as equations utilizing circled numerical symbols as reference markers, or outputs featuring repeated patterns of terms with dotted zeros. Despite their corrupted states, these malformed equations frequently appear in engineering contexts, likely representing reference markers for derivation steps or OCR-garbled stress equilibrium conditions for structures such as thin-walled spherical vessels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd747436c8f7ae4c9f1ad317397dda30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6546507247b4802eb945e2a493cb040&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada0944efa350d49f7642c228e06590&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9c3492ddaae5a544709ab9626c48a75</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904069</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Knoten C">
  <data key="d0">Knoten C</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural node at which concurrent forces are analyzed under static equilibrium conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="S1">
  <data key="d0">S1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The internal axial force within structural member 1, solved as S1 = -F / tan α, with the negative sign indicating compressive loading opposite to the assumed positive direction.&lt;SEP&gt;The axial force in the inclined bar 1.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edab2d4bca40b7e16cfc072b04cebe0c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="S2">
  <data key="d0">S2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The internal axial force within structural member 2, solved as S2 = F / sin α, derived by projecting the axial force into rectangular components using trigonometric functions.&lt;SEP&gt;The normal force (Stabkraft) in the middle vertical bar of a statically indeterminate three-bar system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edab2d4bca40b7e16cfc072b04cebe0c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="F">
  <data key="d0">F</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The external constant compressive force applied to the bar, resulting in the internal force N(x) = -F.&lt;SEP&gt;An applied external concentrated load acting at the structural node.&lt;SEP&gt;The external vertical load applied at the common node K of the structural system.&lt;SEP&gt;Externally applied vertical load at the central node K of the three-bar truss system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edab2d4bca40b7e16cfc072b04cebe0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9966746921724686a393e234327d9e41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alpha">
  <data key="d0">Alpha</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The angle of inclination of the second structural member relative to the horizontal direction, used in trigonometric projections of axial force.&lt;SEP&gt;The inclination angle of bars 1 and 3 relative to the horizontal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edab2d4bca40b7e16cfc072b04cebe0c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Stress">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A topic covered in the surrounding lecture slides, specifically mentioned as mechanical stress (Spannung).&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanical quantity denoted by sigma whose discussion precedes the technical normal strain in a conical bar.&lt;SEP&gt;The stress calculated from internal forces using the formula σ = S/A, determined after solving the equilibrium equations.&lt;SEP&gt;The forces acting upon the material element, represented by red arrows indicating both normal and shear components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd72d98943d7e0523296223aa7032d98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f7f241f746726b97e3dc567365e483</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904038</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Longitudinal Deformation">
  <data key="d0">Longitudinal Deformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The axial deformation of a structural member calculated using Δl = Sl/EA, based on the determined internal forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structural Displacement">
  <data key="d0">Structural Displacement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The geometrical displacement at node C derived from longitudinal deformations under the assumption of small deformations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Force System">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Force System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A system of forces whose lines of action intersect at a common point, analyzed at a structural node to establish static equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trigonometric Functions">
  <data key="d0">Trigonometric Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical functions, specifically sin α and cos α, used to project the axial force S2 into its rectangular components.&lt;SEP&gt;Functions such as $\cos 2\varphi$ and $\sin 2\varphi$ that weight linear combinations of stress variables in the transformation equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903990</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Axial Elongation Equation">
  <data key="d0">Total Axial Elongation Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An equation representing the total axial elongation (Δl) of a prismatic bar under combined mechanical and thermal loading, derived by integrating the strain-displacement relation along the bar's length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896847</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Component">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Component</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The additive term in the axial elongation equation representing the mechanical deformation, calculated as Fl/(EA) and obtained via Hooke's Law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Component">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Component</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The additive term in the axial elongation equation representing free thermal expansion due to a uniform temperature differential, calculated as αT·ΔT·l.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic/Thermal Constitutive Model">
  <data key="d0">Elastic/Thermal Constitutive Model</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A fundamental pillar of structural analysis defining the material behavior under elastic and thermal influences (Elastizitätsgesetz).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Determinate Systems">
  <data key="d0">Statically Determinate Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural systems in which thermal expansion produces only elongation without inducing additional internal stresses, as the structure can freely deform.&lt;SEP&gt;Structural systems where thermal expansion occurs freely without introducing additional stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Structural systems where the internal normal force is found from equilibrium alone, and thermal expansion causes displacement without generating additional stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;A category of mechanics problems where support reactions can be determined using equilibrium equations alone, exemplified by a hanging rod under self-weight.&lt;SEP&gt;Structural systems where free expansion is permitted, preventing thermal strains from generating internal forces.&lt;SEP&gt;Structural systems where equilibrium, constitutive law, and kinematics can be solved sequentially, unlike statically indeterminate systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Systems where thermal expansion does not produce stresses</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59e8bd68bf620257999ef2c55d39d49c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed4c24e6b357be46b6c720d32cdb5f62</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Axial Elongation (Δl)">
  <data key="d0">Total Axial Elongation (Δl)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The total change in length of a prismatic bar under combined mechanical and thermal loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Point Load (F)">
  <data key="d0">Axial Point Load (F)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The constant normal force or Einzelkraft applied to the prismatic bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bar Length (l)">
  <data key="d0">Bar Length (l)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The total length of the prismatic bar being analyzed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Stiffness (EA)">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Stiffness (EA)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The product of Young's modulus and cross-sectional area (Dehnsteifigkeit), representing the resistance of the bar to elastic deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Expansion Coefficient (αT)">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Expansion Coefficient (αT)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A material property representing the rate at which a material expands per degree of temperature change.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Temperature Change (ΔT)">
  <data key="d0">Temperature Change (ΔT)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The uniform temperature differential applied to the bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Young's Modulus (E)">
  <data key="d0">Young's Modulus (E)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Modulus of elasticity, a material parameter used in Hooke's Law to relate stress and strain&lt;SEP&gt;A measure of the stiffness of a solid material (Elastizitätsmodul), defining the relationship between stress and strain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-sectional Area (A)">
  <data key="d0">Cross-sectional Area (A)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The geometric area (Querschnittsfläche) of the cross-section of a structural member over which the internal force distributes&lt;SEP&gt;The area of the cross-section of the prismatic bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prismatic Bar">
  <data key="d0">Prismatic Bar</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A straight, structural element with a constant cross-section, subjected to axial loads.&lt;SEP&gt;A structural member with a uniform cross-section that produces uniform stress, contrasting with a conical bar.&lt;SEP&gt;A structural member with constant axial stiffness that exhibits a linear displacement distribution under end loads when no distributed load is present.&lt;SEP&gt;A structural member (Einzelstab) subjected to combined mechanical and thermal loading, whose total axial elongation is analyzed by the equation.&lt;SEP&gt;A structural member (Einzelstab) under axial loading for which the elongation formula specifically applies&lt;SEP&gt;A structural element that undergoes longitudinal thermal expansion when subjected to uniform temperature changes</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-738c698a563cc3405e3c1de5794fa318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b5a4dfb0ae1aefebcb3a9f19f155c76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a81d55be35cb5a2d86799183a37d1295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-635877c2ca4bd8652a8de3e6ef23d5f9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain-Displacement Relation">
  <data key="d0">Strain-Displacement Relation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical relation ∫du = ∫ε dx integrated along the bar's length, from which the Total Axial Elongation Equation is derived.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Force (N)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Force (N)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The internal axial force in the bar, equal to the applied axial point load (N = F) under the equilibrium condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress (σ)">
  <data key="d0">Stress (σ)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The measure of internal force represented on the vertical axis of the Stress-Strain Diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;The internal stress in the bar cross-section, calculated as σ = N/A = F/A, used in Hooke's Law to derive strain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73a8a17ee36ec3a25041cb22c3b85cd5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain (ε)">
  <data key="d0">Strain (ε)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strain defined kinematically via displacement u(x), paired with stress to form the two pillars of the constitutive model (Stoffgesetz)&lt;SEP&gt;The measure of deformation represented on the horizontal axis of the Stress-Strain Diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;The mechanical strain of the bar material, defined by Hooke's Law as ε = σ/E.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73a8a17ee36ec3a25041cb22c3b85cd5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Baustatik">
  <data key="d0">Baustatik</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The field of structural analysis in which the Total Axial Elongation Equation is used for analyzing thermal prestressing, designing expansion joints, and computing displacements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distributed Axial Load (n)">
  <data key="d0">Distributed Axial Load (n)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A distributed load along the bar's length, specified as zero (n = 0) as a special case condition for deriving the equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="A_erf">
  <data key="d0">A_erf</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The variable denoting the required cross-sectional area (erforderliche Querschnittsfläche), representing the minimum geometric area that ensures structural safety.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2a6dab57c43d26f48e0a5a1f5535c85</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896848</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Equilibrium is a fundamental pillar and basic concept in engineering mechanics and structural analysis, representing a physical state of balance where internal forces balance external loads. It serves as a foundational principle for analyzing mechanical systems, providing the logical framework necessary for single bar analysis. By imposing a condition of force balance within a system, equilibrium enables engineers to derive support reactions and establish the governing differential equations for statically determinate problems.

Mathematically, equilibrium manifests in several ways depending on the specific structural context. It dictates that the normal force equals the applied axial load (N = F) and establishes that the derivative of the normal force equals the negative distributed load. Furthermore, it represents the precise condition of force balance across specific geometric axes, such as the projection of forces onto the vertical axis—which contributes a cosine factor to the overall equilibrium equation. This physical principle is also expressed through formulas demonstrating how internal structural forces counteract external loads, such as the relationship 2S₁ cos α + S₂ = F.

In practical structural and mechanical applications, equilibrium is utilized to derive the initial relationships between forces acting at specific connection points, such as node K. Known in German as "Gleichgewicht," it is also recognized as one of three essential conditions that must be evaluated simultaneously when analyzing statically indeterminate bar systems. Through these diverse mechanical and mathematical representations, equilibrium remains the essential physical state from which structural behaviors, force distributions, and system stabilities are determined.&lt;SEP&gt;A state of physical balance maintained by corresponding stress vectors acting on the opposite faces of the square element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e31c68f0ed98d537493847b61aa6024c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad1fe24f4724db937ef65aa2cd2c2fab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edab2d4bca40b7e16cfc072b04cebe0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cdbbf7d68148fe88c3174f0ea8e4742</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903914</data>
