  <data key="d2">A structural component used as an example to establish the relationship between elementary stress theory and the dimensioning process.&lt;SEP&gt;A pressure-loaded structural member with a truncated cone geometry whose radius varies linearly along its longitudinal axis.&lt;SEP&gt;A tapered (non-prismatic) mechanical bar depicted in two technical diagrams showing 3D and 2D representations, subjected to constant axial compressive forces at both ends&lt;SEP&gt;An axially loaded bar with conical geometry and circular cross-sections, having end radii r_0 and 2r_0 and total length l.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical object in the shape of a cone, used as an example for cross-sectional area calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;A structural member in the context of which mechanical stress was discussed prior to the technical normal strain equation.</data>
