<node id="Structural Mechanics">
  <data key="d0">Structural Mechanics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural Mechanics is a fundamental field of engineering, physics, and engineering mechanics concerned with analyzing the behavior of solid objects and structures under various loading conditions. Also referred to by the German academic term *Festigkeitslehre*, this subject encompasses the comprehensive study of material behavior, load-bearing capacity, deformations, strains, stress distributions, and support reactions in structural members.

The theoretical framework of Structural Mechanics relies on the fundamental principles of equilibrium, kinematics, and constitutive laws, including Hooke's Law. The discipline utilizes essential formulations such as the normal stress equation, the axial force equilibrium equation, and the differential equilibrium condition for one-dimensional bar elements under axial loading. Additionally, displacement equations act as central results within the field, demonstrating the simultaneous application of equilibrium, constitutive laws, and kinematics to resolve structural behavior.

In practice, Structural Mechanics is applied to the analysis of specific components such as axially loaded members, columns, and truss systems, including two-bar trusses. The field extensively evaluates both statically determinate and statically indeterminate bar systems, concurrent force systems, and complex structures. To solve these problems, particularly statically indeterminate systems, engineers apply compatibility conditions and geometric boundary constraints. The superposition method is a fundamental approach utilized within this framework to resolve static indeterminacy and to analyze thermal stresses in constrained structures. Furthermore, Structural Mechanics broadly encompasses the analysis of structural systems subjected to combined thermomechanical loading and various environmental conditions, ensuring their stability and integrity under diverse operational demands.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of engineering where the simplified single-index notation for normal stresses is widely applied.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of study where stress transformation equations are critical for analyzing components where critical planes are not aligned with coordinate axes.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of engineering where Mohr's Circle constructions and stress transformation analysis are applied.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of study in which the equilibrium equation for spherical pressure vessels is fundamental.&lt;SEP&gt;An application domain for the equilibrium equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b926a0a835e6812b4f20902d253d486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47064af1c6b2b2b7fb34a9c6b98111d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b9805139d42f6eef23df04f62f07811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c527b23d18394e188c355b1b1185acec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d49057dad0c3acc1a3ce1afb3e687a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1db4055b5d566c7d454787a7ff1515b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd72d98943d7e0523296223aa7032d98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7f9fd51bacb1b5261081565de649fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abc3cadf3a789dbb882aa778366ab3b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f99cae1296009661e572167acf5fe19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-542bf3a4290d267cd5257b52563c594f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-413d03b5c0b6ff01a4da279c609d09f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1626d6e3dffee746e5efebf5c0f44ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7fa545fe4bb1d6483b658ddec935e5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904014</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Node C">
  <data key="d0">Node C</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific point in a structural system where node equilibrium analysis is performed in the context of statically determinate bar systems.&lt;SEP&gt;A point in the free-body diagram represented by a small open circle where three force vectors originate, central to analyzing equilibrium of a statically determinate bar system&lt;SEP&gt;The connection node in the two-bar system where equilibrium conditions are applied and displacements are determined&lt;SEP&gt;A specific point in the mechanical system where equilibrium conditions are calculated and displacements are determined.&lt;SEP&gt;The nodal point in the statically determinate two-bar system where the vertical load is applied and horizontal displacement occurs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fe61c354100bb43a98ebc62d57cf28c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71dad38576055c6e013578d06064a913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc78afbb216db05f32ee167a8643581f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64ae8f78050d6e3fb28375ca609b6390&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c113cfbbfa4f8142d716b5538931026</data>
