</node>
<node id="Tensile Stiffness">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Stiffness</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A compound material-geometric parameter representing the resistance of the bar to axial deformation, calculated as Young's Modulus multiplied by cross-sectional area.&lt;SEP&gt;The product EA (Dehnsteifigkeit), a key parameter in structural analysis representing resistance to axial deformation&lt;SEP&gt;The product EA representing resistance to axial deformation, shared equally by both bars in the system</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fe61c354100bb43a98ebc62d57cf28c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-738c698a563cc3405e3c1de5794fa318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896876</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Axial Force">
  <data key="d0">Internal Axial Force</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal force N induced in the rod by the boundary constraints, which creates mechanical strains that exactly offset the thermal expansion.&lt;SEP&gt;The force S within a bar, determined from equilibrium conditions at node C&lt;SEP&gt;The internal force ($S_i$) within each bar of the three-bar system, which Hooke's Law relates to the change in length, initial length, and axial rigidity</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fe61c354100bb43a98ebc62d57cf28c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe1aa2ab22c57b58f370cf064cf7ce98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abc3cadf3a789dbb882aa778366ab3b3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896876</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elongation">
  <data key="d0">Elongation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The physical extension of the solid body's length as a result of applied tensile forces.&lt;SEP&gt;The deformation or change in length of a bar under load, calculated as Δl = Nl/EA, where N is normal force, l is length, E is elastic modulus, and A is cross-sectional area&lt;SEP&gt;The total change in length of the bar, represented by Δl, calculated using the classical elongation formula.&lt;SEP&gt;Elongation Δl = u(l) computed from the displacement field solution at the free end&lt;SEP&gt;The change in length, denoted as $\Delta l$, of an axially loaded structural member, derived from the displacement field.&lt;SEP&gt;The extension of the vertically hanging rod (denoted as Δl), calculated using the axial force distribution equation.&lt;SEP&gt;The change in length (Δl) of a bar computed using Hooke's Law for axial deformation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fe61c354100bb43a98ebc62d57cf28c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dfca5c7dbae86836330061b0f49bc01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896876</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Indeterminate Systems">
  <data key="d0">Statically Indeterminate Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Statically Indeterminate Systems are structural systems and a category of mechanics problems in which reaction forces and internal forces cannot be determined solely by the equations of static equilibrium. Unlike statically determinate systems, where variables can be solved sequentially, the analysis of statically indeterminate systems requires the simultaneous solution of equilibrium conditions, constitutive laws, and kinematic relations. When subjected to external loading, these systems mandate the use of additional compatibility equations, specifically enforcing displacement compatibility, to accurately resolve the unknown forces.

A critical characteristic of Statically Indeterminate Systems is their behavior under temperature changes. Because thermal expansion is structurally constrained within these systems, thermal strains inevitably generate additional internal forces and stresses. A quintessential example used to illustrate this mechanic is a clamped rod subjected to thermal expansion.

Within the academic study of structural mechanics, Statically Indeterminate Systems serve as a fundamental topic. Their detailed treatment typically follows the foundational discussion of statically determinate systems, such as beginning on page 19 or being featured in Section 10.6 of a standard lecture document. The study of these systems is also prominently featured in technical mechanics literature, including documents that cite the textbook "Technische Mechanik 2".</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fc1a89775e67ed1be0c24a11e1773f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-738c698a563cc3405e3c1de5794fa318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdbe00f1344b6e8391f5aa4709a1d925&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fed5dfab36680bc0a576b0ed9cc7e5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59e8bd68bf620257999ef2c55d39d49c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f99cae1296009661e572167acf5fe19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed4c24e6b357be46b6c720d32cdb5f62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-635877c2ca4bd8652a8de3e6ef23d5f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2866a3f417ff78b075338fa558392e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
