<node id="Superposition Principle">
  <data key="d0">Superposition Principle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The principle stating that total strain is the sum of independent mechanical and thermal contributions, holding under linear-elastic, isotropic material behavior within the proportional limit.&lt;SEP&gt;A principle stating that the total strain is the sum of independent strain contributions for linear-elastic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;The principle stating that total strain equals the sum of mechanical and thermal strain components, valid under small deformations and linear-elastic material behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;A principle referenced in an alternative solution approach where thermal expansion and mechanical constraint effects are combined.&lt;SEP&gt;A method that transforms a statically indeterminate thermoelastic problem into two solvable sub-problems: (0) pure thermal expansion in a released system, and (1) mechanical correction enforcing compatibility&lt;SEP&gt;A problem-solving approach that decomposes a problem into statically determinate and loaded subsystems, enforcing compatibility.&lt;SEP&gt;A principle central to linear elasticity exemplified by the elongation calculation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fe61c354100bb43a98ebc62d57cf28c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c38a79e5e8255bb32751c63ad9a1aca1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-542bf3a4290d267cd5257b52563c594f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2866a3f417ff78b075338fa558392e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de</data>
