<node id="Young's Modulus">
  <data key="d0">Young's Modulus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Young's Modulus, also referred to as the modulus of elasticity or Elastizitätsmodul, is a scalar material property that defines the stiffness of an elastic material. Universally denoted by the variable E, it serves as a material-specific proportionality constant that dictates how a structure or component resists deformation when subjected to an applied load. 

As the foundational constant in Hooke's Law and the linear-elastic constitutive relation, Young's Modulus governs the linear-elastic behavior of materials by acting as the inverse scaling factor between mechanical stress and strain. It is prominently featured in the mechanical strain equation ($\varepsilon = \sigma/E$) to calculate mechanical strain. This property is measured in units of pressure, such as Pascals or Newtons per square meter (N/m²), and is frequently scaled to megapascals (MPa) for practical engineering applications.

In structural mechanics, Young's Modulus is an essential parameter utilized across various governing equations for axial displacement and deformation. For structural components such as uniaxial bar elements and rods, E is applied to calculate axial rigidity (EA), internal normal forces, the elongation of bar segments, and elastic shortenings. It appears explicitly in the mechanical elongation terms of compatibility equations, the axial force equation, and the governing differential equations for structural materials. Throughout these analytical contexts, Young's Modulus is consistently treated as an assumed constant that accurately represents the elastic stiffness of the material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fe61c354100bb43a98ebc62d57cf28c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-738c698a563cc3405e3c1de5794fa318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a371746d42ee15c97ccf2f983b9237ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe1aa2ab22c57b58f370cf064cf7ce98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dfca5c7dbae86836330061b0f49bc01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-055d89277cf2e87034384489ad8dc567&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-783555881f4ab73d4d04ea548b9fae1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a093ad0a488172322e66eed43c540981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abc3cadf3a789dbb882aa778366ab3b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c38a79e5e8255bb32751c63ad9a1aca1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e6428916bed03d77d29954942400a6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c71f393772a794391e49730d47a8bda4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed4c24e6b357be46b6c720d32cdb5f62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896911</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bar 1">
  <data key="d0">Bar 1</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">"Bar 1" refers to a structural member analyzed across various distinct mechanical and structural engineering problems, specifically within statically determinate base, two-bar, and three-bar systems. Due to variations across different problem contexts, the descriptions of "Bar 1" can be categorized into two primary structural configurations.

In the context of statically determinate two-bar and base systems, "Bar 1" is frequently analyzed for its length change due to pure thermal expansion. In one specific two-bar system, it is described as a horizontal structural member extending to the right from Point A and terminating at Joint C. In this horizontal configuration, the bar is subjected to compression, carrying an internal force of S₁ = -F/tan(α). Alternatively, within a separate two-bar truss system, "Bar 1" is described as a vertical bar.

In another distinct context involving a three-bar structural system, "Bar 1" represents an inclined structural bar. It is suspended from a fixed support on the left, pinned at its upper end to a rigid horizontal ceiling, and connected at its lower end to node K. This bar is symmetrically inclined towards the center at an angle α relative to both the central bar and the vertical and horizontal axes. Characterized by an axial stiffness or axial rigidity of EA₁, the bar carries an internal axial force S₁. The mechanical behavior of this inclined bar dictates that its elongation, denoted as Δl₁, is determined by the projection of node K's displacement along the bar's axis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fe61c354100bb43a98ebc62d57cf28c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed3c14262e18f66ed415c1b72900af48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b9805139d42f6eef23df04f62f07811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad1fe24f4724db937ef65aa2cd2c2fab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d49057dad0c3acc1a3ce1afb3e687a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f72457639c5767731980d0c54a4fe069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1db4055b5d566c7d454787a7ff1515b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9966746921724686a393e234327d9e41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-542bf3a4290d267cd5257b52563c594f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94aa1e1d6b738ff40ac94957c9a7084a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896915</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-sectional Area">
  <data key="d0">Cross-sectional Area</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Cross-sectional Area, commonly denoted by the symbol A or A(x) and sometimes referred to by its German term Querschnittsfläche, is a fundamental geometric property of an object, structural member, bar, or rod. It is typically measured in units such as square millimeters. Depending on the specific geometry of the object, this area can be considered a constant value, such as in a uniform hanging rod, or it can vary along the longitudinal axis as a function of a linearly changing radius. For example, in a conical bar with a circular cross-section, the area is calculated using the formula A(x) = π·r(x)².

In structural mechanics and engineering, the cross-sectional area is crucial for analyzing how materials respond to physical forces. It is a primary component used in the definition of normal stress within a material. Furthermore, when multiplied by Young's Modulus (E), the cross-sectional area is used to determine axial stiffness and axial rigidity. This property is also utilized to calculate internal forces from stress via the mathematical product EA·u'(x). Additionally, the area serves as an essential parameter in Hooke's Law for computing axial deformation and is an integral feature in the governing differential equations for structural analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fe61c354100bb43a98ebc62d57cf28c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf7cfa532d0224ec6532c6354495df59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ec7d98bfb03bb42a21ae886e54257cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe1aa2ab22c57b58f370cf064cf7ce98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dfca5c7dbae86836330061b0f49bc01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Equilibrium Conditions, also known by their original German names Gleichgewichtsbedingung or Gleichgewichtsbedingungen, are fundamental mechanical principles that dictate the balance of forces and moments required for a physical system or structure to remain in static equilibrium. As foundational conditions, they describe the relationship of stress tensor components and stress states within mechanical bodies. In the field of structural analysis, these conditions are essential for maintaining static balance and ensuring the overall stability of structural systems.

In structural analysis and elastostatics, Equilibrium Conditions represent one of the three fundamental equation types—alongside Hooke's Law and kinematic compatibility conditions—necessary to solve bar and rod problems. They form a simultaneous system of equations used to determine stresses, deformations, and internal forces, such as axial forces. In practical applications, such as single-bar element analysis (*Einzelstab*), these conditions are applied to free-body diagrams. By making a cut perpendicular to the bar axis, engineers establish foundational mathematical relationships like the axial force equilibrium equation (N = F). Furthermore, they are vital for analyzing both statically determinate and statically indeterminate systems, ensuring static balance through mathematical formulations expressed as B − C = 0 within the force method (*Kraftgrößenverfahren*). This also includes analyzing balanced states derived from free-body diagrams where the force from internal pressure is balanced by the resultant force of tensile stresses. Beyond evaluating individual elements, node equilibrium analysis (such as at node C) is used to assess the stability of entire bar systems. Solving these initial force balance equations at the nodes serves as the critical first step to calculating subsequent elongations, compressions, and overall displacements.

Additionally, Equilibrium Conditions are extensively applied at the infinitesimal scale to derive stress transformation equations. By applying the principle that the sum of forces equals zero in specific directions to an infinitesimal triangular element extracted from a thin plate (*Scheibe*) under plane stress conditions, engineers formulate the mathematical basis for deriving the three fundamental stress transformation equations.</data>
