<node id="Cross-Sectional Area">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Sectional Area</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cross-Sectional Area is a fundamental geometric property of a structural member or rod, defined as the internal surface area exposed when the structural bar is theoretically cut. Universally denoted by the symbol A, this property is typically measured in square meters. While it is frequently assumed to be constant across a straight bar, a structural member can also feature varying geometries along its length. In such instances, different cross-sectional areas are assigned to distinct segments of the rod, commonly denoted as A1 for the first segment and A2 for the second.

In structural mechanics and engineering, Cross-Sectional Area is critical for evaluating material behavior under load. It is a primary component in the stress formula σ = N/A, representing the specific area across which stress is assumed to be uniformly distributed. Additionally, this geometric property is integral to mechanical elongation calculations, a context where it is occasionally referred to by the German term Querschnittsfläche. Furthermore, the Cross-Sectional Area of a structural member is directly combined with Young's modulus to compute the axial stiffness and overall axial rigidity of the section, making it an indispensable parameter in the analysis of structural integrity and deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-738c698a563cc3405e3c1de5794fa318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61bee0456fb356f553c6809b0f9fb7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a81d55be35cb5a2d86799183a37d1295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abc3cadf3a789dbb882aa778366ab3b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c38a79e5e8255bb32751c63ad9a1aca1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896959</data>
