<node id="Elastostatics">
  <data key="d0">Elastostatics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Elastostatics is a foundational branch of mechanics situated within the broader disciplines of continuum mechanics, engineering mechanics, and technical mechanics. The field is concerned with analyzing the behavior of elastic solid bodies at rest or subjected to static loads and forces that produce deformation. As a classical theoretical framework, Elastostatics focuses on understanding the equilibrium of these bodies, specifically exploring elastic deformation and internal stress states under load. The domain extensively covers core mechanical concepts, including stress analysis, strain, and constitutive laws.

In practical engineering and physical analysis, Elastostatics deals with statically determinate problems and examines the behavior of structural elements, such as bars under tension and compression. It is utilized to predict deformation profiles, such as those of a statically determinate vertical member subjected to gravity. The field also explores the critical relationship between internal forces and external distributed loads. Key theoretical components utilized in these analyses include the differential equilibrium equation and the force method, both of which connect broader mechanical principles to practical application.

In an academic context, Elastostatics is a standard subject that provides the foundational framework for lecture content within the technical mechanics curriculum. It is extensively covered in educational literature, which frequently utilizes schematic diagrams to illustrate mechanical principles. Most notably, Elastostatics is a core topic featured in Volume 2 of the "Technische Mechanik" textbook series, specifically titled "Technische Mechanik 2: Elastostatik." Within this academic course and textbook, the subject serves as a primary reference and standard for students studying the elastic deformation of materials and internal stress states under load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e05e8f931eaf44276170a34134cf8c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17dfe04089064853421a9f1af5f1cb77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c527b23d18394e188c355b1b1185acec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ec7d98bfb03bb42a21ae886e54257cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f2ecbe17c739fbd7bdf88a5cb116b3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7acbcd6b20eaf7e62181f096df8a5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b87724a453e2805522671a76c088ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10de6334f28b94ed6f4ea9af906f66c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7191762e3c837260ad5bf3f1bc50f25a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd50f7cb97e77515d873719c81dafca9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a93c57c2e2028051403eda908d55fe3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0dae0b4038721550e833d3e9407ccd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45f0f3e4c4e1775f77972c4268710cbb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2866a3f417ff78b075338fa558392e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94aa1e1d6b738ff40ac94957c9a7084a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eae0b855416df86edb6af7ac8d0407fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e6a6756f3e11958caa7f25916a94bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61ee301f89076e345159fdaef391ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d59edb59253a1c7bdee6d99d3f2cc4d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ea2012f708d083bcf1f8357bfab292&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56ba60dee95d9f5127479d5f80c141c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e5f0e058d84e9de59b337c2234e2ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0f20e6bdef6f836f08f3407dd794183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e3cdc6151a0814d04d2cc632b0969f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f44965262ad3a19924f66261ac5694c5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
