<node id="σ(x=0) = 4·σ(x=l)">
  <data key="d0">σ(x=0) = 4·σ(x=l)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation evaluating the normal stress function σ(x) at the two boundary points of an axially loaded conical bar, establishing a quantitative 4:1 stress ratio between the narrower end (x=0) and the wider end (x=l).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Compressive Force F">
  <data key="d0">Axial Compressive Force F</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A constant axial compressive force applied to the conical bar, resulting in a uniform internal normal force N(x) = -F along the bar's length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Normal Force N(x)">
  <data key="d0">Internal Normal Force N(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The internal normal force within the conical bar, equal to -F and uniform along the entire length l of the bar.&lt;SEP&gt;The force represented by $E \cdot A \cdot u'$, varying linearly as $N(x) = G(1 - x/l)$ in a statically determinate hanging bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dfca5c7dbae86836330061b0f49bc01</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-sectional Area A(x)">
  <data key="d0">Cross-sectional Area A(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The circular cross-sectional area at position x, varying from πr_0^2 at the narrower end (x=0) to 4πr_0^2 at the wider end (x=l).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Solid Mechanics">
  <data key="d0">Solid Mechanics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Solid Mechanics is a comprehensive branch of physics and technical mechanics dedicated to analyzing the behavior of solid materials under stress. As a fundamental academic field, it provides the theoretical framework for material strength assessment and the production of detailed engineering documentation. Within this discipline, the analysis of stress distributions is paramount, frequently utilizing specific mathematical formulations, such as the normal stress equation defined as σ(x) = N(x)/A(x), to evaluate internal forces and satisfy equilibrium conditions within solid objects.

The field relies heavily on advanced mathematical concepts, specifically utilizing the Cauchy stress tensor and symmetric stress tensors to establish its constitutive equations. Stress transformation formulations are fundamental tools in Solid Mechanics, a characteristic it shares with the broader study of continuum mechanics. These concepts are commonly applied to evaluate plane stress transformations and are frequently illustrated using coordinate transformation diagrams, which are a staple of the field's technical visual style.

Furthermore, Solid Mechanics involves the detailed evaluation of material failure and structural integrity. Engineers and physicists in this domain analyze principal stresses and apply failure criteria, often utilizing mathematical parameters like the first stress invariant to comprehensively predict how solid materials will perform under various loading conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f7f241f746726b97e3dc567365e483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6efac359e95ae176e5b41c44f20b3bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65f35adb2f34cbe81a9755002339ad40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dbabf4bbc38075b362efc1cf68c92ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Required Cross-sectional Area A_erf">
  <data key="d0">Required Cross-sectional Area A_erf</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The minimum cross-sectional area needed to prevent material failure in structural design, directly informed by stress concentration principles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="End Radius r_0">
  <data key="d0">End Radius r_0</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The radius of the narrower end (x=0) of the conical bar, which results in a circular cross-sectional area of πr_0^2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="End Radius 2r_0">
  <data key="d0">End Radius 2r_0</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The radius of the wider end (x=l) of the conical bar, twice the value of r_0, resulting in a circular cross-sectional area of 4πr_0^2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structural Design">
  <data key="d0">Structural Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An engineering discipline in which the principle of stress concentration in smaller cross-sections directly informs the required cross-sectional area to prevent material failure.&lt;SEP&gt;An engineering discipline that relies on stress transformation and Mohr's circle for analyzing structural integrity.&lt;SEP&gt;Engineering application field where stress transformation equations are essential for determining stress components in any direction&lt;SEP&gt;The field of designing structures, which relies on the theoretical basis provided by stress analysis and failure criteria.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of engineering to which principal stresses and their associated failure criteria are central.&lt;SEP&gt;An engineering application area where the identification of principal angles and stresses is critical.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of engineering where principal stress analysis is considered fundamental.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae7aec8cf21235bd79c851b9cc6978f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553477d678ccfb56c56a99c6a713a1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Failure">
  <data key="d0">Material Failure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The failure mode that proper determination of the required cross-sectional area (A_erf) aims to prevent in structural design.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a453764f8253f8b0d724e7b0d2878c7c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896984</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation u = |Δl₁| = (Fl)/(EA·tan α)">
  <data key="d0">Equation u = |Δl₁| = (Fl)/(EA·tan α)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation representing the horizontal displacement of nodal point C in a statically determinate two-bar system under a vertical load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fc78afbb216db05f32ee167a8643581f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
