  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Tension">
  <data key="d0">Axial Tension</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mechanical force that causes the solid body to stretch.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Initial Position">
  <data key="d0">Initial Position</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The original state of the solid body before deformation, referred to as 'Ausgangslage' in the schematic.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="After Deformation">
  <data key="d0">After Deformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The elongated state of the solid body following the application of tensile forces, referred to as 'Nach der Verformung'.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Original Length ($l$)">
  <data key="d0">Original Length ($l$)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable representing the initial length of the solid body before deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Change In Length ($\Delta l$)">
  <data key="d0">Change In Length ($\Delta l$)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable representing the difference in length of the solid body after deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-sectional Area ($A$)">
  <data key="d0">Cross-sectional Area ($A$)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The constant cross-sectional area of the solid body, denoted as $A = \text{konst.}$</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Force ($F$)">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Force ($F$)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">External forces applied to both longitudinal ends of the solid body, represented by prominent red arrows.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Technical Strain ($\varepsilon$)">
  <data key="d0">Technical Strain ($\varepsilon$)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A physical parameter calculated using the change in length, specifically demonstrating a positive strain where $\Delta l &gt; 0$.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Deformation">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Deformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The change in shape or size of an object, specifically affecting a solid body under axial tension in this context.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Positive Strain">
  <data key="d0">Positive Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific instance of technical strain characterized by elongation, mathematically indicated by a change in length greater than zero.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tension And Compression In Bars">
  <data key="d0">Tension And Compression In Bars</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The overarching mechanical engineering topic indicated by the image path, concerning the study of axial forces in structural members.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4bbb3c0a685ba49ba367e68297459ff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Indeterminate Bar Systems">
  <data key="d0">Statically Indeterminate Bar Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An engineering mechanics topic discussed in Section 10.6, positioned near the cited footer content on page 18.&lt;SEP&gt;Structural systems (statisch unbestimmte Stabsysteme) where equilibrium alone is insufficient to solve for internal forces and must be supplemented by kinematic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanical engineering topic involving systems that cannot be solved using static equilibrium equations alone, covered in the page content attributed to Technische Mechanik 2&lt;SEP&gt;Constrained bar systems where equilibrium, elasticity law, and kinematics must be considered simultaneously, and temperature changes cause thermal stresses&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanics concept discussed in the surrounding educational material sourced from the textbook.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f2ecbe17c739fbd7bdf88a5cb116b3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d49057dad0c3acc1a3ce1afb3e687a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ad3e7d061ebaf26776716772bd7daeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-42f0665902c9df9b5ec4573eb02f74a3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Verschiebungspläne">
  <data key="d0">Verschiebungspläne</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Displacement diagrams, a specific elastostatics topic covered in the referenced textbook.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f2ecbe17c739fbd7bdf88a5cb116b3a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Truss Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Truss Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An area of engineering mechanics where kinematic relationships are studied using methodological content adapted from the cited textbook.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f2ecbe17c739fbd7bdf88a5cb116b3a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Constitutive Relationship For Axial Deformation">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Constitutive Relationship For Axial Deformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A first-order ordinary differential equation representing axial deformation of a rod under combined mechanical and thermal loading, relating the displacement field u(x) to internal normal force, material properties, and thermal effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Displacement Function">
  <data key="d0">Axial Displacement Function</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The spatial function u(x) representing the axial displacement of a rod along the longitudinal coordinate x, obtained by integrating the strain field.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Axial Strain">
  <data key="d0">Total Axial Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The total strain of the rod equal to the spatial derivative du/dx of the axial displacement, consisting of the sum of mechanical and thermal strain components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Rigidity">
  <data key="d0">Axial Rigidity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The ratio EA combining Young's modulus and cross-sectional area, representing the axial stiffness of the cross-section against deformation.&lt;SEP&gt;The product of Young's Modulus and cross-sectional area ($EA$), isolated on the left side of the equation.&lt;SEP&gt;The product of Young's Modulus (E) and cross-sectional area (A), denoted as EA, representing a bar's resistance to axial deformation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe1aa2ab22c57b58f370cf064cf7ce98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Longitudinal Coordinate">
  <data key="d0">Longitudinal Coordinate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Represented by x, the longitudinal coordinate along the bar's axis, used as the spatial variable in the differential equilibrium equation.&lt;SEP&gt;The position coordinate along the axis of the bar, measured in millimeters.&lt;SEP&gt;The spatial coordinate x along the axis of the rod, with respect to which the axial displacement function is defined.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-413d03b5c0b6ff01a4da279c609d09f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compatibility Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Compatibility Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Kinematic conditions that must be solved simultaneously with equilibrium equations and the governing differential equation in statically indeterminate systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Conditions such as Δl = Δl₁ + Δl₂ = 0 used for analyzing more complex configurations within the elastostatic analysis framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical constraints that supplement equilibrium and constitutive equations when analyzing statically indeterminate cases.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dfca5c7dbae86836330061b0f49bc01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium Equations">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium Equations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Equations used to determine the internal normal force in a structure, required alongside compatibility conditions for statically indeterminate systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Equations for force balance in the three-bar system, connected to the final solution through intermediate derivation steps on page 24.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5d7eea8f9e0a901768256832130203b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linear-elastic Material Behavior">
  <data key="d0">Linear-elastic Material Behavior</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of material response characterized by linear proportionality between stress and strain, governed by Hooke's Model.&lt;SEP&gt;An assumption required for the validity of the superposition principle, where material response follows a linear relationship between stress and strain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e6d370949ced2e82caca9443dede62a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Integration Constants">
  <data key="d0">Integration Constants</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Constants, such as C_1 and C_2, that arise during the integration of the differential equation and are determined using boundary conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Constants $C_1$ and $C_2$ in the general solution of the ODE, determined by the physical boundary conditions of the bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dfca5c7dbae86836330061b0f49bc01</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Technical Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Technical Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A minimalist technical diagram found in engineering and physics textbooks showing an infinitesimal segment of a one-dimensional object with two parallel vertical boundary lines, a green horizontal double-headed arrow indicating length, and the mathematical notation 'dx' above it.&lt;SEP&gt;Image consisting of three technical diagrams labeled 'a', 'b', and 'c', arranged horizontally, illustrating the mechanical analysis of a statically indeterminate bar system using high-contrast black lines for the structure, red for static forces, and green for kinematic displacements.&lt;SEP&gt;The visual image providing a graphical representation of Mohr's circle, illustrating the pure shear stress state with labeled points and axes in a Cartesian coordinate system&lt;SEP&gt;A technical diagram used in mechanics and materials science to represent a state of stress within the context of Mohr's circle, using a Cartesian coordinate system with black axes and text on a white background.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cebf917adc3d92ce2d5e155d10154396&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74378ee7045b50cecf2c625c47685477&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2662124ebaa998fdaa13ffecb3add380</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sub-figure A">
  <data key="d0">Sub-figure A</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical structural model displaying three bars numbered 1, 2, and 3 pinned to a rigid horizontal ceiling at their upper ends, converging at a lower joint labeled K. Bar 2 is vertical with length l, while bars 1 and 3 are symmetrically inclined at angle α.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sub-figure B">
  <data key="d0">Sub-figure B</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Free-body diagram focusing on node K, visualizing internal axial forces S1, S2, and S3 within the bars that balance the downward external load F. All force vectors are rendered in red.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sub-figure C">
  <data key="d0">Sub-figure C</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Displacement plan (Verschiebungsplan) illustrating the kinematic deformation of the system, showing node K shifting to a new position K' due to applied load, with bar elongations Δl1, Δl2, and Δl3 marked in green.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="External Force F">
  <data key="d0">External Force F</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A tensile or pulling force applied outward to the outer ends of a structural bar.&lt;SEP&gt;A load represented by a red arrow pointing vertically downward at joint C.&lt;SEP&gt;Vertical external force, represented by a thick red arrow, acting downward at node K on the statically indeterminate bar system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a81d55be35cb5a2d86799183a37d1295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94aa1e1d6b738ff40ac94957c9a7084a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Force S3">
  <data key="d0">Internal Force S3</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Axial force within bar 3 acting upward and outward along the axis of the inclined bar. Equal to S1 due to symmetry of the bar system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Indeterminate Bar System">
  <data key="d0">Statically Indeterminate Bar System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural mechanics problem involving a bar constrained in a way that creates redundant supports under thermal load.&lt;SEP&gt;Mechanical system consisting of three bars pinned to a rigid ceiling and converging at a single node, which cannot be solved by static equilibrium alone and requires compatibility conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A structural mechanics system depicted in the image, consisting of multiple bars and a fixed support.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1db4055b5d566c7d454787a7ff1515b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df979c16e8c52bf800fb84c3a4a4bdea</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Small-Displacement Assumption">
  <data key="d0">Small-Displacement Assumption</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Approximation used in the displacement plan where circular arcs of movement are replaced by straight lines (tangents) perpendicular to original bar directions, relating individual bar elongations to total vertical displacement of the node.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rigid Horizontal Ceiling">
  <data key="d0">Rigid Horizontal Ceiling</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Upper support structure to which all three bars of the statically indeterminate system are pinned at their upper ends.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="K'">
  <data key="d0">K'</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The new position of node K after kinematic deformation and displacement due to the applied load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Δl3">
  <data key="d0">Δl3</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The elongation of Bar 3, marked in green in the displacement plan.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Technical Normal Strain">
  <data key="d0">Technical Normal Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A dimensionless ratio quantifying the average relative deformation of a one-dimensional body, defined by the equation epsilon equals delta l over l.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="$\varepsilon$">
  <data key="d0">$\varepsilon$</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The symbol representing the technical strain, a scalar value representing the proportional change in length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="$\Delta l$">
  <data key="d0">$\Delta l$</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The absolute change in length, defined as the final length minus the initial length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="$l$">
  <data key="d0">$l$</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The original undeformed length of a specimen or structural member, used as a reference scalar in the strain equation.&lt;SEP&gt;The total length of the bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ec62b2ae01b69aeae754d5bb1c9dcb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastomechanics">
  <data key="d0">Elastomechanics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The specific application domain and context within which the technical normal strain equation is situated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Local Continuous Strain Formulation">
  <data key="d0">Local Continuous Strain Formulation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The local formulation of strain given by the derivative of the displacement field, used for elements with variable cross-sections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structural Engineering">
  <data key="d0">Structural Engineering</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural Engineering is a broad and practical field of engineering focused on the design and analysis of structures that support or resist loads. As a comprehensive engineering framework, it utilizes advanced mathematical formulations to ensure structural integrity, evaluate designs, and predict potential material failure.

A central component of this discipline is the application of stress analysis to determine whether a component's stress remains within admissible limits when subjected to a load. Structural engineering heavily relies on concepts such as the stress tensor for overall structural design and evaluation. By utilizing stress transformation formulations, engineers are able to determine principal stresses and maximum shear stresses. The calculation of these principal and shear stresses is vital for accurately predicting material behavior and preventing structural collapse.

Furthermore, the field applies specific mathematical equations to compute displacement and deformation responses. Engineers utilize the strain equation to calculate length changes in structural members, alongside formulations to compute axial deformations and internal stress distributions. A fundamental application of these principles is observed in the linear stress distribution of axially loaded bars. This specific analytical approach is essential for the practical design and safety evaluation of critical structural components, including tension members, cables, and vertical hangers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d97fb385691a70a5c314b2a77a107e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904084</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Science">
  <data key="d0">Material Science</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A field of study where the strain equation is used to determine material limits such as yield strain.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of science that is the thematic context of the surrounding lecture content.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a438fa813ee176d3e3d64cd5a7e2dba</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statics">
  <data key="d0">Statics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The study of internal normal force and stress, from which the study transitions to kinematics via the strain equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constitutive Laws">
  <data key="d0">Constitutive Laws</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress-strain relationships that fully describe elastic body behavior, for which the strain equation lays the foundation.&lt;SEP&gt;Laws (Stoffgesetze) that phenomenologically connect strains to stresses, formulated based on the geometric foundation of the kinematic relationship&lt;SEP&gt;Material models (Stoffgesetze) describing the physical relationship between stress and strain, which are material-dependent.&lt;SEP&gt;A topic within elastostatics typically covered in the textbook Technische Mechanik 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ae0372192096a8671eab765c6a11a90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7191762e3c837260ad5bf3f1bc50f25a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f44965262ad3a19924f66261ac5694c5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinetics">
  <data key="d0">Kinetics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The branch of mechanics dealing with applied forces, which result in geometric changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Displacement Field">
  <data key="d0">Displacement Field</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical field, denoted as u(x), that varies along the length of the bar, representing the displacement of points within the bar.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical field whose derivative provides the local, continuous formulation of strain, denoted as $du(x)/dx$.&lt;SEP&gt;The position-dependent displacement field u(x) that varies along the bar's longitudinal axis, enabling integration u(x) = ∫ε(x)dx to recover displacements from known strain distributions&lt;SEP&gt;Denoted by u(x), a position-dependent quantity describing displacement, from which local strain is derived as the first spatial derivative.&lt;SEP&gt;A field, denoted as u(x), representing the axial displacement along the rod.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba3dc6bb30208edf4354dd20376fa9f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ae0372192096a8671eab765c6a11a90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yield Strain">
  <data key="d0">Yield Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific material limit that can be determined using the technical normal strain equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Body Behavior">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Body Behavior</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The physical response of an elastic material that is fully described by constitutive laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Normal Force N">
  <data key="d0">Internal Normal Force N</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The resultant internal force acting at the cut surface of a bar to maintain equilibrium against external forces.&lt;SEP&gt;The internal normal force represented by N in Hooke's Law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a81d55be35cb5a2d86799183a37d1295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e6428916bed03d77d29954942400a6f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Sigma">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Sigma</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The intensity of internal force per unit area, acting perpendicularly to a cut surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a81d55be35cb5a2d86799183a37d1295</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Technical Stress">
  <data key="d0">Technical Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The concept of stress defined according to the principles of Augustin Louis Cauchy, assuming a uniform distribution across a cross-section.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a81d55be35cb5a2d86799183a37d1295</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cut S-s">
  <data key="d0">Cut S-s</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A theoretical cut or plane used to determine the internal forces acting within a structural element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a81d55be35cb5a2d86799183a37d1295</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Surface Forces">
  <data key="d0">Surface Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Distributed forces or stresses that act over the cross-sectional area of a cut material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a81d55be35cb5a2d86799183a37d1295</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation dN/dx + n = 0">
  <data key="d0">Equation dN/dx + n = 0</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A first-order linear ordinary differential equation representing the differential equilibrium condition for a one-dimensional structural element subjected to a distributed axial load, where the spatial gradient of the normal force must exactly balance the applied distributed load for mechanical equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="n">
  <data key="d0">n</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Distributed line load (Linienkraft) measured in force per unit length [N/m], representing the load distributed along the bar's longitudinal axis&lt;SEP&gt;A distributed line load (Streckenlast) per unit length, arising from the self-weight of the hanging bar, computed as n = G/l.&lt;SEP&gt;A variable representing the constant distributed load, defined as the weight G divided by the length l.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b87724a453e2805522671a76c088ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="G">
  <data key="d0">G</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The total weight of the structural element, used to compute the distributed line load.&lt;SEP&gt;A variable representing the total weight (Gewicht) or self-load of the bar.&lt;SEP&gt;Total self-weight force of the bar, where the constant line load n = G/l represents self-weight per unit length and l is the bar length&lt;SEP&gt;The total weight of the rod, given by G = ρgAl, where ρ is density, g is gravitational acceleration, A is cross-sectional area, and l is rod length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b87724a453e2805522671a76c088ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="u(x)">
  <data key="d0">u(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The displacement field of the bar as a function of the longitudinal coordinate, determined by solving the governing second-order differential equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hanging Bar">
  <data key="d0">Hanging Bar</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A one-dimensional structural element (rod or bar) analyzed as an example, where the distributed load arises from self-weight and specific boundary conditions at the fixed and free ends apply.&lt;SEP&gt;A statically determinate structural member suspended and subjected to its own weight.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C_1">
  <data key="d0">C_1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The integration constant determined by boundary conditions when integrating the equilibrium equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Relation">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Relation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A purely geometric relationship defining longitudinal strain as the spatial derivative of the displacement field with respect to the axial coordinate.&lt;SEP&gt;Relation ε = u'(x) defining strain as the spatial derivative of displacement&lt;SEP&gt;The relation denoted as ε = du/dx, linking strain to the derivative of displacement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4f759f99f5a784630c625df026ca993&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Definition">
  <data key="d0">Stress Definition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The relation denoted as σ = N/A, defining stress in terms of normal force and cross-sectional area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="A">
  <data key="d0">A</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable representing the constant cross-sectional area of the bar.&lt;SEP&gt;The cross-sectional area of the structural element, used in the stress definition σ = N/A.&lt;SEP&gt;The constant cross-sectional area of the hanging rod used in stress calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b87724a453e2805522671a76c088ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="E">
  <data key="d0">E</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The elastic modulus or stiffness parameter of the material, used in Hooke's law (σ = Eε) and specified as constant in the product E · A.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Governing Second-order Differential Equation">
  <data key="d0">Governing Second-order Differential Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The differential equation for the displacement field u(x) of the bar, produced by combining the equilibrium relation, kinematic relation, constitutive law, and stress definition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
