</node>
<node id="Bar Equilibrium Equation">
  <data key="d0">Bar Equilibrium Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation E·A·u' = -G/l·x + C_1 representing the integrated form of the bar's equilibrium differential equation, governing the displacement field of a hanging rod under self-weight in structural mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="First-order ODE">
  <data key="d0">First-order ODE</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A first-order ordinary differential equation that governs the displacement field of a hanging rod, of which the Bar Equilibrium Equation is an integrated form.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hanging Rod">
  <data key="d0">Hanging Rod</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A statically determinate structural element (Einzelstab) analyzed under self-weight, fixed at x=0 (top) with u(x=0)=0 and free at x=l (bottom) where strain vanishes u'(x=l)=0.&lt;SEP&gt;A rod (Einzelstab) subjected to its own weight under statically determinate conditions, serving as the physical model for stress distribution analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Strain">
  <data key="d0">Axial Strain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The deformation of a bar in the longitudinal direction, defined as the first derivative of the displacement field with respect to position.&lt;SEP&gt;Denoted as u' = du/dx (Verzerrung), representing the axial deformation of the rod per unit length, vanishing at x=l.&lt;SEP&gt;The axial deformation per unit length, denoted ε(x), represented by the first derivative of the displacement function u'(x) = du/dx in the constitutive law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Weight">
  <data key="d0">Total Weight</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as G, the total weight of the hanging rod, used in calculating line load and determining the integration constant C_1 = G.&lt;SEP&gt;The total weight of the rod (Gewicht), denoted G, representing the maximum axial force at the top support where the entire rod weight is carried.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rod Length">
  <data key="d0">Rod Length</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Denoted as l, the length of the hanging rod, used in calculating line load n = G/l.&lt;SEP&gt;The total length of the hanging rod, denoted l, used in normalizing the position coordinate x and defining the free-end boundary at x = l.&lt;SEP&gt;The length of the hanging rod, denoted as $l$, representing the coordinate at the free end.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Integration Constant C_1">
  <data key="d0">Integration Constant C_1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A constant of integration in the Bar Equilibrium Equation, determined by boundary conditions to equal G for the hanging rod configuration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second-order Equilibrium ODE">
  <data key="d0">Second-order Equilibrium ODE</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The differential equation EA·u'' = -n = -G/l representing the bar's equilibrium, from which the Bar Equilibrium Equation is derived by integrating once.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastostatic Analysis Framework">
  <data key="d0">Elastostatic Analysis Framework</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A framework for analyzing axially loaded members (Stäbe), serving as a foundation for solving statically indeterminate problems and connecting to compatibility and equilibrium conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Self-weight">
  <data key="d0">Self-weight</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The weight of the hanging rod itself, acting as a body force that generates the cumulative line load and causes varying normal force along the length of the rod.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Beidseitig Eingespannter Stab">
  <data key="d0">Beidseitig Eingespannter Stab</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A doubly fixed or clamped rod serving as an example of a statically indeterminate problem discussed subsequently in the context of elastostatic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A both-sides clamped rod with varying cross-sections, used as a statically indeterminate example in single rod/bar analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A clamped/clamped rod configuration representing a statically indeterminate single rod (Einzelstab) problem subjected to thermal loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd50f7cb97e77515d873719c81dafca9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a907de5d496ddcf9ab58b307c8f8ed</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axially Loaded Members">
  <data key="d0">Axially Loaded Members</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural components, referred to as Stäbe, that are subjected to axial loads and are analyzed within the elastostatic analysis framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Structural Elements">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Structural Elements</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural components, such as cables, columns, and hangers, designed to bear tensile forces where self-weight is a significant factor in design.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17078647094adcc12a6785492579bc48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile And Compression Tests">
  <data key="d0">Tensile And Compression Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental procedures used for the determination of material properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7191762e3c837260ad5bf3f1bc50f25a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Models">
  <data key="d0">Material Models</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Models (also referred to as constitutive laws) describing the physical relationship between stress and strain, which are dependent on the material.&lt;SEP&gt;Theoretical representations of material behavior, studied alongside elastostatics using data from tensile or compression tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Abstract representations of material behavior (Stoffmodelle) whose determination is explicitly stated to be possible only through experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Definitions and models related to material behavior discussed in the academic material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7191762e3c837260ad5bf3f1bc50f25a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-783555881f4ab73d4d04ea548b9fae1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b35bef5073db0c5a31b6dd2e83c0ddb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="2017">
  <data key="d0">2017</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The publication year of the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2'.&lt;SEP&gt;The publication year of the textbook Technische Mechanik 2.&lt;SEP&gt;The year of publication for the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2'.&lt;SEP&gt;The publication year of the textbook Technische Mechanik 2.&lt;SEP&gt;The publication year of the textbook "Technische Mechanik 2".</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7191762e3c837260ad5bf3f1bc50f25a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b35bef5073db0c5a31b6dd2e83c0ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f04350734b994ec6a731daabee6c318c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be68185d5f9be3b57dbcc2d1f774fbb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ff4ce898bbbc110abe830de75521c6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f868d6a430faab3f01aae01801aa90be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165c1196600ad5f85ab89d1be16e9bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dead11f22d1654614fa266e394b63218</data>
