  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Static Boundary Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Static Boundary Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Conditions used to determine the constants of integration in the analytical solution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fixed Top Support">
  <data key="d0">Fixed Top Support</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A boundary condition where displacement is zero, yielding the constant C_2 = 0.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Free Bottom End">
  <data key="d0">Free Bottom End</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A boundary condition with zero strain or stress, yielding the constant C_1 = G.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Deformations">
  <data key="d0">Axial Deformations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The change in shape or size of a structural component under axial load, commonly denoted as $\Delta l$.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Stress Distributions">
  <data key="d0">Internal Stress Distributions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The internal stress pattern within a structural member, calculated as $\sigma = E \cdot u'$.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Serviceability Limit States">
  <data key="d0">Serviceability Limit States</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Design conditions ensuring a structural component remains functional and comfortable under normal use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strength Limit States">
  <data key="d0">Strength Limit States</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Design conditions ensuring a structure can withstand maximum applied loads without collapse or failure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Body Force">
  <data key="d0">Body Force</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A force acting throughout the volume of a body, such as self-weight, accounted for by the particular integral.&lt;SEP&gt;Force components (f_x, f_y) acting at the geometric center of the infinitesimal element, representing distributed forces throughout the body volume.&lt;SEP&gt;Force such as weight acting on the element volume dx·dy·t, represented by volume force density in the equilibrium equation&lt;SEP&gt;A force acting throughout the volume of a body, corresponding to the Volumenkraft and likely represented by the 0.5m term in the equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinematic Solution">
  <data key="d0">Kinematic Solution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The specific solution obtained by substituting boundary condition constants into the general analytical equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-920d899bb09d20ddc75b79ec193db2e5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Stress (σ)">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Stress (σ)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mechanical normal stress defined as the ratio of axial force F to cross-sectional area A, representing material loading (Beanspruchung des Materials) measured in megapascals [MPa]</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Force (F)">
  <data key="d0">Axial Force (F)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Axial force (tensile or compressive force) measured in Newtons [N], derived experimentally via tensile or compressive testing (Zug- bzw. Druckversuch)</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Sectional Area (A)">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Sectional Area (A)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The cross-sectional area of the structural member, identified by a black, italicized letter 'A' with a thin black leader line pointing toward the rightmost face of the bar.&lt;SEP&gt;Cross-sectional area (Querschnittfläche) measured in mm² or m², which may be location-dependent (ortsabhängig veränderliche Querschnittfläche) when strain is not constant</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eae0b855416df86edb6af7ac8d0407fb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Proportional Limit Stress (σ_p)">
  <data key="d0">Proportional Limit Stress (σ_p)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress threshold defining the upper bound of the linear-elastic regime where Hooke's Law applies</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yield Stress (σ_F)">
  <data key="d0">Yield Stress (σ_F)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Material parameter separating elastic behavior (σ &lt; σ_F) from plastic behavior (σ &gt; σ_F)&lt;SEP&gt;A characteristic material limit (Fließgrenze) separating elastic and plastic regimes of material behavior, discussed in section 10.3</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constitutive Model (Stoffgesetz)">
  <data key="d0">Constitutive Model (Stoffgesetz)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Material model formed by the combination of stress σ and strain ε as its two foundational pillars</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile/Compressive Testing (Zug- bzw. Druckversuch)">
  <data key="d0">Tensile/Compressive Testing (Zug- bzw. Druckversuch)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental testing method used to derive the axial force F through tensile or compressive loading of specimens</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linear-Elastic Regime">
  <data key="d0">Linear-Elastic Regime</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Material response regime defined by σ ≤ σ_p, within which Hooke's Law applies and material behavior is linear and elastic&lt;SEP&gt;The range (σ ≤ σ_p) within which Hooke's Law applies and strain maps linearly to stress.&lt;SEP&gt;The range of material behavior in which the linear proportionality between stress and strain remains valid, specifically bounded by the proportional limit stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677c3b37a16b17c8907603bf39babaae</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mechanical Design">
  <data key="d0">Mechanical Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Application domain using stress analysis to ensure component stresses remain within admissible limits&lt;SEP&gt;A field of engineering where the formulation for computing displacements and deformation responses is applied.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Characterization">
  <data key="d0">Material Characterization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Application domain where stress analysis is used to classify material response as elastic or plastic</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Displacement Equation">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Displacement Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation representing the vertical displacement v of node K in a statically indeterminate three-bar system, derived through simultaneous consideration of equilibrium, kinematic compatibility, and Hooke's law, with the denominator 1 + 2(EA1/EA2)cos³α encapsulating stiffness distribution among all three bars.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Force S2">
  <data key="d0">Axial Force S2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The internal axial force in bar 2 of the three-bar system, determined by solving the coupled system of equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Stiffness EA1">
  <data key="d0">Axial Stiffness EA1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The axial stiffness (Dehnsteifigkeit) of the inclined bars 1 and 3 in the three-bar system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Stiffness EA2">
  <data key="d0">Axial Stiffness EA2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The axial stiffness (Dehnsteifigkeit) of the vertical bar 2 in the three-bar system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elongation Of Bar 2">
  <data key="d0">Elongation Of Bar 2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The change in length of bar 2 (denoted as $\Delta l_2$), which equals the vertical displacement v of node K and is calculated using Hooke's law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Load Sharing">
  <data key="d0">Load Sharing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The physical phenomenon where the stiffness of adjacent members directly influences the force and displacement in any given member, demonstrated by the equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second-Order Ordinary Differential Equation EA·u''(x) = -n">
  <data key="d0">Second-Order Ordinary Differential Equation EA·u''(x) = -n</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The governing differential equation for which the axial displacement field is the solution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distributed Axial Load Per Unit Length n">
  <data key="d0">Distributed Axial Load Per Unit Length n</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The constant load per unit length arising from the bar's self-weight.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Weight G">
  <data key="d0">Total Weight G</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The overall weight of the hanging bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Length l">
  <data key="d0">Total Length l</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The complete length of the hanging bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Sectional Area A">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Sectional Area A</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The constant area of the bar's cross-section.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Position Coordinate x">
  <data key="d0">Position Coordinate x</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The spatial coordinate measured from the fixed support, ranging from 0 to the total length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dimensionless Shape Function">
  <data key="d0">Dimensionless Shape Function</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical function, (2x/l - x²/l²), that captures the quadratic variation of displacement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Characteristic Displacement Scale">
  <data key="d0">Characteristic Displacement Scale</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The prefactor Gl/(2EA) representing the scale of displacement in the equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain ε(x)">
  <data key="d0">Strain ε(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strain defined as the derivative of displacement with respect to position, ε(x) = du/dx.&lt;SEP&gt;The axial strain derived by differentiating the displacement field, equal to (G/EA)(1 - x/l).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e2db8e0cc453f4ff50201dc37d1fa0c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress σ(x)">
  <data key="d0">Stress σ(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The axial stress derived from the displacement, equal to (G/A)(1 - x/l), with maximum value at the fixed support.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Theory of Elastostatics">
  <data key="d0">Theory of Elastostatics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A branch of mechanics dealing with the behavior of elastic solid bodies in static equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thermally Loaded Both-Ends-Fixed Bar">
  <data key="d0">Thermally Loaded Both-Ends-Fixed Bar</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific type of statically indeterminate structural member subject to thermal loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Displacement u(l)">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Displacement u(l)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The highest axial displacement of the bar, occurring at the free end with a value of Gl/(2EA).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Stress σ_max">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Stress σ_max</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The highest axial stress of the bar, occurring at the fixed support with a value of G/A.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784896993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Static Equilibrium">
  <data key="d0">Static Equilibrium</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Static Equilibrium is a fundamental mechanical principle and physical state of balance central to solid mechanics and stress analysis. Fundamentally, it describes a condition in which the sum of all forces and force components—such as in the x and y directions—equals zero (ΣF = 0, ΣFx = 0). By ensuring that there is no net resultant force acting on a system, Static Equilibrium guarantees the internal consistency of the stress field within a loaded body. This state is foundational not only for macroscopic structures but also for infinitesimal and differential elements, such as those analyzed within a pressure vessel or a hanging bar.

In mathematical and engineering applications, Static Equilibrium serves as the basis for deriving various governing equations. For example, applying this condition to a differential or finite bar element—where the spatial derivative of the internal normal force plus the distributed line load equals zero—allows for the derivation of the axial force equilibrium equation. Similarly, summing the forces in the x and y directions and setting them to zero leads to the formulation of governing partial differential equations. Furthermore, assuming a state of Static Equilibrium, where stresses are considered constant across the thickness, is a crucial prerequisite for deriving stress transformation equations, including those used to solve for transformed and maximum shear stresses.

Conceptually and visually, Static Equilibrium within a material element is represented by a balanced arrangement of internal forces. This mechanical condition is satisfied by drawing corresponding opposing stress arrows on opposite faces of a material element, or by arranging maximum shear stress arrows in opposing pairs on adjacent faces. Ultimately, whether deriving specific stress formulas for a pressure vessel or verifying the physical state of a hanging bar, satisfying Static Equilibrium ensures that the calculated stress distributions are mechanically stable and physically accurate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-413d03b5c0b6ff01a4da279c609d09f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e57c82a12143d5b7fd3e7f96dc75f203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae7aec8cf21235bd79c851b9cc6978f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c2cf3a337f73c88af5880c2708e9c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b3409eb9a461816c78110bf52f37f07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012dfb2f1f77750672c0c98cb5c5f782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
