<node id="Continuum Mechanics">
  <data key="d0">Continuum Mechanics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Continuum Mechanics is a foundational branch of mechanics, physics, and mechanical engineering that analyzes the kinematics and mechanical behavior of materials modeled as a continuous mass rather than as discrete particles. As an extensive theoretical framework, it provides the bedrock for studying deformation and stress in continuous matter, comprehensively covering the essential relationships between kinematic and dynamic quantities. The discipline demands rigorous unit consistency and dimensional homogeneity in its computational practices, establishing canonical formulations for material analysis.

Within this framework, Continuum Mechanics formally establishes several core mechanical and mathematical concepts. It defines mechanical normal stress and treats technical normal strain as a fundamental kinematic quantity. Consequently, it serves as the overarching domain for the fundamental strain-displacement relationship and the definition of the engineering strain equation, particularly in its one-dimensional form. To help represent and analyze these behaviors, the infinitesimal rod element acts as a fundamental kinematic notation within the discipline.

A central focus of Continuum Mechanics is the rigorous analysis of stress. The field encompasses the conceptual transition from stress vectors to the stress tensor. The Cauchy stress tensor is a primary analytical tool used to characterize the state of stress at a point within a deformable body, a definition that relies on six independent components. The mathematical framework utilizes the stress tensor's symmetry property to describe three-dimensional stress states through mutually perpendicular sectional planes. Uniaxial tension serves as a fundamental reference state and building block for more complex stress superpositions, providing the context for applied engineering discussions such as pressure vessel analysis. Additionally, finite element computations are fundamental within this field for determining stress states at critical points.

Furthermore, Continuum Mechanics extensively explores the transformation of stress states under coordinate rotation. The derivation of stress transformation equations effectively solves the eigenvalue problem for the Cauchy stress tensor, demonstrating how stress components change with the observer's coordinate system to calculate the state of stress on arbitrary planes. Principal stress analysis is a fundamental application used for determining critical stress states and loading planes. In this context, the first stress invariant is utilized alongside failure criteria, and it is established that shear stresses vanish at principal directions while normal stresses attain extremal maximum and minimum values. Mohr's stress circle is extensively applied for the graphical characterization of these stress states, including plane stress conditions.

Finally, the discipline relies on fundamental analytical tools such as free-body diagrams, as well as differential and moment equilibrium equations, to analyze the equilibrium of deformable bodies. As an academic subject, the principles of Continuum Mechanics, including the analysis of normal and shear stresses, are routinely illustrated through technical schematic diagrams and adapted from dedicated academic literature, such as the textbook "Technische Mechanik 2." Any anomalous equations that fail to adhere to these foundational principles are strictly identified as unrepresentative of the field.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17dfe04089064853421a9f1af5f1cb77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ae0372192096a8671eab765c6a11a90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10de6334f28b94ed6f4ea9af906f66c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7191762e3c837260ad5bf3f1bc50f25a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89b8532cdf159a4bfe5be48dea8b78e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85da86ad0233986cf40614b65dc61ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e2db8e0cc453f4ff50201dc37d1fa0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cdbbf7d68148fe88c3174f0ea8e4742&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdf4cd832b592c8035347d2e6e3804e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7fa545fe4bb1d6483b658ddec935e5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-271a171e393b79c95418cb0645e75d72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6db9821cc411ad75c459848cb9d08e4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ea2012f708d083bcf1f8357bfab292&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-765ad24fd0f91713e3dc722570316ee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ef0d1a935f39ab673c43461c36533f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0cc3665d01054e69899dc58796aab4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82d26e50b1a6b9a189224f9805e4217e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49d921c1af07b074