</node>
<node id="Page 21">
  <data key="d0">Page 21</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A page in the German engineering mechanics textbook containing the chapter summary Section 10.7, positioned after the three-bar system solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A page number element located at the bottom of page index 20 at coordinates [27, 907, 42, 927], serving as a navigation and reference marker.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3891c97c9b24173fa3943bf0d59b8fa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97b33a57b26cea4acd1fcb9fd8389687</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Rigidities EA₁, EA₂, EA₃">
  <data key="d0">Axial Rigidities EA₁, EA₂, EA₃</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The axial rigidities of the three bars in the three-bar system, where EA₂ and EA₃ are equal to EA₁.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3891c97c9b24173fa3943bf0d59b8fa9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Probe">
  <data key="d0">Probe</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A specimen or test piece with a reduced cross-section in its middle portion, used in tensile or compression tests to localize deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Längungsmesser">
  <data key="d0">Längungsmesser</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An extensometer represented as a rectangular sensor, used to measure the change in length (elongation or contraction) of material under load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lastzelle">
  <data key="d0">Lastzelle</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A load cell positioned at the top of the testing assembly to measure the applied force during tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bewegliches Querhaupt">
  <data key="d0">Bewegliches Querhaupt</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A movable crosshead forming the bottom plate of the testing apparatus, driven vertically by rotating threaded rods to apply tension or compression to the specimen.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastizitätsmodul">
  <data key="d0">Elastizitätsmodul</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Young's modulus (E), a material property representing stiffness, determined from the stress-strain diagram in the study of elastostatics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Proportionalitätsgrenze">
  <data key="d0">Proportionalitätsgrenze</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The proportional limit, a material property indicating the maximum stress at which strain remains directly proportional to stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fließgrenze">
  <data key="d0">Fließgrenze</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The yield point (also called Streckgrenze), a material property indicating the stress at which a material begins to deform plastically.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Universal Testing Machine">
  <data key="d0">Universal Testing Machine</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A machine configuration used to perform experimental setups such as tensile or compression tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Streckgrenze">
  <data key="d0">Streckgrenze</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Another term for the Fließgrenze (yield point), indicating the stress at which a material begins to deform plastically.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7bb8c329536d2e721390485300fec34e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elongation Equation Δl = u(l) − u(0)">
  <data key="d0">Elongation Equation Δl = u(l) − u(0)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical equation representing the total elongation (change in length) of a one-dimensional structural member, defined as the difference between axial displacements at the free end (x = l) and the fixed end (x = 0) of a rod.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Field ε(x)">
  <data key="d0">Strain Field ε(x)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The spatial derivative of the axial displacement field (ε(x) = du/dx), representing local deformation within the structural member.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Governing Differential Equation E·A·u''(x) + n(x) − E·A·α·ΔT' = 0">
  <data key="d0">Governing Differential Equation E·A·u''(x) + n(x) − E·A·α·ΔT' = 0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The second-order differential equation that governs the axial displacement field in a rod under combined mechanical and thermal loading, incorporating Young's modulus, cross-sectional area, line load, and temperature change.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Line Load n">
  <data key="d0">Line Load n</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The constant distributed load denoted by n = G/l, representing the self-weight per unit length of the rod (Streckenlast).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Determinate Hanging Rod">
  <data key="d0">Statically Determinate Hanging Rod</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural scenario where a single rod is fixed at one end and free at the other, subjected to self-weight (Eigengewicht), yielding u(0) = 0 and Δl = u(l).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Indeterminate Doubly-clamped Rod">
  <data key="d0">Statically Indeterminate Doubly-clamped Rod</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural scenario where a rod is restrained at both ends (beidseitig eingespannter Stab), subjected to thermal loading, enforcing the compatibility condition Δl = 0 (i.e., u(l) = u(0)).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fundamental Theorem Of Calculus">
  <data key="d0">Fundamental Theorem Of Calculus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical theorem used to derive Δl = u(l) − u(0) from the integration of the strain field over the rod length: Δl = ∫₀ˡ u'(x) dx.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principle Of Virtual Work">
  <data key="d0">Principle Of Virtual Work</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An analytical principle in structural mechanics for which the elongation equation serves as a foundational link between local strain/displacement and global structural response.&lt;SEP&gt;A principle embodied by the equilibrium equation to ensure mechanical stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7fa545fe4bb1d6483b658ddec935e5f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Castigliano's Theorem">
  <data key="d0">Castigliano's Theorem</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A method in structural mechanics for computing displacements and forces, for which the elongation equation provides foundational support.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stiffness-based Finite Element Formulations">
  <data key="d0">Stiffness-based Finite Element Formulations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Computational methods for bar and truss analysis that rely on the elongation equation as a foundational relationship connecting boundary conditions to physical observables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="One-dimensional Structural Member (Einzelstab)">
  <data key="d0">One-dimensional Structural Member (Einzelstab)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural element, such as a single rod, analyzed in one dimension for its longitudinal displacement and elongation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Support Reactions (Lagerreaktionen)">
  <data key="d0">Support Reactions (Lagerreaktionen)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical observables representing the forces and moments exerted by supports on a structural member to maintain equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bar And Truss Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Bar And Truss Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The engineering practice of calculating forces, stresses, and displacements in structures composed of bars and trusses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Self-weight (Eigengewicht)">
  <data key="d0">Self-weight (Eigengewicht)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The force exerted by gravity on the mass of the structural member itself, acting as a distributed load along its length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ductile Material">
  <data key="d0">Ductile Material</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of material exhibiting non-linear mechanical behavior, including elastic deformation, strain hardening, and necking, before eventual failure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Test">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An experimental procedure used to derive material properties and relationships, producing stress-strain diagrams.&lt;SEP&gt;An experiment used to determine material models by applying tensile forces to a specimen to observe its mechanical behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;An experimental method (Zugversuch) explicitly identified as the primary experimental approach for determining material models and material properties in mechanics and materials science.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-055d89277cf2e87034384489ad8dc567&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-783555881f4ab73d4d04ea548b9fae1b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Engineering Stress Curve">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Stress Curve</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A graphical representation, shown as a red solid line, depicting stress calculated using the original cross-sectional area ($A$) throughout the tensile test.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="True Stress Curve">
  <data key="d0">True Stress Curve</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A graphical representation, shown as a black dashed line, depicting stress calculated using the instantaneous reduced cross-sectional area ($A_w$) during necking.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yield Point">
  <data key="d0">Yield Point</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The stress point, marked as $\sigma_F$, indicating the transition from elastic to plastic deformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strain Hardening">
  <data key="d0">Strain Hardening</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A phase, labeled 'Verfestigung', where the material strengthens and the stress-strain curve rises again after the yield point is surpassed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Necking">
  <data key="d0">Necking</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A localized reduction in cross-sectional area, labeled 'Einschnürung', occurring after the peak stress, causing the engineering stress curve to slope downward.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fracture">
  <data key="d0">Fracture</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The failure or breaking point of the material, marked with an 'X' and labeled 'Bruch', terminating the tensile test.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unloading">
  <data key="d0">Unloading</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The removal of the tensile load, labeled 'Entlastung', represented by a line parallel to the initial elastic modulus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Specimen">
  <data key="d0">Test Specimen</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A cylindrical bar, depicted in the diagram inset as 'Probenstab', featuring a defined gauge length and cross-sectional area, subjected to tensile forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Force">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Force</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The equal and opposite pulling forces, labeled '$F$', applied to the ends of the test specimen during the tensile test.&lt;SEP&gt;The total tensile force acting in the wall cross-section, expressed as σ_t · 2π · r · t on the left-hand side of the equilibrium equation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-493acae899ca4eaaedf536afa97f481b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Law">
  <data key="d0">Material Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A theoretical model, referred to as 'Stoffgesetz/-modell', defining the mechanical behavior and properties of a material.&lt;SEP&gt;The constitutive equations governing material behavior, forming one part of the three-equation system for structural analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Concepts presented in the technical mechanics lecture and verified using the referenced textbook.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a093ad0a488172322e66eed43c540981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1a7b2afc01b89dc35586063ca901c7f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Engineering Stress Formula">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Stress Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical formula $\sigma = \frac{F}{A}$ used to calculate engineering stress based on the original cross-sectional area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="True Stress Formula">
  <data key="d0">True Stress Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical formula $\sigma_w = \frac{F}{A_w}$ used to calculate true stress based on the instantaneous reduced cross-sectional area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gauge Length">
  <data key="d0">Gauge Length</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The defined original length, marked as '$l$', of the test specimen used as a reference for measuring strain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="True Stress">
  <data key="d0">True Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as 'Wahre Spannung' ($\sigma_w$), it represents the actual stress based on the instantaneous reduced cross-sectional area ($A_w$) during deformation.&lt;SEP&gt;The stress accounting for the reduction in cross-sectional area as the material stretches and necks (σ_w = F/A_w), represented by a dashed black line.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73a8a17ee36ec3a25041cb22c3b85cd5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Original Cross-sectional Area">
  <data key="d0">Original Cross-sectional Area</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The initial cross-sectional area of the test specimen, denoted as $A$, used to calculate the engineering stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Instantaneous Reduced Cross-sectional Area">
  <data key="d0">Instantaneous Reduced Cross-sectional Area</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The actual, reduced cross-sectional area of the test specimen during necking, denoted as $A_w$, used to calculate the true stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-763b254f857bc066044052010175b2b1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Displacement Equation">
  <data key="d0">Axial Displacement Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation providing the analytical solution for the axial displacement field of a uniformly hanging rod under its own self-weight, specifically evaluating displacement at the free end as $u(l) = \frac{1}{2}\frac{Gl}{EA}$.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uniformly Hanging Rod">
  <data key="d0">Uniformly Hanging Rod</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A theoretical structural element analyzed for axial deformation and endpoint displacement caused by its own self-weight.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rod Weight">
  <data key="d0">Rod Weight</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The total weight of the rod, denoted as $G$, calculated as the product of density, gravitational acceleration, and volume ($W = \rho g V = \rho g A l$).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second-Order Ordinary Differential Equation">
  <data key="d0">Second-Order Ordinary Differential Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The governing mathematical equation of axial equilibrium, expressed as $EA \cdot u''(x) = -n$, which yields a quadratic displacement profile upon double integration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dirichlet-Type Boundary Condition">
  <data key="d0">Dirichlet-Type Boundary Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A boundary condition specifying a fixed value, such as $u(0) = 0$ at the fixed support of the hanging rod.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distributed Line Load">
  <data key="d0">Distributed Line Load</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Represented by n, the distributed line force (Linienlast/Streckenlast) with units of force per length [N/m], acting on the bar element.&lt;SEP&gt;A load, denoted as n (Linienkraft or Streckenlast), acting per unit length along the bar's longitudinal axis.&lt;SEP&gt;A load distributed along the length of a bar element (Linienkraft) measured in force per unit length [N/m], whose resultant equals n·l for uniform loading&lt;SEP&gt;A constant load distributed along the length of the rod, representing self-weight, denoted as $n = G/l$.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f862d3f8b712d95184fc656859d1e6cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-413d03b5c0b6ff01a4da279c609d09f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faaf238bf3e3a6116edca46159e939df</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Theory Of Axially Loaded Bars">
  <data key="d0">Theory Of Axially Loaded Bars</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific theoretical framework within structural mechanics, also known as Stabtheorie, focusing on axially loaded structural members.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Determinate Single Rod">
  <data key="d0">Statically Determinate Single Rod</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural element (statisch bestimmter Einzelstab) with exactly enough supports to maintain equilibrium without redundancy, serving as the canonical problem for the equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Integration Constant">
  <data key="d0">Integration Constant</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical constants, determined as $C_1 = G$ and $C_2 = 0$, found by applying fixed-end and stress-free boundary conditions to the integrated governing equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quadratic Displacement Profile">
  <data key="d0">Quadratic Displacement Profile</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The shape of the deformation field along the rod, resulting from the double integration of the governing ordinary differential equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concentrated Point Load">
  <data key="d0">Concentrated Point Load</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A theoretical force applied at a single point on the structure, used as a comparison to illustrate the physical significance of the 1/2 factor in self-weight elongation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Self-Weight Elongation">
  <data key="d0">Self-Weight Elongation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The axial extension of the rod caused by its own mass, which equals exactly half the elongation that would result if the total weight were applied as a concentrated point load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clamped-Clamped Rod">
  <data key="d0">Clamped-Clamped Rod</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific structural element fixed at both ends, serving as an example of a statically indeterminate problem under thermal loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section 10.4">
  <data key="d0">Section 10.4</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A section of the document covering practical bar element analysis involving equilibrium conditions, kinematics, and elasticity models.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific section in the text that uses the axial displacement equation as a reference solution before progressing to more complex cases.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab59a548a45f0f75802088fe89a02c30</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Axial Equilibrium">
  <data key="d0">Axial Equilibrium</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The balance of forces along the longitudinal axis of the rod, described by a differential equation incorporating the distributed line load.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linear-Elastic Material Behavior">
  <data key="d0">Linear-Elastic Material Behavior</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material behavior regime in which stress is proportional to strain, modeled by Hooke's Law and characterized by Young's Modulus.&lt;SEP&gt;The physical condition, valid when stress is below the proportional limit, under which the equation applies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-055d89277cf2e87034384489ad8dc567&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f862d3f8b712d95184fc656859d1e6cd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compressive Test">
  <data key="d0">Compressive Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An experimental procedure used to derive material properties and relationships, producing stress-strain diagrams.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-055d89277cf2e87034384489ad8dc567</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinetic Quantities">
  <data key="d0">Kinetic Quantities</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Forces and stresses that are connected to kinematic quantities through the theoretical bridge of constitutive laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-055d89277cf2e87034384489ad8dc567</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Regimes">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Regimes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A category of material behavior identified by yield stress limits in the discussion of constitutive laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-055d89277cf2e87034384489ad8dc567</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic Regimes">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Regimes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A category of material behavior identified by yield stress limits in the discussion of constitutive laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-055d89277cf2e87034384489ad8dc567</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structural Deformations">
  <data key="d0">Structural Deformations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Deformations of a structure under load that require the mathematical and physical rules established by constitutive laws to be calculated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-055d89277cf2e87034384489ad8dc567</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Δl = α_T · ΔT · l">
  <data key="d0">Δl = α_T · ΔT · l</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation describing the thermal expansion of a rod or bar element, representing the change in length due to a uniform temperature change when no mechanical normal force is present (N = 0)</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Δl = ∫₀ˡ (N/(EA) + α_T ΔT) dx">
  <data key="d0">Δl = ∫₀ˡ (N/(EA) + α_T ΔT) dx</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">General elongation formula presented in the Zusammenfassung (summary) section, from which the specialized thermal expansion equation is derived</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ε_T = α_T · ΔT">
  <data key="d0">ε_T = α_T · ΔT</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Thermal strain component whose integration over the length of the bar yields the thermal expansion equation Δl = α_T · ΔT · l</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thermoelasticity">
  <data key="d0">Thermoelasticity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Field of study concerned with interactions between thermal and mechanical strain, for which the principle of superposition is fundamental&lt;SEP&gt;Framework combining mechanical and thermal effects for axially loaded members, encompassing constitutive relationships</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ab72d2dd30449c995d80393a0354227</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zusammenfassung">
  <data key="d0">Zusammenfassung</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Summary section where the general elongation formula combining mechanical and thermal effects is presented&lt;SEP&gt;The summary section (Chapter 11.4) of a continuum mechanics or strength of materials lecture/document, which comprehensively wraps up the preceding discussion on stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6db9821cc411ad75c459848cb9d08e4c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="α_T">
  <data key="d0">α_T</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The coefficient of thermal expansion (Wärmeausdehnungskoeffizient) of the bar material&lt;SEP&gt;Coefficient of thermal expansion, known as Wärmeausdehnungskoeffizient</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-542bf3a4290d267cd5257b52563c594f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ΔT">
  <data key="d0">ΔT</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The uniform temperature change applied to the bar&lt;SEP&gt;Temperature difference driving the thermal expansion</data>
