  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Elasticity Theory">
  <data key="d0">Linear Elasticity Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A theoretical framework in mechanics for which the generalized Hooke's Law serves as the foundational constitutive equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a093ad0a488172322e66eed43c540981</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cauchy Stress Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Cauchy Stress Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Cauchy Stress Tensor, also referred to as the Spannungstensor, is a fundamental second-rank tensor utilized in continuum mechanics and solid mechanics to comprehensively characterize the state of stress at a specific point within a deformable body or material under load. Because stress at a point cannot be fully described by a single vector, this mathematical construct maps the unit normal vector of any cutting plane to the corresponding traction vector, representing internal force intensities typically measured in units of MPa or N/mm². It is generally represented as a 3×3 symmetric matrix in three-dimensional space or as a 2×2 symmetric tensor for two-dimensional plane stress states.

A defining characteristic of the Cauchy Stress Tensor, commonly denoted as σ with components σ_ij, is its inherent symmetry. Established through moment equilibrium conditions about the coordinate axes through a given point, the tensor satisfies the matrix condition σ = σ^T. This means that the off-diagonal shear components are equal across the main diagonal (e.g., σ_xy = σ_yx, σ_xz = σ_zx, and σ_yz = σ_zy). This symmetry property effectively reduces the number of independent stress components from nine to six.

The mathematical properties of the Cauchy Stress Tensor dictate how stress components transform between different orthogonal Cartesian coordinate systems. Its transformation behavior, which gives rise to double-angle formulations under coordinate rotation, is governed by specific geometric rules and stress transformation equations. Solving the eigenvalue problem for this tensor yields its eigenvalues, which correspond to the principal stresses acting on planes where the shear stress completely vanishes. Additionally, the tensor features mathematical invariants, notably its first invariant denoted as I₁.

In specific loading scenarios, the general representation of the stress tensor simplifies into distinct diagonal forms. For example, in a two-dimensional plane stress state under uniaxial tension where the only non-zero normal stress acts along the x-axis (σ_x = σ_0), the tensor reduces to a 2×2 diagonal matrix represented as diag(σ_0, 0), with zero shear components (τ_xy = τ_yx = 0). Similarly, a state characterized by equal principal normal stresses combined with zero off-diagonal shear stress components will also result in a simplified diagonal matrix representation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a093ad0a488172322e66eed43c540981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb966affea49c8515afe80d893d4ba39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1626d6e3dffee746e5efebf5c0f44ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cabab253ce4ee75c98c51cfd7f1a27b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d8ef0753f64ed72eb451c29b8108d08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95309ae773a0c5fe9703868d35942eff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f852bec3a8e229cf7fc9b647545ae29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcd5ad0575ad16303ca4601a26ea32f2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904014</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linearized Strain Tensor">
