<node id="Cartesian Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">Cartesian Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Cartesian Coordinate System is an orthogonal reference framework utilized extensively in technical and engineering diagrams to define spatial points, physical quantities, and graphical relationships. It is characterized by perpendicular axes that provide precise spatial orientation and serve as a foundational basis for mathematical transformations and data visualization.

In the context of material mechanics and stress analysis, the Cartesian Coordinate System is frequently overlaid on differential material elements to specify their orientation and location in space. In two-dimensional applications, it is commonly depicted—often visually highlighted in blue—with a horizontal x-axis (or occasionally a y-axis) pointing to the right, and a vertical y-axis (or z-axis) pointing upward. The origin of this system is typically anchored to the bottom-left corner or vertex of the material element. In three-dimensional spatial contexts, it operates as a right-handed coordinate system comprising x, y, and z axes. This spatial reference frame is essential for defining initial stress components and executing coordinate transformations, such as translating data between an original x,y system and a rotated eta,xi system.

Furthermore, the Cartesian Coordinate System provides the graphical plotting foundation for analytical diagrams, including stress-strain diagrams and Mohr's Circle. When applied to Mohr's Circle, the system utilizes perpendicular axes to map the state of stress as a locus of points. The horizontal axis (typically labeled as sigma or normal stress) represents the normal stress component, while the vertical axis (labeled as tau or shear stress) represents the shear stress component. These axes generally feature directional arrows indicating positive values, allowing for a clear, standardized visual representation of complex stress states and material behaviors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73a8a17ee36ec3a25041cb22c3b85cd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e57c82a12143d5b7fd3e7f96dc75f203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-341774a38a3b4fd47c6656391af81dd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b44241248f288278ba7f5d8fa8160e3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b2a42c115ed62e72e715daa89e2370&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95ef86ae323cfba68764c2acb2d46785&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98ff466f481e3ef48f03b583574686cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2662124ebaa998fdaa13ffecb3add380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb64a0edb671186691ad9f74cd07ae9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85630b6d3d048fd50a738bf6456c479f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb9e40b1a13627933f97ce043e59f768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c2cf3a337f73c88af5880c2708e9c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ca440e5727deb504109d3cda9b3642d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d97fb385691a70a5c314b2a77a107e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b23912fb95dffaea41a466afd896e511&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcd5ad0575ad16303ca4601a26ea32f2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903888</data>
