</node>
<node id="Constrained System">
  <data key="d0">Constrained System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural system restricted from free deformation, where thermal effects generate internal forces and stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897007</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Normal Force">
  <data key="d0">Internal Normal Force</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Internal Normal Force, frequently denoted as N or N(x) and sometimes referred to as the "Normalkraft," is a fundamental concept studied in statics and structural mechanics. Measured in Newtons, it represents the internal axial force acting within a structural member, such as a rod or a bar. Specifically, it characterizes the force acting on the faces of an infinitesimal element at a given position, x, along the length of the member.

The Internal Normal Force is determined by analyzing the equilibrium conditions of the structure. It is closely studied alongside mechanical stress and serves as a critical parameter in the calculation of mechanical strain components within the material.

Depending on the specific geometry, loading, and boundary conditions of the structural member, the Internal Normal Force can exhibit different distributions along its length. In scenarios where a member is subjected purely to an applied end force, denoted as F (such as when a distributed load parameter n = 0), N(x) remains a continuous and constant value equal to F throughout the entire bar, including conical rods. By standard sign convention, this force is designated as negative (e.g., -F) when it induces compression. Conversely, under different loading setups, the internal force N(x) may not be constant; for instance, it can vary continuously as a function of position x, such as varying linearly from a maximum value at a fixed support to zero at the free end of a rod.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cf7cfa532d0224ec6532c6354495df59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9650cbfdac35d71c763ff0baacdc1c9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-051ef03c9d8a42ed1589c14c82007f4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f862d3f8b712d95184fc656859d1e6cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-413d03b5c0b6ff01a4da279c609d09f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-794d30f8b956a266f2b6d66cc7f678de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897017</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Node K">
  <data key="d0">Node K</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Node K is a central node within a statically indeterminate three-bar structural system. It serves as the common lower junction point where Bar 1, Bar 2, and Bar 3 converge. At this critical connection point, the structural system is subjected to both internal and external forces, notably acting as the exact location where a downward vertical external load, designated as force F, is applied.

Under the influence of this applied load, Node K undergoes both vertical and horizontal displacements. Specifically, a vertical displacement, denoted as v, occurs alongside the horizontal shift. This physical movement causes the node to relocate from its original position to a new position, mathematically and physically referred to as K'. The displacement of Node K is determined using mathematical equations, and it is a fundamental parameter because it directly dictates the elongations experienced by the three connected structural bars. 

Furthermore, Node K is essential for analyzing the overall stability and force distribution of the structure. It is the specific structural node where the horizontal equilibrium condition (ΣH = 0) is applied, which is a necessary step in resolving the forces within the statically indeterminate truss system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad1fe24f4724db937ef65aa2cd2c2fab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e7848db5af896e14553322ef7578977&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d49057dad0c3acc1a3ce1afb3e687a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe1aa2ab22c57b58f370cf064cf7ce98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f72457639c5767731980d0c54a4fe069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1db4055b5d566c7d454787a7ff1515b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cdfa6405eb28772fd593fe970821beaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edab2d4bca40b7e16cfc072b04cebe0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76cd2fb9e592523746e131ec4c914481&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9966746921724686a393e234327d9e41</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897026</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boundary Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Boundary Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Boundary conditions are mathematical and physical constraints applied to the boundaries, ends, or supports of structural elements, such as a rod or a hanging bar. Their primary purpose is to solve the governing differential equations of a structural system. By providing specific constraints at the limits of the structure, boundary conditions allow for the evaluation of integration constants within the displacement field—such as yielding C₂ = 0 and C₁ = G. This evaluation is necessary to completely determine the displacement, stress, and strain distributions throughout the element.

In common structural mechanics scenarios, such as a bar fixed at the top and free at the bottom, boundary conditions define the exact physical state at the supports. A fixed support imposes a zero-displacement condition, which is mathematically expressed as u(x=0) = 0 or u(0) = 0. On the other hand, a free end dictates a stress-free environment with zero strain, expressed as u'(x=l) = 0 or u'(l) = 0. Additionally, boundary conditions can manifest as other system-wide physical limits, such as requiring a total elongation of zero. These mathematical formulations are fundamentally utilized to solve for the displacement of structural systems and are frequently referenced and cross-referenced within textbook formulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Conditions specifying that the top and bottom surfaces of a thin plate are unloaded, leading to zero forces in the z-direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97b092fc202625d8ee8acd1894cf787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f197243eeecfe8da4e162edc85af353&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cc011b4f71d9c304cbdecd2839b81e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dfca5c7dbae86836330061b0f49bc01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-460d07ec9180c3a338bcc5988e8e42a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6e4242a538dfbd7e7ae0462ee78fbc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-213cdd3f8f32396583dc5a4fa4d54dfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59e8bd68bf620257999ef2c55d39d49c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2866a3f417ff78b075338fa558392e29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal Expansion">
  <data key="d0">Thermal Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Thermal Expansion is a fundamental mechanical engineering principle and physical phenomenon describing the tendency of a material to change its shape, area, and volume in response to a temperature change. 

When a structural element, such as a rod or bar, experiences a uniform temperature change (ΔT), it undergoes a natural change in length. This free elongation occurs freely when no mechanical normal force is present and is mathematically represented in the compatibility equation by the term αT ΔT l. This principle is a core focus in thermally-induced stress analysis, particularly in textbook examples exploring the effect of temperature variations on structural elements.

However, complications arise when this natural expansion is constrained. In physical systems, such as clamped bars with varying cross-sections or elements held by fixed supports, the tendency of the bar to expand is physically prevented. When thermal expansion is restricted in such restrained structures, the material cannot freely elongate, which consequently generates internal thermal stresses. Therefore, analyzing the relationship between a material's free elongation and its constrained expansion is crucial for understanding and calculating thermally-induced stresses in mechanical and structural engineering applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-370a72fe18982a27d4543ffa0c4c20f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccf2b44f3719aa488a2e1148c9ed36db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abc3cadf3a789dbb882aa778366ab3b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f99cae1296009661e572167acf5fe19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb28b0e78717850205497e7c5e164dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64ae8f78050d6e3fb28375ca609b6390&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f04350734b994ec6a731daabee6c318c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f547ed992922c6608475aa607ecc95</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897042</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statically Indeterminate Doubly-clapped Rod">
  <data key="d0">Statically Indeterminate Doubly-clapped Rod</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0963d089a81497869e206f806e6e66d1</data>
  <data key="d2">The elongation equation is used in the analysis of statically indeterminate doubly-clamped rods, enforcing the compatibility condition Δl = 0.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784897130</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Vector">
  <data key="d0">Stress Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Stress Vector, also referred to in German as the Spannungsvektor, is a foundational concept in continuum mechanics and engineering stress analysis. It represents the internal forces acting across a surface within a material, specifically defining the state of stress—or force per unit area—at a distinct point on a cross-section or sectional plane under loading. The vector is typically constructed for a cutting surface whose normal points in a specific coordinate direction, such as the x-direction or the xi-axis. Once established, the stress vector can be mathematically decomposed into fundamental directional quantities, specifically normal stresses and shear stresses.

Despite its utility, a single Stress Vector is inherently limited in its capacity to describe the complete stress state at an arbitrary point. Because the vector is highly dependent on both its precise location and the chosen cutting direction, relying on only one vector is insufficient for a comprehensive analysis. To overcome this limitation and fully describe the three-dimensional stress state at a point, multiple individual stress vectors are systematically arranged to construct a stress tensor, or matrix. 

This progression from a single directional vector to a comprehensive tensor theory in three-dimensional bodies is a core component of advanced mechanics analysis. The Stress Vector is frequently introduced early in this systematic development within academic literature. For instance, it is formally presented as a complex directional stress quantity in Chapter 11, Section 11.1, of German technical mechanics textbooks, serving as an essential building block for understanding material stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-271a171e393b79c95418cb0645e75d72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-765ad24fd0f91713e3dc722570316ee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60e545266c1919041c313cfba39a94c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cabab253ce4ee75c98c51cfd7f1a27b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677dd5182b714188875fefaff19c7680&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0f20e6bdef6f836f08f3407dd794183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadc704f7f5e7980d43e7d870873727e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b18872f44af51f12eed1b7bb9c974c8d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903967</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Stress Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The stress tensor, also known in German as the Spannungstensor and commonly referred to as the Cauchy stress tensor (denoted as σ or σ_ij), is a fundamental mathematical concept in continuum mechanics, solid mechanics, and engineering stress analysis. It is a second-order, symmetric tensor that completely characterizes the internal force distribution and the physical state of stress at any given point within a deformable body. Because a single stress vector is insufficient to fully describe the stress at a point, the stress tensor was developed as a complete mathematical object to represent the stress state using three stress vectors on mutually perpendicular planes.

Structurally, the stress tensor is represented by a 3x3 matrix consisting of nine stress components. It utilizes a double-index notation, where the first index denotes the axis normal to the plane and the second denotes the stress vector direction. The main diagonal of the matrix contains the normal stresses (σ_xx, σ_yy, σ_zz), while the off-diagonal components represent the shear stresses (τ_ij). Due to the property of symmetry (σ_ij = σ_ji, meaning τxy equals τyx), these nine components are coupled through equilibrium requirements and reduce to only six independent entries for the general case. In specific scenarios, such as plane stress analysis, this further reduces to three independent components.

While the individual component values of the stress tensor change under coordinate rotation according to a specific rotational prescription, the underlying physical stress state remains invariant. The tensor possesses distinct mathematical properties, including specific invariants such as its trace (the sum of its normal stresses, for example, σx + σy) and its determinant (for example, σxσy - τxy²). Furthermore, the eigenvalues of the stress tensor correspond to the principal stresses, and its eigenvectors represent the principal directions. When expressed in the principal coordinate system, the stress tensor becomes a diagonal matrix. This systematic tensor formulation is widely taught in technical mechanics curricula, often introduced through a progressive build-up from the basic definition of a stress vector in foundational textbook chapters.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e57c82a12143d5b7fd3e7f96dc75f203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3186598e4d31ef04b0826dc0b3dad17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-271a171e393b79c95418cb0645e75d72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ea2012f708d083bcf1f8357bfab292&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-765ad24fd0f91713e3dc722570316ee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4ade0a9e41cfa09698117c999ef93d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60e545266c1919041c313cfba39a94c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cabab253ce4ee75c98c51cfd7f1a27b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677dd5182b714188875fefaff19c7680&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0f20e6bdef6f836f08f3407dd794183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87396546f57986339f19784a816ba8db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d97fb385691a70a5c314b2a77a107e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b3409eb9a461816c78110bf52f37f07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadc704f7f5e7980d43e7d870873727e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dbabf4bbc38075b362efc1cf68c92ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b23912fb95dffaea41a466afd896e511&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b18872f44af51f12eed1b7bb9c974c8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a03cb4a8bcb2904e74a15376b18fddec</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903894</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stress">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shear stress, also known as Schubspannung in German, is a foundational concept in engineering mechanics that describes the tangential component of a stress vector acting parallel to a surface, cutting plane, or material face. It is universally denoted by the Greek letter tau ($\tau$), often accompanied by double-index notation (e.g., $\tau_{xy}$, $\tau_{yx}$, $\tau_{\xi\eta}$) to specify its direction relative to the plane. Mathematically, this stress component is obtained by decomposing the stress vector, and it can be expressed as $\tau = |t - (t \cdot n)n|$. In practical worked examples, such as calculations on inclined planes, it is typically quantified in units of pressure, such as $\tau_{xy} = 30$ MPa.

Within the stress tensor, shear stress components occupy the off-diagonal positions. Due to moment equilibrium and the inherent symmetry property of the stress tensor, these components are not entirely independent of one another. The six possible shear stresses on the positive faces of a volume element ($\tau_{xy}$, $\tau_{yx}$, $\tau_{xz}$, $\tau_{zx}$, $\tau_{yz}$, $\tau_{zy}$) reduce to three independent pairs, where $\tau_{xy} = \tau_{yx}$, $\tau_{xz} = \tau_{zx}$, and $\tau_{yz} = \tau_{zy}$. These components can vary across differential distances $dx$ and $dy$ based on partial derivatives and are entirely absent in a hydrostatic stress state, as indicated by the lack of parallel arrows on the material element.

Shear stress plays a critical role in coordinate transformation analysis, where its magnitude depends on the cutting direction or angle ($\phi$). It is integral to the trigonometric transformation equations used to evaluate stresses in rotated coordinate systems. A defining characteristic of shear stress is its behavior on principal planes (Hauptrichtungen); shear stress components, such as $\tau_{\xi\eta}$ or $\tau_{xy}$, vanish to zero precisely at these principal directions, which is also where normal stresses reach their extremal values. In contrast, shear stress itself reaches extremal magnitudes on planes oriented 45 degrees to the principal directions.

In graphical representations and standard engineering notation, shear stress is a fundamental element of Mohr's Circle. In this Cartesian coordinate system, shear stress ($\tau$) is plotted along the vertical axis, and its components, such as $\tau_{xy}$, are used in the calculation of the circle's radius. Visually, shear stresses are commonly depicted as horizontal and vertical arrows (for instance, four thick red arrows) running parallel to the faces of an infinitesimal material element, indicating the directional, tangential forces applied to the material. When shear stresses are explicitly not shown on certain surface elements, it confirms that the orthogonal normal stresses acting on those planes are the principal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-842c0517bb20a54922d1a75c277c68f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6513413e0a80f005888472d94d385e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-341774a38a3b4fd47c6656391af81dd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-011c3fc433230965d7a734e4052f7689&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85ca1064506b38c8fe493e3ffc533c71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b2a42c115ed62e72e715daa89e2370&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-271a171e393b79c95418cb0645e75d72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95ef86ae323cfba68764c2acb2d46785&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74378ee7045b50cecf2c625c47685477&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-765ad24fd0f91713e3dc722570316ee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98ff466f481e3ef48f03b583574686cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2662124ebaa998fdaa13ffecb3add380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb64a0edb671186691ad9f74cd07ae9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a49d921c1af07b0743c98652a77081e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85630b6d3d048fd50a738bf6456c479f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb9e40b1a13627933f97ce043e59f768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6589bf554e5066ac0fa2960ba8d673c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553477d678ccfb56c56a99c6a713a1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a7d19da473048f7577c45363a2702e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d97fb385691a70a5c314b2a77a107e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b3409eb9a461816c78110bf52f37f07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b026fcfc3bb4dcf15d90b692b7902eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadc704f7f5e7980d43e7d870873727e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65f35adb2f34cbe81a9755002339ad40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f29928f565c2e69cfd84b7a3af24ffe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b18872f44af51f12eed1b7bb9c974c8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a03cb4a8bcb2904e74a15376b18fddec</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress State">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Plane Stress State, also known by its German term "ebener Spannungszustand" (ESZ), is a fundamental concept and idealization in solid and continuum mechanics describing a two-dimensional state of stress. This condition occurs when a body's thickness is negligible compared to its other dimensions, such as in thin plates, disks, or thin-walled vessels, and the loading is confined entirely to the object's own plane. In this state, the stress distribution is constant across the thickness, and the forces or stress components acting in one of the three mutually perpendicular planes—typically the z-direction—are zero. 

Characterized by normal and shear stresses acting within a single plane, the Plane Stress State is primarily defined by the stress components σx, σy, and τxy (where τxy = τyx). These components form a 2x2 symmetric stress matrix that can be mathematically analyzed within a two-dimensional (x, y) coordinate system. For instance, in a specific engineering worked example, this condition is represented by a tensile normal stress σx of 50 MPa, a compressive normal stress σy of -20 MPa, and a shear stress τxy of 30 MPa. Another notable manifestation is the uniaxial tension case, a specific Plane Stress State where the shear stress components are entirely zero.

In engineering mechanics, the Plane Stress State serves as the foundational basis for performing two-dimensional stress transformations and evaluating failure criteria. The transformation of these original stress components is highly relevant to the rotation angle φ and the transformed normal stress σ_ξ. This analytical process frequently utilizes the stress invariant equation and the normal stress transformation law. Engineers commonly employ Mohr's Circle to visually and mathematically analyze these 2D stress conditions, allowing them to accurately determine the principal stresses and maximum shear stresses experienced by infinitesimal elements.

A prominent practical application of the Plane Stress State (ESZ) is found in the structural analysis of thin-walled vessels. Under the ESZ assumption, radial stresses in the vessel wall are considered negligible. Consequently, the analysis is simplified, rendering the longitudinal and circumferential stresses (often denoted as σ_x and σ_φ) as the principal stresses. In this specific state, the vessel wall experiences these principal stresses without the presence of accompanying shear stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ba563b25b03c792bf6e4d826fd0f062&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebe13321a6d5df3c82e760adc093a99c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f2990ab5263ad174ce6fec37fef1115&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553477d678ccfb56c56a99c6a713a1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c2cf3a337f73c88af5880c2708e9c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a1ba292932d5ab22c0f62081869648c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49e2cb25fd70f2b5f3b6c22629fe461a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87396546f57986339f19784a816ba8db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1685984065ed0dcd54dea046d7d4f918&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dbabf4bbc38075b362efc1cf68c92ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f29928f565c2e69cfd84b7a3af24ffe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e3cdc6151a0814d04d2cc632b0969f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9c3492ddaae5a544709ab9626c48a75</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
