<node id="Kesselformeln">
  <data key="d0">Kesselformeln</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Kesselformeln, commonly referred to as boiler formulas or vessel formulas, are fundamental equations used in the engineering and technical context of pressure vessel design. Derived from elementary equilibrium principles, these formulas are primarily utilized for the stress analysis of thin-walled cylinders and pressure vessels. They are used to derive the stress states within these structures, often corresponding directly to the schematic diagrams used in technical analyses.

The core function of the Kesselformeln is to calculate the longitudinal, circumferential, and tangential stresses acting on the walls of a thin-walled vessel. A crucial mechanical principle established by these boiler formulas is that the hoop stress (sigma_phi) is exactly twice the longitudinal stress (sigma_x). Because they outline the fundamental stress parameters for thin-walled cylinders, these equations frequently serve as the primary heading preceding more detailed derivations in engineering literature.

Furthermore, the Kesselformeln are highly versatile in their application to different loading conditions. While they are standardly applied to vessels under internal pressure, they are equally applicable to externally pressurized vessels. In scenarios involving external pressure, the sign of the pressure variable is simply changed. This adjustment correctly accounts for the reversal in force, allowing the formulas to accurately calculate and obtain the resulting compressive stresses within the vessel walls.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-290ff749460960cc500499bfce8172f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35913765c2d7101f6d284ecd039952ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b625d3948531b3dcd9b53a158a3a5265&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd415d5735a4c089d6c4d66fc2f63d48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903895</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dünnwandiger Kessel">
  <data key="d0">Dünnwandiger Kessel</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A thin-walled vessel analyzed under the assumption of a plane stress state (ESZ), characterized by radius and wall thickness, subjected to internal or external pressure.&lt;SEP&gt;A thin-walled, spherical vessel with a radius subjected to internal pressure, producing tangential and circumferential stresses&lt;SEP&gt;German term for thin-walled vessels, introduced in section 11.2.4 as an engineering application in pressure vessel design.&lt;SEP&gt;A section (11.2.4) of the German stress analysis document covering thin-walled vessel theory, including derivation of longitudinal and circumferential (hoop) stresses, vessel formulas, and principal stress analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;German for "thin-walled pressure vessels", a topic covered in the section where the footer citation appears.&lt;SEP&gt;A technical mechanics concept referring to thin-walled pressure vessels discussed in the academic material.&lt;SEP&gt;A German term for thin-walled vessel, mentioned as the surrounding context (Section 11.2.4) for the engineering calculations.</data>
