<node id="Spannungstensor">
  <data key="d0">Spannungstensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A tensor formed by arranging the components of the stress vectors from three mutually perpendicular sectional areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical tensor representing the stress state with 3x3 components in the three-dimensional case (noting symmetry), reduced in the plane stress state&lt;SEP&gt;German technical term for Stress tensor, depicted in the graphic above the letter 'L'.&lt;SEP&gt;The German language term for stress tensors used in the continuum mechanics content.&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for the Cauchy stress tensor&lt;SEP&gt;A stress tensor, a key mechanical principle discussed in the technical content anchored to the textbook Technische Mechanik 2.&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for stress tensors, representing an engineering mechanics concept discussed in the surrounding document.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ff4ce898bbbc110abe830de75521c6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3eaae8681c23616569341d164404cba8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dbabf4bbc38075b362efc1cf68c92ba</data>
