<node id="Ebener Spannungszustand">
  <data key="d0">Ebener Spannungszustand</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Plane stress state (ESZ), assumed for the analysis of thin-walled vessels, where stresses in the radial direction can be neglected if the wall thickness is much smaller than the radius.&lt;SEP&gt;Plane stress state (ESZ) with a reduced stress tensor containing components sigma_x, sigma_y, and tau_xy&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for the plane stress state&lt;SEP&gt;A plane stress state, a mechanical principle discussed in the technical content anchored to the textbook Technische Mechanik 2.&lt;SEP&gt;Plane stress state, a condition in which stress components are analyzed in two dimensions, reducing the stress tensor to three independent components.&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for plane stress conditions, often abbreviated as ESZ, referring to the two-dimensional stress state analyzed in the diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;The German term for the plane stress state, identified as a key mechanical concept summarized in the document.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6db9821cc411ad75c459848cb9d08e4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3eaae8681c23616569341d164404cba8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b3409eb9a461816c78110bf52f37f07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohrscher Spannungskreis">
  <data key="d0">Mohrscher Spannungskreis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mohrscher Spannungskreis is the German technical term for Mohr's stress circle, a graphical method and geometric representation of the coordinate transformation for stresses. It is primarily utilized to analyze and visualize a two-dimensional state of stress (plane stress) at a specific point within a material, such as a plate. Within this graphical construction, every arbitrary section through the point corresponds to a specific point on the circle, allowing engineers to visually determine how the stress state changes depending on the orientation of the section.

The method is highly valuable for determining stresses in arbitrary directions, as well as identifying extreme stress values and their associated section directions. Most notably, it is used to derive the principal stresses and principal shear stresses. The mathematical foundation of the Mohrscher Spannungskreis relies on stress transformation equations that produce quadratic expressions; the appearance of these squared terms is directly consistent with the quadratic forms prevalent in Mohr's circle analysis, specifically those involving sin(φ) and cos(φ).

In the context of engineering education and literature, the Mohrscher Spannungskreis is a fundamental topic frequently discussed in German-language technical mechanics lectures and instructional materials, such as "Technische Mechanik 2." It is commonly presented in dedicated sections of mechanics documents—for example, Section 11.2.3—often alongside the broader context of principal stresses. The instructional coverage of this graphical method typically includes the analysis of several special cases, such as the uniaxial tension state, the pure shear stress state, and the hydrostatic stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5db8101dd80f03fc824342c78093902c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80068bee1bee4c2ec1a3ff3d5d6d4f71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05f0cba91a41b2018384acbb7174e398&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1daacc45ca5900ab841904f26a5061a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb9e40b1a13627933f97ce043e59f768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165c1196600ad5f85ab89d1be16e9bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6546507247b4802eb945e2a493cb040&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada0944efa350d49f7642c228e06590&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93703d14a7c8d37c2c5e2aae854f6240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dbabf4bbc38075b362efc1cf68c92ba</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903990</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hauptspannungen">
  <data key="d0">Hauptspannungen</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hauptspannungen is the German technical term for "Principal Stresses," a fundamental concept in continuum mechanics and the mechanics of materials. It refers to the extreme values of normal stress—specifically, the maximum and minimum normal stresses—that occur at a specific point within a stressed material. These principal stresses, typically computed and denoted as σ₁ and σ₂, act in directions known as principal directions, which define the principal axis system of the stress state.

The values of the Hauptspannungen are mathematically derived from standard stress components (σx, σy, and τxy) using specific formulas. They can also be visually determined as the extreme points on Mohr's stress circle (Mohrscher Spannungskreis). The principal directions dictate the angles (often denoted as φ* and φ**) at which these extreme normal stresses occur, a process that is deeply tied to stress coordinate transformation based on the cutting direction angle φ. Furthermore, these stress values are intrinsically linked to the maximum shear stress (τmax) experienced by the material, which is calculated using the formula (σ₁ − σ₂)/2. 

Within academic and technical documentation, the study of Hauptspannungen frequently appears as a dedicated topic in engineering textbooks. For instance, it is explicitly examined in section 11.2.2 of certain mechanics documents, which analyze the extremal values of normal and shear stresses as functions of the cutting angle φ. Beyond theoretical calculations, the concept has direct practical applications in structural analysis, such as evaluating thin-walled vessels. In such scenarios where no shear stresses occur, the standard longitudinal and circumferential stresses (σx and σφ) align directly with the principal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5db8101dd80f03fc824342c78093902c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-011c3fc433230965d7a734e4052f7689&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85ca1064506b38c8fe493e3ffc533c71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3186598e4d31ef04b0826dc0b3dad17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8735ecf72f07b4cd7b82e22f492ade68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56ba60dee95d9f5127479d5f80c141c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6546507247b4802eb945e2a493cb040&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada0944efa350d49f7642c228e06590&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93703d14a7c8d37c2c5e2aae854f6240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dbabf4bbc38075b362efc1cf68c92ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd415d5735a4c089d6c4d66fc2f63d48</data>
