</node>
<node id="Invariants Of The Stress Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Invariants Of The Stress Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Properties of the stress tensor that do not change value under coordinate transformation, specifically the sum of normal stresses and the determinant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784900562</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Normal Stresses, referred to as Normalspannungen in German, are a fundamental concept in continuum mechanics and advanced engineering mechanics. They are defined as the stress components that act perpendicular to a surface, cutting surface, or respective coordinate plane. Derived from the decomposition of the stress vector, they represent three of its components. In a stress tensor matrix, normal stresses are located along the principal diagonal and are characterized by coinciding tensor indices (i = j). They are commonly denoted using simplified notation as σ_x, σ_y, and σ_z (or σ_xx, σ_yy, and σ_zz), representing the normal stress directions in a given stress state. Additionally, the sum of these perpendicular stress components represents an invariant of the stress tensor.

The magnitude of normal stresses, such as σξ and ση, typically depends on the cutting direction, defined by the angle φ. However, in a specific hydrostatic stress state, these perpendicular stresses are equal in all sectional directions and become independent of the angle φ. Through stress transformation analysis, the extremal values of these normal stresses are often sought. These extremal values are known as the principal stresses, which are attained at specific principal directions.

Normal stresses maintain a specific relationship with shear stresses at critical section orientations. In sections experiencing extreme shear stress, normal stresses generally do not vanish. Conversely, in a defined state of pure shear stress, normal stress components are entirely absent. Furthermore, known values of normal stresses, alongside shear stresses, are utilized to define the geometric properties—specifically the center and radius—of Mohr's Circle. Finally, in specific visual representations within technical documentation, normal stresses are depicted by four thick red arrows originating near the corners and pointing tangentially toward the center of each edge of the material element.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be68185d5f9be3b57dbcc2d1f774fbb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdf4cd832b592c8035347d2e6e3804e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85ca1064506b38c8fe493e3ffc533c71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6efac359e95ae176e5b41c44f20b3bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40562a8ce19d10e78736b201626c9b32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cabab253ce4ee75c98c51cfd7f1a27b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8735ecf72f07b4cd7b82e22f492ade68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56ba60dee95d9f5127479d5f80c141c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677dd5182b714188875fefaff19c7680&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0f20e6bdef6f836f08f3407dd794183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97b33a57b26cea4acd1fcb9fd8389687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-350755d5c7a3a740e91d107394468b5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-269e2be218f17e48feb7ecb2c0ddf3f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903876</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shear Stresses, also referred to in German as *Schubspannungen*, represent a fundamental concept in continuum and advanced engineering mechanics. They are defined as stress components that act parallel to a surface, or specifically, parallel to the cutting surface and faces of an infinitesimal material element. Derived from the decomposition of the stress vector, these tangential stresses are further decomposed into coordinate-direction components. Mathematically, Shear Stresses are represented as the off-diagonal elements within a stress tensor matrix. Denoted typically as τ_ij (including specific components such as τ_xy, τ_xz, τ_yz, and τ_ξη), they account for six of the stress tensor components. These components exhibit symmetry; they generate opposing couples on the parallel faces of a material element that must mathematically cancel each other out to maintain rotational equilibrium.

A defining characteristic of Shear Stresses involves their behavior under specific geometric orientations and stress states. They vanish completely in a purely hydrostatic stress state where normal stresses are equal in all directions. Furthermore, Shear Stresses drop to zero in principal directions, which occur at critical orientations or a specific rotation of the cutting plane. The analytical calculation of these extremal stress values, which are directly related to normal stresses at critical orientations, is essential for defining the geometric properties of Mohr's Circle. 

In practical engineering applications, such as the structural analysis of thin-walled vessels, Shear Stresses are frequently subject to simplifying assumptions. Under the thin-wall assumption, the vessel wall is considered to be in a plane stress state where shear stress components are entirely absent. By concluding that these parallel stresses do not occur in the thin-walled vessel wall or on the planes of the differential material element, engineers ensure that the remaining normal stresses—specifically σ_x and σ_φ—act directly as the principal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be68185d5f9be3b57dbcc2d1f774fbb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb31546e781f11ba51b9575be2e9cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-493acae899ca4eaaedf536afa97f481b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdf4cd832b592c8035347d2e6e3804e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cabab253ce4ee75c98c51cfd7f1a27b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-379e0ab80e418646d3bbaa7c2a022894&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8be23ae96862171a1f9cb78cb1cfcb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8735ecf72f07b4cd7b82e22f492ade68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56ba60dee95d9f5127479d5f80c141c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677dd5182b714188875fefaff19c7680&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0f20e6bdef6f836f08f3407dd794183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd415d5735a4c089d6c4d66fc2f63d48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-269e2be218f17e48feb7ecb2c0ddf3f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9c3492ddaae5a544709ab9626c48a75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903874</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Stress State">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Stress State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Hydrostatic Stress State, also known as "Hydrostatischer Spannungszustand" or "Sonderfall 3" (Special Case 3), is a special stress condition in which all principal normal stresses are equal ($\sigma_x = \sigma_y = \sigma_0$) across all cutting and sectional directions, completely independent of the angle $\phi$. In this state, all shear stress components vanish completely, resulting in a condition that causes purely volumetric change without any distortion of the material.

Within academic and engineering contexts, this stress state serves as a fundamental theoretical concept typically presented before practical worked examples. It is notably discussed within the Mohr's Stress Circle section and in the context of coordinate transformations. Specifically, this special case is detailed in Section 11.2.1 of educational content sourced from the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2'.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdf4cd832b592c8035347d2e6e3804e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3186598e4d31ef04b0826dc0b3dad17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83ac1dafe6c2621a4fb3a412b3493553&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98ff466f481e3ef48f03b583574686cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d8ef0753f64ed72eb451c29b8108d08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f2990ab5263ad174ce6fec37fef1115&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1daacc45ca5900ab841904f26a5061a7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Principal Stresses, also referred to by the German term *Hauptspannungen*, represent a fundamental concept in solid mechanics, structural engineering, and strength of materials. They are defined as the extreme (maximum and minimum) normal stresses acting on a material element at a given point. These extremal stress values occur on specific planes, known as principal planes, where the shear stress components equal zero. Mathematically, principal stresses are the eigenvalues of the Cauchy stress tensor and are conventionally denoted as σ₁, σ₂, and σ₃ (with σ₁ &gt; σ₂ in two-dimensional analyses). 

The determination of principal stresses relies heavily on stress transformation theory, which involves transforming stress components to arbitrary coordinate orientations using tensor notation. Analytically, they are derived by extremizing the normal stress with respect to a rotation angle φ, where the angle is found using the relation tan2φ* = 2τxy / (σx - σy). By substituting trigonometric values into the stress transformation equations, the principal stresses can be mathematically expressed using the first stress invariant as σ₁,₂ = I₁/2 ± √(I₁²/4 - I₂). Graphically, principal stresses are visualized and determined using Mohr's Stress Circle. On this diagram, the principal stresses correspond to the intersections of the circle with the horizontal σ-axis. They can be calculated directly from the circle's center (σM) and radius (R) using the formula σ₁,₂ = σM ± R. 

The identification of principal stresses is a critical analytical step preceding equilibrium conditions, serving as an essential metric for evaluating structural failure and material yielding criteria. In practical engineering applications, such as the analysis of thin-walled pressure vessels under a plane stress state, the longitudinal (tangential) and circumferential (hoop) stresses act directly as the principal stresses because no shear stresses occur on those respective planes. Furthermore, under unique loading conditions like a hydrostatic stress state, all principal stresses become equal (σ₁ = σ₂ = σ₃ = σ₀), causing Mohr's circle to degenerate into a single point on the horizontal axis and rendering every direction a principal direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e6a6756f3e11958caa7f25916a94bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd1f71b01c1aa5c67c36a16a597748fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5f08150d01a4afad6a7e20e54169ec1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1678f499f1d6db4c88e28c21ca3f7ac4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb31546e781f11ba51b9575be2e9cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5db8101dd80f03fc824342c78093902c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb966affea49c8515afe80d893d4ba39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-341774a38a3b4fd47c6656391af81dd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68094852a469c1054fde9d0f452e6077&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-493acae899ca4eaaedf536afa97f481b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e0e1d2a168869746758dfc06077ca50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80068bee1bee4c2ec1a3ff3d5d6d4f71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05f0cba91a41b2018384acbb7174e398&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83ac1dafe6c2621a4fb3a412b3493553&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d8ef0753f64ed72eb451c29b8108d08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82d26e50b1a6b9a189224f9805e4217e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f2990ab5263ad174ce6fec37fef1115&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-379e0ab80e418646d3bbaa7c2a022894&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85630b6d3d048fd50a738bf6456c479f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae7aec8cf21235bd79c851b9cc6978f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb9e40b1a13627933f97ce043e59f768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8be23ae96862171a1f9cb78cb1cfcb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ca440e5727deb504109d3cda9b3642d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012dfb2f1f77750672c0c98cb5c5f782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b625d3948531b3dcd9b53a158a3a5265&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dbabf4bbc38075b362efc1cf68c92ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-350755d5c7a3a740e91d107394468b5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f852bec3a8e229cf7fc9b647545ae29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcd5ad0575ad16303ca4601a26ea32f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-269e2be218f17e48feb7ecb2c0ddf3f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9c3492ddaae5a544709ab9626c48a75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
