<node id="Principal Directions">
  <data key="d0">Principal Directions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Principal Directions, also widely referred to as "Hauptrichtungen" in German, are orthogonal sectional directions or planes along which principal stresses act. In the study of technical mechanics, these directions are identified by examining the extremal values of normal and shear stresses. Specifically, Principal Directions are the two perpendicular orientations, separated by an angle of π/2, at which normal stresses attain their maximum and minimum extreme values. Concurrently, the shear stress completely vanishes along these orientations.

Mathematically, the angles defining these directions, typically denoted as φ* (and separated by π/2 due to the periodicity of the tangent function), are determined by the equation tan(2φ*) = 2τ_xy / (σ_x - σ_y). Because the shear stress τξη drops to zero at these specific angles, Principal Directions directly correspond to the eigenvectors of the stress tensor. Additionally, these orientations can be determined graphically using Mohr's circle construction. 

A practical example of this concept is observed in cases of uniaxial tension, where the Principal Directions are oriented at φ* = 0° and φ** = 90°. This indicates that the principal stress directions align directly with the applied loading direction. Whether derived through trigonometric extremal values, stress tensor eigenvectors, or geometric constructions, these directions are fundamental for analyzing stress states in technical mechanics lecture documents (such as Section 11.2.2).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1626d6e3dffee746e5efebf5c0f44ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85ca1064506b38c8fe493e3ffc533c71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3186598e4d31ef04b0826dc0b3dad17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f2990ab5263ad174ce6fec37fef1115&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-379e0ab80e418646d3bbaa7c2a022894&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904066</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Axis System">
  <data key="d0">Principal Axis System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific coordinate system whose axes are aligned parallel to the principal directions of stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Hauptachsensystem, it is discussed in the technical mechanics document in relation to principal stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;A coordinate system associated with principal stresses discussed in section 11.2.2.&lt;SEP&gt;A coordinate system (Hauptachsensystem) where the 1-axis and 2-axis align with principal stress directions σ1 and σ2, used as a reference framework for stress analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Hauptachsensystem, a coordinate system discussed within stress transformation theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56ba60dee95d9f5127479d5f80c141c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97b33a57b26cea4acd1fcb9fd8389687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-350755d5c7a3a740e91d107394468b5c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Shear Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Principal Shear Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The extreme values of shear stresses, calculated from the derivative of the shear stress equation.&lt;SEP&gt;Referred to as Hauptschubspannungen, these are extremal values of shear stresses calculated during stress transformation analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum shear stresses calculated during stress transformation, used to predict material yielding or fracture.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum and minimum shear stresses found through geometric construction of Mohr's circle, alongside principal stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Extreme shear stress values (Hauptschubspannungen) identified using Mohr's Stress Circle construction methodology.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum shear stresses at a point, referred to as Hauptschubspannungen in German, determined graphically using Mohr's Stress Circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5f08150d01a4afad6a7e20e54169ec1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1678f499f1d6db4c88e28c21ca3f7ac4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5db8101dd80f03fc824342c78093902c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903843</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr's Stress Circle">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Stress Circle</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Mohr's Stress Circle, also known by its German name *Mohrscher Spannungskreis*, is a graphical method named after Otto Mohr that is widely utilized in continuum mechanics and engineering stress analysis. It serves as a visual and analytical framework for representing, computing, and transforming the state of stress at a given point. The method is primarily applied to two-dimensional or plane stress states, allowing for the graphical determination of principal stresses, principal directions, extreme or maximum shear stresses, and overall stress tensor transformations. 

The technique operates by constructing a circle within the sigma-tau ($\sigma$-$\tau$) plane, which represents normal stress and shear stress, respectively. The circle is defined by specific geometric properties—namely its center (such as a mean stress, $\sigma_m$, which may be located at a specific value like 15 MPa in given problem contexts) and its radius—which are derived from known initial normal and shear stresses. A fundamental geometric principle of this method is that physical angles of rotation ($\phi$) in the material's cross-section are represented as double the angle ($2\phi$) with an opposite sign on the circle. Through this geometric rotation and construction, engineers can evaluate stresses acting on arbitrary cross-sections and identify extreme values.

In educational and instructional contexts, such as the German engineering mechanics textbook *Technische Mechanik 2* (often discussed in dedicated sections like topic 11.2.3), Mohr's Stress Circle is taught as a foundational step-by-step procedural methodology. The curriculum covers the general theory of biaxial stress states alongside specific special cases, providing a comprehensive framework for analyzing uniaxial tension, pure shear stress states, and hydrostatic stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e6a6756f3e11958caa7f25916a94bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5f08150d01a4afad6a7e20e54169ec1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1678f499f1d6db4c88e28c21ca3f7ac4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5db8101dd80f03fc824342c78093902c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a32ade397f323fb086e2f86bb63b1a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e0e1d2a168869746758dfc06077ca50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6db9821cc411ad75c459848cb9d08e4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4ade0a9e41cfa09698117c999ef93d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9d212905be253e446a02b9ce4b7ba85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f2990ab5263ad174ce6fec37fef1115&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165c1196600ad5f85ab89d1be16e9bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6546507247b4802eb945e2a493cb040&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a1ba292932d5ab22c0f62081869648c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac4ea6191b5ef268551e8e517a153c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97b33a57b26cea4acd1fcb9fd8389687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e3cdc6151a0814d04d2cc632b0969f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-269e2be218f17e48feb7ecb2c0ddf3f7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903843</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Otto Mohr">
  <data key="d0">Otto Mohr</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A historical figure (1835-1918) known for developing the graphical method for stress analysis known as Mohr's circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ba92f82e2b057ed886fb58574a01ea0a-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
