<node id="Page Number 5 (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 5 (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '5' from a German textbook on Technical Mechanics (Technische Mechanik 2: Elastostatik). This page covers Section 11.1 introducing stress vectors and stress tensors, including definitions of normal and shear stresses in loaded bodies. Part of a Springer Viewex academic textbook series.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eadc704f7f5e7980d43e7d870873727e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram of a loaded body with an internal cross-section (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram of a loaded body with an internal cross-section (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This schematic illustrates a solid body under complex loading conditions, including individual forces ($F_1, F_2, F_i$) and a distributed surface load ($p$). A green dashed line indicates an arbitrary cut surface $s$ through the body. In the context of the provided text regarding stress vectors and tensors, this image serves as the primary model for defining how external mechanical loads induce internal stresses that vary across an internal sectional area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2616f80e6ffe22a7bb18ebfbbc1b015a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Vector Decomposition at a Point (image)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Vector Decomposition at a Point (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram visually defines the stress vector at a point P on a sectional area A of a loaded body. It illustrates how the internal resultant force ΔF acting on a differential area ΔA is decomposed into a normal stress component (σ) acting perpendicular to the surface and a shear stress component (τ) acting tangentially. This graphical representation serves as the primary visual aid for Section 11.1, explaining the transition from macroscopic internal forces to localized stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-13b9a52b59b74e8f8b6c3180f71ccba2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Definition Limit Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Definition Limit Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents the fundamental limit process defining stress at a point within a loaded body, transitioning from average stress (ΔF/ΔA) over an area element to pointwise stress. Contextually supports the introduction of the stress tensor concept, showing that complete stress state description requires consideration of all possible cutting orientations, not just a single stress vector.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 6 - Introduction to Stress Vector Properties (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 6 - Introduction to Stress Vector Properties (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 6 of a German mechanics textbook covering stress vector fundamentals. Establishes that stress vectors are location-dependent, that complete stress distribution requires knowing vectors at all points, and critically, that a single stress vector cannot adequately describe the stress state at a point since stresses depend on cutting direction (normal vector). This motivates the introduction of the stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-271a171e393b79c95418cb0645e75d72</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 7 - Stress Tensor Introduction (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 7 - Stress Tensor Introduction (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 7 of Section 11.1 marks a pivotal transition from single stress vector limitations to complete stress state characterization using three perpendicular stress vectors on Cartesian coordinate planes. It introduces decomposition into normal and shear stress components and the double-index notation system, forming the conceptual bridge to the formal stress tensor definition in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-677dd5182b714188875fefaff19c7680</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Volume Element Stress Tensor Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Volume Element Stress Tensor Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the nine components of the Cauchy stress tensor acting on an infinitesimal cubic element at point P. Aligned with a Cartesian coordinate system (x, y, z), the cube's dimensions are denoted by dx, dy, and dz. Red vectors represent three normal stresses (σxx, σyy, σzz) and six shear stresses (τxy, τxz, τyx, τyz, τzx, τzy) on the positive faces. This visualization supports the text's explanation that the stress state at a point is fully characterized by these components on three mutually perpendicular planes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d97fb385691a70a5c314b2a77a107e6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Element Stress State Diagram (y-z plane) (image)">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Element Stress State Diagram (y-z plane) (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the stress components acting on an infinitesimal rectangular element centered at point M in the y-z plane. It visually defines the normal stresses ($\\sigma_y, \\sigma_z$) and shear stresses ($\\tau_{yz}, \\tau_{zy}$) that constitute the stress tensor at a point. The visualization supports the textual explanation of how the state of stress is determined by vectors on mutually perpendicular planes and demonstrates the positive sign convention for stresses in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b23912fb95dffaea41a466afd896e511</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Tensor Index Notation Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Tensor Index Notation Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image serves as a visual legend for the double-index notation of stress components (specifically shear stress $\tau_{xy}$). It explains that the first subscript refers to the direction of the surface normal ('Richtung der Flächennormale') and the second subscript refers to the direction of the stress component itself ('Richtung der Spannungskomponente'). This diagram directly supports the technical discussion regarding the definition and sign conventions of the stress state at a point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a49d921c1af07b0743c98652a77081e4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 8 - Stress Tensor Notation and Sign Convention (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 8 - Stress Tensor Notation and Sign Convention (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 8 of Section 11.1 introduces simplified notation for normal stresses, constructs the stress vector for x-normal cutting surfaces, and establishes the sign convention where positive stresses indicate tension and negative stresses indicate compression on infinitesimal volume elements. This page transitions from double-index notation toward the complete stress tensor formulation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-60e545266c1919041c313cfba39a94c4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Notational equivalence for normal stress components (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Notational equivalence for normal stress components (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the simplified notation σ_x ≡ σ_xx, σ_y ≡ σ_yy, σ_z ≡ σ_zz for the diagonal (normal) stress components of the Cauchy stress tensor. Since normal stresses have identical index pairs, the double-index tensor notation can be abbreviated with a single index, streamlining written expressions in stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Cauchy Stress Tensor Symmetry Condition (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Cauchy Stress Tensor Symmetry Condition (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Establishes symmetry of the Cauchy stress tensor through moment equilibrium: τ_ij = τ_ji. Derived by balancing moments about axes through the infinitesimal element's center, proving conjugate shear stresses are equal. Reduces independent stress components from nine to six, forming the theoretical basis for stress analysis in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95309ae773a0c5fe9703868d35942eff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 9 - Stress Tensor Symmetry Derivation (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 9 - Stress Tensor Symmetry Derivation (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 9 of Section 11.1 (Spannungsvektor und Spannungstensor) continues the derivation of the stress tensor by decomposing stress vectors into three normal and six shear stresses, then establishing their interdependence through moment equilibrium about coordinate axes through point P. This critical page demonstrates shear stress symmetry, reducing nine components to six independent stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9cabab253ce4ee75c98c51cfd7f1a27b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Source citation footer - Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source citation footer - Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">Bibliographic source citation on page 8 attributing content about stress vectors and stress tensors (Section 11.1) to Gross, Hauger, Schröder, Wall (2017), 'Technische Mechanik 2', published by Springer Vieweg. This footer provides academic attribution for diagrams and explanations covering stress state decomposition, notation conventions, and moment equilibrium of shear stresses. It enables source verification and further study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Element Diagram in y-z Plane (image)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Element Diagram in y-z Plane (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram visualizes the positive sign convention for normal and shear stresses on a 2D differential element in the y-z plane. Centered at point M, the element shows normal stresses ($\\sigma_y, \\sigma_z$) and shear stresses ($\\tau_{yz}, \\tau_{zy}$) as red vectors. The green labels indicate dimensions $dy$ and $dz$. This illustration directly supports the text's explanation of stress vector decomposition, the definition of the symmetric stress tensor, and the physical basis for stress equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cb64a0edb671186691ad9f74cd07ae9d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Moment Equilibrium Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Moment Equilibrium Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation $\Sigma M^{(M)} = 0$ defines the moment equilibrium around point M for an infinitesimal volume element. It is the foundational principle used to prove the symmetry of the stress tensor, establishing that cross shear stresses are equal and thereby reducing the independent stress components from nine to six.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Moment Equilibrium Derivation for Shear Stress Symmetry (image)">
  <data key="d0">Moment Equilibrium Derivation for Shear Stress Symmetry (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image illustrates the mathematical proof for the symmetry of shear stresses within a stress tensor. By establishing a moment equilibrium equation around the x-axis for an infinitesimal element, as described in the surrounding text regarding 'Momentengleichgewicht', the derivation demonstrates that the shear stress component τ_yz must equal τ_zy. This relationship is fundamental to mechanics as it confirms the stress tensor is symmetric, reducing the number of independent components from nine to six.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bf36ff85412f3dea295a176bbfb87119</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Tensor Symmetry Condition (Moment Equilibrium) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Tensor Symmetry Condition (Moment Equilibrium) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation represents the symmetry condition of the Cauchy stress tensor derived from moment equilibrium. It establishes that shear stress components are pairwise equal (σ_ij = σ_ji), reducing the nine stress components to six independent ones. This is fundamental to continuum mechanics and stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Trivial Identity (Likely OCR Artifact or Placeholder) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Trivial Identity (Likely OCR Artifact or Placeholder) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The expression 1000 = 1000 is a tautological identity with no mathematical significance. In the context of the stress tensor discussion, it likely represents an OCR error, formatting artifact, or placeholder, as the actual mathematical content concerns the symmetry conditions of the Cauchy stress tensor derived from moment equilibrium (τ_ij = τ_ji).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page Number 10 (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page Number 10 (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 10 acts as a navigational marker within a German engineering mechanics text. It identifies the specific page where the formal definition of the symmetric stress tensor (Spannungstensor) as a 3x3 matrix is introduced, synthesizing the principles of normal and shear stresses derived in preceding sections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-765ad24fd0f91713e3dc722570316ee2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Cauchy Stress Tensor (Symmetric 3×3) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Cauchy Stress Tensor (Symmetric 3×3) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents the Cauchy stress tensor σ as a symmetric 3×3 matrix containing three normal stresses (diagonal) and three independent shear stresses (off-diagonal). Symmetry follows from moment equilibrium, yielding six independent stress components. This tensor uniquely defines the stress state at a point and underpins subsequent analysis of plane stress conditions in thin structural elements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 11 - Introduction to Plane Stress State (Ebener Spannungszustand) (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 11 - Introduction to Plane Stress State (Ebener Spannungszustand) (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 11 introduces section 11.2 on plane stress state (Ebener Spannungszustand). It defines a 'Scheibe' as a thin planar element loaded in-plane, establishes that z-direction stresses vanish, and that remaining stresses (σx, σy, τxy) are constant across thickness. This page transitions from the general 3D stress tensor discussion to the simplified 2D case, forming the basis for subsequent plane stress analysis methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1685984065ed0dcd54dea046d7d4f918</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram of a Plate Subjected to Plane Stress (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram of a Plate Subjected to Plane Stress (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This schematic illustrates the physical definition of a 'Scheibe' (plate) used to model a plane stress state. It shows a thin rectangular element of thickness t subjected to in-plane forces (F1, F2, Fi). The visualization reinforces the concept that for such structures, internal stresses are assumed to act only within the x-y plane, allowing for the simplification of complex 3D structural problems into 2D analyses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-49e2cb25fd70f2b5f3b6c22629fe461a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Trivial Equilibrium Identity (Stress Transformation Context) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Trivial Equilibrium Identity (Stress Transformation Context) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A trivial algebraic identity (0=0) appearing in the context of equilibrium analysis for an infinitesimal triangular element during coordinate transformation of plane stress states. Likely represents force cancellation or a degenerate equilibrium condition where opposing stress contributions nullify each other, consistent with the symmetric stress tensor properties discussed in the surrounding continuum mechanics lecture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b3409eb9a461816c78110bf52f37f07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress Tensor (Ebener Spannungstensor) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress Tensor (Ebener Spannungstensor) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The 2×2 symmetric stress tensor representing the plane stress state (ebener Spannungszustand) for thin plates. Contains normal stresses σ_x, σ_y on the diagonal and shear stress τ_xy off-diagonal, with three independent components. Derived from the 3D stress tensor by assuming vanishing z-direction stresses due to thin geometry and in-plane loading. Serves as foundation for coordinate transformation analysis in section 11.2.1.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Triangular Element for Stress Transformation (Technical Diagram)">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Triangular Element for Stress Transformation (Technical Diagram)</data>
  <data key="d1">Technical Diagram</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram visualizes the derivation of stress transformation equations for a plane stress state. It shows an infinitesimal triangular element with sides dx, dy, and dη, subjected to normal and shear stresses (σx, σy, τxy, σξ, τξη) in both the original (x, y) and a rotated (ξ, η) coordinate system. The orientation is defined by the angle φ. This model is essential for calculating stresses on any arbitrary plane passing through a point in a loaded thin structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c2cf3a337f73c88af5880c2708e9c48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium equation for normal stress transformation (ξ-direction) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium equation for normal stress transformation (ξ-direction) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This is the force balance in the ξ-direction for an infinitesimal triangular element cut from a plate under plane stress. It relates the normal stress σ_ξ on an inclined plane at angle φ to the stress components σ_x, σ_y, and τ_xy in the original coordinate system, forming the basis for coordinate transformation of the stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium Equation for Shear Stress Coordinate Transformation (Plane Stress) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium Equation for Shear Stress Coordinate Transformation (Plane Stress) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Force equilibrium in the ξ-direction for an infinitesimal triangular element under plane stress. Used to derive the transformation formula for shear stress τ_ξη when rotating coordinates by angle φ. This is a key step in establishing Mohr's circle theory and determining principal stresses in two-dimensional stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae7aec8cf21235bd79c851b9cc6978f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 13 - Coordinate Transformation Stress Equations (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 13 - Coordinate Transformation Stress Equations (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number '13' located at bottom-left of page 12 (index). Situated within section 11.2.1 on coordinate transformation for plane stress states. The page presents key stress transformation formulas including σ_ξ calculation using Mohr's circle trigonometric relations and the method for determining σ_η through π/2 rotation. Serves as reference point for the mathematical derivation of stress components under arbitrary section angles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b5475536a7fccb42b9f6f9e39d32b77</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium Equation for Stress Transformation in the ξ-Direction (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium Equation for Stress Transformation in the ξ-Direction (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This static equilibrium equation balances the force components acting along the rotated ξ-axis on an infinitesimal triangular element. By projecting the known x-y stress components onto the inclined surface, it mathematically derives the 2D coordinate transformation for normal stress. It explicitly demonstrates that stress components transform according to specific trigonometric rules, thereby proving that the state of stress is a second-order tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-82d26e50b1a6b9a189224f9805e4217e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stress Equilibrium Equation in Rotated Coordinates (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress Equilibrium Equation in Rotated Coordinates (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Force equilibrium in the η-direction for an infinitesimal triangular element extracted from a 2D stress body. Projects all stress components from the x,y-system onto the rotated ξ,η-system. This equation is the key intermediate step in deriving the tensor transformation law for plane stress, demonstrating that stresses transform as second-order tensors under coordinate rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Transformation Infinitesimal Wedge Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Transformation Infinitesimal Wedge Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This technical diagram depicts an infinitesimal triangular element used to derive the coordinate transformation equations for plane stress. It shows the relationship between stress components in the original $x,y$ coordinate system ($\\sigma_x, \\sigma_y, \\tau_{xy}$) and those in a rotated $\\xi,\\eta$ system ($\\sigma_\\xi, \\tau_{\\xi\\eta}$) defined by the angle $\\varphi$. The visual serves as a fundamental proof that stress is a second-order tensor whose components change predictably with rotation, maintaining the same physical state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65f35adb2f34cbe81a9755002339ad40</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stress Transformation Equation (Plane Stress) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress Transformation Equation (Plane Stress) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation transforms the shear stress component τ_xy from the original (x,y) coordinate system to the shear stress τ_ξη in a rotated (ξ,η) coordinate system at angle φ. It is one of the three stress transformation equations demonstrating that stress is a second-rank tensor. The relation is essential for determining principal stresses, principal directions, and maximum shear stresses in plane stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress Coordinate Transformation Equation for Orthogonal Normal Stress (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress Coordinate Transformation Equation for Orthogonal Normal Stress (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the normal stress $\sigma_{\eta}$ on an orthogonal plane by applying the 2D stress transformation formula at a rotation angle of $\varphi + \pi/2$. It demonstrates how stress components transform under coordinate rotation, serving as fundamental proof that the state of stress is a second-order tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Transformation Equation for $\sigma_{\eta}$ (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Transformation Equation for $\sigma_{\eta}$ (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation calculates the normal stress component $\sigma_{\eta}$ in a rotated coordinate system by applying a $\pi/2$ angular shift to the formula for $\sigma_{\xi}$. It uses basic trigonometric identities to demonstrate how stress components transform under rotation, establishing the mathematical foundation for the stress tensor in a 2D plane stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1f852bec3a8e229cf7fc9b647545ae29</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
