  <data key="d0">Corrupted Arithmetic Expression (Likely Principal Stress Formula) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">An algebraic chain of equalities involving the addition and division of simple fractions. Evaluated literally, the mathematical equality is false. Given the context of Mohr's circle and principal stresses, this is highly likely an OCR-misinterpreted formula for calculating stress transformations or principal stress angles in solid mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Extremal Shear Stress Derivation Fragment (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Extremal Shear Stress Derivation Fragment (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A numerical expression appearing within the section on principal shear stresses (Hauptschubspannungen) in plane stress analysis. The equation appears to be part of a derivation or example related to finding extremal shear stress values via Mohr's circle methodology, though as transcribed it contains apparent extraction artifacts that prevent direct mathematical verification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Corrupted/Anomalous Equation in Principal Stress Analysis Context (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Corrupted/Anomalous Equation in Principal Stress Analysis Context (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The expression $2 \times (10)^2 = 11$ is arithmetically invalid ($200 \neq 11$) and appears to be a digitization artifact. It bears no recognizable relationship to the surrounding discussion on principal stresses, shear stress extrema, or Mohr's stress circle in this German engineering mechanics text. Likely an OCR error or extraction corruption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93703d14a7c8d37c2c5e2aae854f6240</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Coefficient Evaluation at Origin in Principal Stress Coordinate System (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Coefficient Evaluation at Origin in Principal Stress Coordinate System (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluates coefficient function $a$ at coordinate origin $(0,0)$, yielding the zero vector through scalar multiplication by $\frac{11}{12}$. This computation arises within the principal stress analysis framework, likely as part of a numerical stress transformation calculation in the Hauptachsensystem.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Element at Maximum Shear Orientation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Element at Maximum Shear Orientation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates a 2D stress element oriented at the angle $\\varphi^{**}$ where shear stress is maximized ($\\tau_{\\text{max}}$). It visually demonstrates that while shear stress is at its peak, non-zero normal stresses ($\\sigma_M$) still act on the element. The included formula, $\\varphi^{**} = \\varphi^* + \\pi/4$, confirms that the plane of maximum shear stress is always rotated by 45 degrees from the principal stress planes, a fundamental concept in the analysis of stress states and Mohr's Circle discussed in the accompanying text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-012dfb2f1f77750672c0c98cb5c5f782</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr's Circle Stress Transformation Equation (OCR Corrupted) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Circle Stress Transformation Equation (OCR Corrupted) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">An OCR-garbled algebraic equation evaluating plane stress components (likely maximum shear stress or associated normal stress) at a 45-degree angle ($\varphi^{**}$), evidenced by the $\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{2}$ term, situated within the context of Mohr's Circle and principal stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6f29928f565c2e69cfd84b7a3af24ffe</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 21 - Transition to Mohr's Stress Circle (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 21 - Transition to Mohr's Stress Circle (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 21 marks the page introducing section 11.2.3 'Mohrscher Spannungskreis' (Mohr's Stress Circle). It follows the discussion of principal stresses (Hauptspannungen) and extreme shear stresses, serving as the transition point from analytical stress transformation methods to the graphical Mohr's circle approach in this continuum mechanics document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-97b33a57b26cea4acd1fcb9fd8389687</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Quelle Citation Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Quelle Citation Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">Bibliographic citation footer referencing the source textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' (2017) by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall, published by Springer Vieweg. Located at the bottom of page 21, it provides attribution for content covering principal stresses and shear stress theory in engineering mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-350755d5c7a3a740e91d107394468b5c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Analytical Stress Transformation and Mohr's Circle Formulation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Analytical Stress Transformation and Mohr's Circle Formulation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Despite severe OCR corruption, this equation represents the fundamental algebraic derivation of principal stresses and maximum shear stresses in a 2D plane stress state. It serves as the analytical foundation for constructing Mohr's Circle, allowing engineers to evaluate extremal stress values and predict structural failure without solely relying on graphical methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcd5ad0575ad16303ca4601a26ea32f2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Source Citation Footer – Gross et al. Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source Citation Footer – Gross et al. Technische Mechanik 2 (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A bibliographic source attribution footer citing the textbook 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017, Springer Vieweg). Located on a page discussing Mohr's Stress Circle construction for plane stress analysis. This footer indicates the educational content and diagrams derive from a standard reference in German engineering mechanics literature.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8e6a6756f3e11958caa7f25916a94bb6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Invalid/Corrupted Stress Transformation Expression (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Invalid/Corrupted Stress Transformation Expression (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematically invalid expression in the context of Mohr's stress circle analysis. The left-hand side evaluates to 0, not 1.5. It does not correspond to any standard stress transformation formula and likely represents a transcription error or corrupted equation within the discussion of Mohr's circle construction for plane stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a5f08150d01a4afad6a7e20e54169ec1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Mohrscher Spannungskreis Konstruktionsdiagramm (image)">
  <data key="d0">Mohrscher Spannungskreis Konstruktionsdiagramm (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a step-by-step graphical representation for constructing Mohr's Circle in mechanics. It visually demonstrates how to plot the stress states of two perpendicular faces (points P and P') using normal stresses ($\\sigma_x$, $\\sigma_y$) and shear stress ($\\tau_{xy}$) to find the circle's center ($\\sigma_M$) and radius ($r$). This construction is essential for determining principal stresses and maximum shear stresses in a material under load, as detailed in the accompanying section on stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-842c0517bb20a54922d1a75c277c68f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Formula for Mohr's Circle Radius and Stress Invariants (image)">
  <data key="d0">Formula for Mohr's Circle Radius and Stress Invariants (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image presents the mathematical formula for calculating the squared radius of Mohr's Circle of stress. It explicitly identifies the stress invariants—the components of the stress tensor that do not change with rotation—by using braces to label parts of the equation as 'Invariante'. This provides the technical basis for constructing the circle once the initial stress components ($\\sigma_x, \\sigma_y, \\tau_{xy}$) are known, as outlined in section 11.2.3 of the source material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d6513413e0a80f005888472d94d385e0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 23 - Mohr's Stress Circle Analysis (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 23 - Mohr's Stress Circle Analysis (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 23 from a German mechanics textbook, located within section 11.2.3 on Mohr's Stress Circle. This page covers stress state analysis using Mohr's circle, including stress transformation in rotated coordinate systems, principal stresses, and principal shear stresses. It builds on construction methods from page 21.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1678f499f1d6db4c88e28c21ca3f7ac4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Source citation footer for Mohr's Stress Circle content (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source citation footer for Mohr's Stress Circle content (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">A bibliographic citation footer attributing page 22 content about Mohr's Stress Circle to 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, and Wall (2017, Springer Vieweg). It provides academic provenance for diagrams and explanations of stress circle construction and principal stress determination within the lecture material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5db8101dd80f03fc824342c78093902c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr's Circle Diagram for Plane Stress Transformation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Circle Diagram for Plane Stress Transformation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a graphical representation of Mohr's Circle for a plane stress state. It illustrates the method for determining principal stresses (σ1, σ2) and the normal (σξ) and shear (τη) stresses acting on a plane oriented at an angle φ. The visualization directly supports the technical example provided in the text, which involves calculating these values for a specific set of initial stress components (σx, σy, τxy).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5ca440e5727deb504109d3cda9b3642d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-341774a38a3b4fd47c6656391af81dd6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 24 - Mohr's Stress Circle Special Cases (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 24 - Mohr's Stress Circle Special Cases (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number marker '24' for the page beginning coverage of special cases in Mohr's Stress Circle analysis (Sonderfälle), specifically uniaxial tension and pure shear stress states. Continues section 11.2.3 from the theoretical foundations established on pages 22-23.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a32ade397f323fb086e2f86bb63b1a7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Tensor for Uniaxial Tension (Einachsiger Zug) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Tensor for Uniaxial Tension (Einachsiger Zug) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This 2×2 stress tensor matrix represents Sonderfall 1 (uniaxial tension) in Mohr's stress circle analysis, where only σ_x = σ_0 is non-zero. It serves as a fundamental special case illustrating principal stress concepts, with Mohr's circle centered at σ_0/2 with radius σ_0/2, yielding maximum shear stress τ_max = σ_0/2 at 45° rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Uniaxial Tension Principal Stress Relation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Tension Principal Stress Relation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Establishes that under uniaxial tension (Sonderfall 1), the applied normal stress $\sigma_0$ directly corresponds to the first principal stress $\sigma_1$. This simplification occurs because no shear stresses are present, making the loading direction coincident with the principal stress direction in Mohr's Circle analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1626d6e3dffee746e5efebf5c0f44ad9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Uniaxial Tension Boundary Condition (Sonderfall 1) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Tension Boundary Condition (Sonderfall 1) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the zero-stress boundary conditions for uniaxial tension (einachsiger Zug) in Mohr's stress circle analysis. Sets σ_y = 0 and σ_2 = 0, establishing a degenerate stress state where only one principal stress is non-zero. Serves as a fundamental reference case alongside pure shear and hydrostatic stress states in the comparative study of two-dimensional stress transformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Uniaxial Tension Stress Element Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Tension Stress Element Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image displays a square stress element subjected to uniaxial tension ($\sigma_0$) along the x-axis. It serves as a primary visual aid for the section on Mohr's Circle, specifically illustrating the 'Uniaxial Tension' special case where normal stress exists in only one direction, facilitating the determination of principal stresses and maximum shear stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b44241248f288278ba7f5d8fa8160e3e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr's Circle Diagram for Uniaxial Tension (image)">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Circle Diagram for Uniaxial Tension (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram visualizes Mohr's circle for the special case of uniaxial tension (Sonderfall 1). It graphically represents the state of stress where one principal stress (σ₁) equals σ₀ and the other (σ₂) is zero. The circle's diameter extends from the origin to σ₀ on the normal stress axis, identifying the mean stress (σ_M) at the center and the maximum shear stress (τ_max) at the apex, demonstrating the fundamental relationships between normal and shear stresses in this specific loading condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30b2a42c115ed62e72e715daa89e2370</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress State Diagram for 45-Degree Orientation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Stress State Diagram for 45-Degree Orientation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram depicts a material element rotated at 45 degrees to illustrate a specific stress state within the framework of Mohr's circle analysis. It visually confirms that for a reference stress σ_0, both the normal stress (σ_M) and the maximum shear stress (τ_max) acting on these 45-degree planes are equal to σ_0/2. This serves as a graphical representation of 'Sonderfall 2' (Special Case 2: Pure Shear Stress State) or the derivation of maximum shear stress from uniaxial tension.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98f7f241f746726b97e3dc567365e483</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Pure Shear Stress State Mean Stress Calculation (image)">
  <data key="d0">Pure Shear Stress State Mean Stress Calculation (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image illustrates the mathematical definition of a pure shear stress state ('Reiner Schubspannungszustand') as part of the discussion on Mohr's circle. It shows a stress tensor matrix with zero normal stresses and equal shear stresses ($\tau_0$), leading to the conclusion that the mean normal stress ($\sigma_M$) is zero. This signifies that in a Mohr's circle representation for pure shear, the circle is centered exactly at the origin of the stress coordinate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6efac359e95ae176e5b41c44f20b3bbc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Pure Shear Stress Element Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Pure Shear Stress Element Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram depicts a square material element subjected to a state of pure shear stress, denoted by $\\tau_0$. It visually represents 'Special Case 2' from the section on Mohr's circle, showing how shear stresses are oriented on the faces of an element when normal stresses are absent. This representation is fundamental for determining principal stresses and maximum shear stresses using Mohr's graphical method.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95ef86ae323cfba68764c2acb2d46785</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr's Circle for Pure Shear Stress State (image)">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Circle for Pure Shear Stress State (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates Mohr's circle for a pure shear stress state ('Reiner Schubspannungszustand'). Centered at the origin of the $\\sigma$-$\\tau$ coordinate system, the circle shows that the principal stresses ($\\sigma_1, \\sigma_2$) are equal in magnitude to the applied shear stress $\\tau_0$ but act in opposite directions ($+\\tau_0$ and $-\\tau_0$). The radius of the circle represents the maximum shear stress $\\tau_{\\text{max}}$, providing a geometric method to determine stress transformations for this specific loading condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-74378ee7045b50cecf2c625c47685477</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram of Principal Stresses in a Pure Shear State (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram of Principal Stresses in a Pure Shear State (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the 'Pure Shear Stress State' (Sonderfall 2) as part of the discussion on Mohr's Circle of Stress. It depicts a material element rotated by $45^\\circ$, where the pure shear stress ($\\tau_0$) has transformed into principal stresses: a tensile stress $\\sigma_1 = \\tau_0$ and a compressive stress $\\sigma_2 = -\\tau_0$. This visualizes the fundamental relationship where maximum normal stresses occur at a $45^\\circ$ orientation relative to the planes of maximum shear.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40562a8ce19d10e78736b201626c9b32</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 25 - Mohr's Circle Special Case 3: Hydrostatic Stress (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 25 - Mohr's Circle Special Case 3: Hydrostatic Stress (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page 25 of a German engineering mechanics document covering section 11.2.3 'Mohrscher Spannungskreis.' This page presents Sonderfall 3: Hydrostatischer Spannungszustand (Hydrostatic Stress State), the final special case in a three-part series following uniaxial tension and pure shear. It represents a key reference point for understanding stress state analysis in strength of materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-05f0cba91a41b2018384acbb7174e398</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram of Hydrostatic Stress State (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram of Hydrostatic Stress State (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This image provides a visual representation of a hydrostatic (or isotropic) stress state within a 2D material element. It shows equal tensile normal stresses, denoted as $\\sigma_0$, acting along both the x and y axes without any shear stress components. As identified in the document's section on Mohr's Circle (11.2.3), this is 'Sonderfall 3', a unique case where principal stresses are equal, resulting in a Mohr's circle with zero radius.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98ff466f481e3ef48f03b583574686cc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Mohrscher Spannungskreis für einachsigen Zug (image)">
  <data key="d0">Mohrscher Spannungskreis für einachsigen Zug (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the Mohr's circle representation for a state of uniaxial tension (Einachsiger Zug). It shows a single point at $(\\sigma_0, 0)$ on the normal stress axis ($\\sigma$), indicating that the material is subjected only to a tensile stress $\\sigma_0$ with no accompanying shear stress ($\\tau = 0$). In Mohr's circle theory, this represents a degenerate case where the circle has a radius of zero, serving as a foundational example for understanding more complex planar stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2662124ebaa998fdaa13ffecb3add380</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Stress Tensor (Sonderfall 3) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Stress Tensor (Sonderfall 3) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the hydrostatic stress state as a special case of Mohr's circle analysis, where σ_x = σ_y = σ_0 with zero shear stresses. The stress tensor is isotropic, Mohr's circle collapses to a point, and no shear stresses exist in any plane. Represents uniform compression/tension such as in fluids at rest.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0d8ef0753f64ed72eb451c29b8108d08</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Diagram of Rotated Material Element under Uniaxial Tension (image)">
  <data key="d0">Diagram of Rotated Material Element under Uniaxial Tension (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram depicts a square material element subjected to uniaxial tensile stress $\sigma_0$, rotated by an angle $\varphi$ from the horizontal axis. It visually represents 'Special Case 1: Uniaxial Tension' within the study of Mohr's Circle, serving to illustrate the geometric relationship used to determine normal and shear stresses on inclined planes during stress transformation analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-153ac624cdef095260ab75841c027b8f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 26 - Mohr's Circle Example Problem (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 26 - Mohr's Circle Example Problem (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 26 in a German mechanics textbook, located in section 11.2.3 on Mohr's Stress Circle. This page transitions from theoretical special cases to a practical worked example: determining principal stresses and directions for a plane stress state (σ_x=50 MPa, σ_y=-20 MPa, τ_xy=30 MPa) using Mohr's circle graphical method.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7f2990ab5263ad174ce6fec37fef1115</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr's Circle Diagram for Plane Stress Analysis (image)">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Circle Diagram for Plane Stress Analysis (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates Mohr's Circle, a graphical method for determining principal stresses ($\\sigma_1, \\sigma_2$) and stress transformations. In the context of the provided text (Section 11.2.3), it serves as the solution basis for an example problem involving a given stress state ($\\sigma_x=50$ MPa, $\\sigma_y=-20$ MPa, $\\tau_{xy}=30$ MPa). The visualization demonstrates how the initial stress components define the circle's diameter and how the angle $2\\varphi^*$ identifies the orientation of principal planes relative to the x-axis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fb9e40b1a13627933f97ce043e59f768</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Principal Stress (σ₁) from Mohr's Circle (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Principal Stress (σ₁) from Mohr's Circle (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Represents the maximum principal stress σ₁ ≈ 61 MPa for a plane stress state with σₓ=50 MPa, σᵧ=-20 MPa, τₓᵧ=30 MPa, determined via Mohr's circle. It is the largest eigenvalue of the stress tensor, indicating the peak tensile stress on the principal plane where shear vanishes. Critical for failure analysis and structural design.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Minor Principal Stress from Mohr's Circle Analysis (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Minor Principal Stress from Mohr's Circle Analysis (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation σ₂ ≈ -31 MPa represents the minor (compressive) principal stress determined via Mohr's circle for a plane stress state with σ_x = 50 MPa, σ_y = -20 MPa, τ_xy = 30 MPa. It is the minimum normal stress where shear vanishes, essential for failure analysis and stress transformations in engineering mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3553477d678ccfb56c56a99c6a713a1b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Angle Determination via Mohr's Circle (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Principal Angle Determination via Mohr's Circle (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation demonstrates the angle-doubling property in Mohr's circle analysis, where $2\varphi^* = 40°$ on the circle corresponds to the physical principal direction $\varphi^* = 20°$. For the given stress state (σx=50 MPa, σy=-20 MPa, τxy=30 MPa), it identifies the orientation of principal planes where shear stress equals zero, essential for determining maximum normal stresses in structural analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress in Rotated Section Plane (σ_ξ) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress in Rotated Section Plane (σ_ξ) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation gives the normal stress component σ_ξ ≈ 58.4 MPa in a plane whose normal is inclined at φ = 30° to the x-axis, computed using Mohr's circle for the given plane stress state (σ_x = 50 MPa, σ_y = −20 MPa, τ_xy = 30 MPa). It is the result of a stress transformation and is relevant for component safety analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a1ba292932d5ab22c0f62081869648c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Transformed Shear Stress in Rotated Coordinate System (Mohr's Circle) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Transformed Shear Stress in Rotated Coordinate System (Mohr's Circle) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation gives the shear stress τ_{ξη} ≈ -13.5 MPa acting on a plane inclined at φ = 30° to the x-axis, computed via Mohr's circle transformation for a plane stress state (σ_x=50, σ_y=-20, τ_xy=30 MPa). It is part b) of an example demonstrating stress transformation, foundational for analyzing structural components like thin-walled pressure vessels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b1f8906173d951132fd77877e8868d53</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 28 (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 28 (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number indicating the start of section 11.2.4, 'Dünnwandiger Kessel' (Thin-walled Vessels). It marks the transition from Mohr's Circle examples to the analysis of cylindrical pressure vessels, outlining initial assumptions like plane stress state and beginning the derivation of longitudinal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dfafff355a9da39ab811d6385cb83520</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Thin-walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel Stress Analysis Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Thin-walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel Stress Analysis Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a comprehensive visual breakdown of the mechanical stresses in a thin-walled cylindrical vessel under internal pressure. It identifies the key variables—internal pressure (p), radius (r), wall thickness (t), longitudinal stress (σx), and circumferential stress (σφ)—and illustrates the free-body diagrams necessary for deriving the standard thin-walled vessel formulas where σφ = 2σx.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7b026fcfc3bb4dcf15d90b692b7902eb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Longitudinal Force Equilibrium for Thin-Walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Longitudinal Force Equilibrium for Thin-Walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Force balance in the axial direction of a thin-walled cylindrical pressure vessel under internal pressure p. Equates the wall's resisting force σ_x·A_ring to the pressure force p·πr². Applying the thin-wall approximation (t≪r, so πt²≈0) yields the boiler formula σ_x = pr/(2t), one of two principal stresses in the vessel wall.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Analysis Intermediate Result (Mohr's Circle Context) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Analysis Intermediate Result (Mohr's Circle Context) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A fragmentary computational result $(27,3) = \frac{11}{2}$ extracted from a German mechanics textbook section covering Mohr's stress circle and thin-walled pressure vessel formulas. The equation likely represents an intermediate calculation step or exercise solution within the stress transformation framework for a plane stress state with given values σx=50 MPa, σy=−20 MPa, τxy=30 MPa at φ=30°, or a related quantity from the boiler formula derivation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b625d3948531b3dcd9b53a158a3a5265</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 29 - Thin-walled Vessel Stress Formulas (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 29 - Thin-walled Vessel Stress Formulas (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 29 identifies the page containing key results for thin-walled pressure vessel analysis in section 11.2.4. It marks where hoop stress derivation, the fundamental relationship σφ = 2·σx, Kesselformeln, principal stress identification, and external pressure applications are presented, completing the vessel stress analysis begun on page 28.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd415d5735a4c089d6c4d66fc2f63d48</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Source Attribution Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 Reference (footer)">
  <data key="d0">Source Attribution Footer - Technische Mechanik 2 Reference (footer)</data>
  <data key="d1">footer</data>
  <data key="d2">Bibliographic footer citing 'Technische Mechanik 2' by Gross, Hauger, Schröder, Wall (2017), Springer Vieweg, as the source for technical content on thin-walled vessels, Mohr's stress circle, and plane stress states. Provides academic provenance and reference traceability for engineering mechanics material.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6e3cdc6151a0814d04d2cc632b0969f9</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium equation for thin-walled spherical pressure vessel (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium equation for thin-walled spherical pressure vessel (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation expresses the force balance for a thin-walled spherical pressure vessel under internal pressure. The left side (2·σ_φ·Δl·t) represents wall resisting forces, while the right side (p·Δl·2r) represents pressure forces on the projected area. Simplifying yields σ_φ = pr/t, the standard formula for membrane stress in spherical shells, demonstrating uniform biaxial stress due to spherical symmetry.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b0bd1d5444d931cb0efa80cdff770977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="OCR-Corrupted Thin-Walled Pressure Vessel Formulas (Kesselformeln) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">OCR-Corrupted Thin-Walled Pressure Vessel Formulas (Kesselformeln) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A syntactically invalid expression caused by OCR corruption. Contextually, it is intended to represent the classic 'Kesselformeln' for thin-walled pressure vessels, which define the principal hoop stress ($\sigma_\varphi = p \cdot r / t$) and longitudinal stress ($\sigma_x = p \cdot r / 2t$) based on internal pressure and geometric equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Kesselformel Spannungsgleichgewicht (Boiler Formula Stress Equilibrium) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Kesselformel Spannungsgleichgewicht (Boiler Formula Stress Equilibrium) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A stress equilibrium equation for thin-walled pressure vessels, relating hoop and longitudinal stresses under internal pressure. The equation appears corrupted but likely represents the Kesselformeln demonstrating σ_φ = 2σ_x for cylindrical vessels or equal biaxial stress states for spherical vessels, derived from force balance in plane stress conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a9c3492ddaae5a544709ab9626c48a75</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Shear Stress in Thin-Walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Shear Stress in Thin-Walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Calculates the maximum shear stress (τ_max) in a thin-walled cylindrical pressure vessel under internal pressure p. Derived from Mohr's circle with principal stresses σ_φ = p·r/t and σ_x = p·r/(2t), yielding τ_max = p·r/(4t). Critical for applying the Tresca yield criterion in vessel failure analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Thin-walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel Stress State Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Thin-walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel Stress State Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the fundamental stress analysis of a thin-walled cylindrical vessel ('dünnwandiger zylindrischer Kessel') subjected to internal pressure '$p$'. Part (a) establishes the geometric context with radius '$r$' and identifies a differential surface element. Part (b) visualizes the resulting plane stress state on that element, identifying the principal stresses: longitudinal stress ('$\sigma_x$') and circumferential stress ('$\sigma_\varphi$'). This visualization supports the theoretical conclusion that for such vessels, the circumferential stress is twice the magnitude of the longitudinal stress ('$\sigma_\varphi = 2 \cdot \sigma_x$'), explaining why failure typically manifests as longitudinal cracking.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-887f545fd528c5ed1b7980ed83618c38</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Equilibrium Diagrams for Thin-Walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessels (image)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Equilibrium Diagrams for Thin-Walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessels (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">Technical illustrations depicting the free-body diagrams for a thin-walled cylindrical vessel under internal pressure. Diagram 'c' demonstrates the equilibrium for longitudinal stresses ($\\sigma_x$), and diagram 'd' shows the equilibrium for circumferential/hoop stresses ($\\sigma_\\varphi$). These diagrams provide the visual basis for the engineering formulas used to predict material failure in pressure vessels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-290ff749460960cc500499bfce8172f5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Spherical Thin-Walled Pressure Vessel Stress Analysis Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Spherical Thin-Walled Pressure Vessel Stress Analysis Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates a thin-walled spherical vessel subjected to internal pressure 'p' with a radius 'r'. It visually supports the derivation of stress formulas for boilers, highlighting the relationship between internal pressure, geometry, and the resulting stress state on an infinitesimal surface element (the green square).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-35913765c2d7101f6d284ecd039952ce</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Element Diagram for Spherical Thin-Walled Vessel (image)">
  <data key="d0">Stress Element Diagram for Spherical Thin-Walled Vessel (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the two-dimensional stress state of a surface element in a thin-walled spherical pressure vessel. It depicts the principal stresses—tangential stress ($\sigma_t$) and circumferential stress ($\sigma_\varphi$)—acting in perpendicular directions. The outward-pointing arrows indicate that the vessel is subjected to internal pressure causing tensile stresses, consistent with the equilibrium conditions discussed in the context of thin-walled boiler theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fb31546e781f11ba51b9575be2e9cb66</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Thin-Walled Spherical Vessel Stress Diagram (image)">
  <data key="d0">Thin-Walled Spherical Vessel Stress Diagram (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram provides a visual representation of the stress analysis for a thin-walled spherical vessel under internal pressure. It identifies key variables including internal pressure (p), wall thickness (t), and the resulting tangential stress (σt). The illustration supports the theoretical discussion on equilibrium conditions and boiler formulas (Kesselformeln) for calculating principal stresses in spherical containers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c8ec6927ab0bb507f573af8049b45847</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Kesselformel für Tangentialspannung (Vessel Formula for Tangential Stress) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Kesselformel für Tangentialspannung (Vessel Formula for Tangential Stress) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation derives the tangential stress σ_t in a thin-walled pressure vessel by equating the wall tensile force (σ_t·2πr·t) to the internal pressure force (p·πr²). The result σ_t = ½·p·r/t is a fundamental Kesselformel used in mechanical engineering for designing pressure vessels, boilers, and pipelines, establishing the relationship between internal pressure, vessel geometry, and resulting membrane stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-493acae899ca4eaaedf536afa97f481b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Boiler Formula for Spherical Pressure Vessel (Kesselformel - Kugelkessel) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Boiler Formula for Spherical Pressure Vessel (Kesselformel - Kugelkessel) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Derives the circumferential stress σ_φ = (1/2)·p·r/t in a thin-walled spherical vessel under internal pressure through vertical force equilibrium. The resulting stress is half that of a cylindrical vessel's hoop stress due to biaxial symmetry. This formula is central to pressure vessel design and relates to the broader discussion of equilibrium conditions and principal stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Thin-Walled Pressure Vessel Stress Equilibrium Calculation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Thin-Walled Pressure Vessel Stress Equilibrium Calculation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A numerical example or derivation step in thin-walled pressure vessel analysis, demonstrating force equilibrium relationships that yield the boiler formulas (Kesselformeln). The equation illustrates the 2:1 ratio between hoop stress (σ_φ) and longitudinal stress (σ_x), which is fundamental for designing cylindrical pressure vessels and predicting failure modes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e80e31b80a7c1439506d701b5bbac342</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Page 31 - Equilibrium Conditions Introduction (page_number)">
  <data key="d0">Page 31 - Equilibrium Conditions Introduction (page_number)</data>
  <data key="d1">page_number</data>
  <data key="d2">Page number 31 in a German mechanics textbook. This page transitions from thin-walled spherical vessel stress analysis to equilibrium conditions (Gleichgewichtsbedingungen), introducing stress tensor components, volumetric forces, and infinitesimal element analysis in plane stress states. Marks a key conceptual shift from application-specific formulas to general stress state theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ba563b25b03c792bf6e4d826fd0f062</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium Conditions for an Infinitesimal Plane Stress Element (image)">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium Conditions for an Infinitesimal Plane Stress Element (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A technical diagram showing an infinitesimal rectangular element in a plane stress state (ESZ). It illustrates the normal stresses ($\\sigma_x, \\sigma_y$), shear stresses ($\\tau_{xy}, \\tau_{yx}$), and body forces ($f_x, f_y$) acting on the element, including their first-order variations over distances $dx$ and $dy$. This visual aid is used to derive the differential equilibrium equations for solid mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="X-Direction Equilibrium Equation for Plane Stress Element (equation)">
  <data key="d0">X-Direction Equilibrium Equation for Plane Stress Element (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Force balance in x-direction for an infinitesimal element in plane stress state (ESZ). It equates the sum of normal stress forces, shear stress forces (with first-order Taylor expansions), and body forces to zero. When simplified, it yields the differential equilibrium equation ∂σ_x/∂x + ∂τ_yx/∂y + f_x = 0, one of the fundamental Cauchy equilibrium equations in 2D continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Numerical Evaluation of 2D Stress Equilibrium (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Numerical Evaluation of 2D Stress Equilibrium (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation represents a numerical finite difference calculation for 2D equilibrium conditions (Gleichgewichtsbedingungen) in a plane stress state. It evaluates discretized spatial derivatives of stresses (e.g., 0.02/0.02) and balances them against a volumetric force term (0.5m) to verify static equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Force Equilibrium Condition (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Force Equilibrium Condition (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">∑F_y = 0 is the static equilibrium equation in the y-direction, requiring that the sum of all vertical forces (surface stresses and body forces) on an infinitesimal element vanishes. In this context, it underpins the stress analysis of thin-walled spherical pressure vessels and the differential equilibrium conditions governing plane stress states (ESZ) in continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7fa545fe4bb1d6483b658ddec935e5f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
<node id="Thin-Walled Vessel Stress Calculation (Corrupted Expression) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Thin-Walled Vessel Stress Calculation (Corrupted Expression) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematically inconsistent chain of fractional equalities (6000/0.6 = ... = 0) appearing in the context of thin-walled spherical pressure vessel analysis. Likely corrupted or OCR-garbled, it probably represents equilibrium calculations relating internal pressure to membrane stresses, connecting to the lecture's discussion of stress states and Cauchy equilibrium conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784901716</data>
</node>
