<node id="Thin-Walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel">
  <data key="d0">Thin-Walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of pressure vessel with a wall thickness much smaller than its inner radius, subjected to internal pressure, whose stresses are analyzed using the boiler formula.&lt;SEP&gt;A cylindrical vessel designed to withstand internal pressure, where wall thickness t is much smaller than vessel radius r, justifying the thin-wall approximation and the neglect of radial stresses&lt;SEP&gt;A cylindrical vessel with rounded end caps designed to contain internal pressure, having a diameter of 2r and a thin wall, analyzed for mechanical stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-887f545fd528c5ed1b7980ed83618c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903832</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Pressure (p)">
  <data key="d0">Internal Pressure (p)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The pressure applied internally to the cylindrical pressure vessel, a key parameter in the stress formulas&lt;SEP&gt;The pressure radiating outward from the center of the wall inside the cylindrical vessel, represented by red arrows in Diagram A.&lt;SEP&gt;The radial pressure acting outward from the inner surface of the cylindrical vessel, which generates resultant forces on the projected areas of the vessel.&lt;SEP&gt;The pressure exerted on the interior surface of a pressure vessel, generating tensile stresses in the vessel wall</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-887f545fd528c5ed1b7980ed83618c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-290ff749460960cc500499bfce8172f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-493acae899ca4eaaedf536afa97f481b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903832</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Longitudinal Stress (σ_x)">
  <data key="d0">Longitudinal Stress (σ_x)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The stress acting along the vessel axis, equal to p·r/(2t), serving as the smaller principal stress&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Längsspannung, the tensile stress acting along the axis of the cylinder, induced by internal pressure and represented by horizontal arrows on the square element in Diagram B.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Längsspannungen, the axial stresses that develop within the vessel wall parallel to the central axis of the cylinder to resist the pressure acting on the end caps.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of stress formula related to thin-walled vessel theory, derived from the cited reference.&lt;SEP&gt;The longitudinal stress component in cylindrical vessels, equal to the tangential stress and acting as a principal stress when no shear stresses occur</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-887f545fd528c5ed1b7980ed83618c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-290ff749460960cc500499bfce8172f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61ee301f89076e345159fdaef391ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-493acae899ca4eaaedf536afa97f481b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903832</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Circumferential Stress (σ_φ)">
  <data key="d0">Circumferential Stress (σ_φ)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as hoop stress or Umfangsspannung, the tensile stress acting around the perimeter of the cylinder, induced by internal pressure and represented by vertical arrows on the square element in Diagram B.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as hoop stresses or Umfangsspannungen, the stresses acting across the cut surfaces of the vessel wall opposing the resultant force of internal pressure in the circumferential direction.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of stress formula related to thin-walled vessel theory, derived from the cited reference.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-887f545fd528c5ed1b7980ed83618c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-290ff749460960cc500499bfce8172f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61ee301f89076e345159fdaef391ab6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903832</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Square Element">
  <data key="d0">Square Element</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A small, light-green square element highlighted on the outer surface of the cylinder, oriented with sides parallel and perpendicular to the cylinder's central axis, used to visualize the stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-887f545fd528c5ed1b7980ed83618c38</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903832</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vessel Diameter (2r)">
  <data key="d0">Vessel Diameter (2r)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The diameter of the thin-walled cylindrical pressure vessel, denoted as '$2r$', indicated by a double-headed arrow in Diagram A.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-887f545fd528c5ed1b7980ed83618c38</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903832</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Central Axis">
  <data key="d0">Central Axis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The central longitudinal line of the cylinder, relative to which the square element is oriented.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-887f545fd528c5ed1b7980ed83618c38</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903832</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Vectors">
  <data key="d0">Stress Vectors</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An advanced engineering mechanics concept discussed in the technical content surrounding the citation.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Spannungsvektoren or traction vectors; act on cutting planes and characterize the three-dimensional stress state.&lt;SEP&gt;A continuum mechanics concept, also known as Spannungsvektor, discussed in the surrounding content of the academic document.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanical principle discussed in the technical content anchored to the textbook Technische Mechanik 2.&lt;SEP&gt;Four thick red arrows representing the stress vectors acting on the material element's faces, oriented perpendicular to the square.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be68185d5f9be3b57dbcc2d1f774fbb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98ff466f481e3ef48f03b583574686cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3eaae8681c23616569341d164404cba8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903832</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Tensors">
  <data key="d0">Stress Tensors</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An advanced engineering mechanics concept discussed in the technical content surrounding the citation.&lt;SEP&gt;A continuum mechanics concept, also known as Spannungstensor, involving tensor symmetry and moment equilibriums as mathematical formulations.&lt;SEP&gt;A technical mechanics concept discussed in the document, relating to the analysis of stress in materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Spannungstensor, an engineering mechanics concept involving tensor analysis of stress, discussed in the document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-be68185d5f9be3b57dbcc2d1f774fbb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ff4ce898bbbc110abe830de75521c6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dead11f22d1654614fa266e394b63218</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
