<node id="Thin-walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel">
  <data key="d0">Thin-walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A thin-walled cylindrical vessel subjected to internal pressure, whose theoretical examination includes fundamental assumptions such as plane stress condition, negligible radial stresses due to high radius-to-thickness ratio, and derivation of longitudinal stresses through force equilibrium.&lt;SEP&gt;A closed cylinder, also referred to as Dünnwandiger Kessel, with total diameter 2r, subjected to uniform internal pressure, analyzed for the biaxial stress state acting on its wall.&lt;SEP&gt;A pressure vessel (Kessel) with thin walls subjected to internal pressure, whose mechanical stress states are analyzed using longitudinal and circumferential stress components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-290ff749460960cc500499bfce8172f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfafff355a9da39ab811d6385cb83520&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b026fcfc3bb4dcf15d90b692b7902eb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Transformation Equations">
  <data key="d0">Stress Transformation Equations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress Transformation Equations are mathematical formulations used in continuum mechanics and 2D stress analysis to transform stress components between different coordinate systems. They are specifically used to express unknown stresses on an inclined plane in terms of known stresses on orthogonal planes and the angle of rotation. 

These equations are derived by analyzing the equilibrium conditions of normal and shear stresses acting on an infinitesimal triangular element cut from a thin plate (Scheibe). By utilizing trigonometric relations, particularly involving functions of twice the rotation angle (2φ), the equations govern how stress components transform under a rotation of the coordinate system. The general mathematical expressions for these transformations are sigma_n (or σ_ξ) = (sigma_x + sigma_y)/2 + ((sigma_x - sigma_y)/2)*cos(2phi) + tau_xy*sin(2phi) for normal stress, and tau_n (or τ_{ξη}) = -((sigma_x - sigma_y)/2)*sin(2phi) + tau_xy*cos(2phi) for shear stress.

Beyond basic coordinate rotation, Stress Transformation Equations relate directly to the properties of the stress tensor and its invariants. By substituting the trigonometric functions of the principal angle (such as sin(2φ*) and cos(2φ*)) into these equations, one can evaluate stress components on various planes to determine the principal stresses. Consequently, these equations are essential for finding the maximum and minimum normal stresses on an infinitesimal element and serve as the analytical foundation for constructing Mohr's circle in plane stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd1f71b01c1aa5c67c36a16a597748fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e57c82a12143d5b7fd3e7f96dc75f203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0cc3665d01054e69899dc58796aab4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82d26e50b1a6b9a189224f9805e4217e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0139ebf1ce9f9e12ac7fedf1b939a2f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65f35adb2f34cbe81a9755002339ad40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b625d3948531b3dcd9b53a158a3a5265&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903863</data>
