<node id="Stress State">
  <data key="d0">Stress State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The state of stress at a specific point in a material, which a single stress vector is insufficient to fully describe and requires a stress tensor for its complete characterization&lt;SEP&gt;The internal distribution of force per unit area within a body, initially described by a single vector but fully characterized by a tensor.&lt;SEP&gt;A condition of stress that is described through coordinate planes with double-index notation in the context of continuum mechanics.&lt;SEP&gt;The state of stress at a point, defined using three stress vectors on mutually perpendicular sectional planes.&lt;SEP&gt;Describes the state of stress at a point (Spannungszustand), a topic covered in the textbook</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-84aaf5f754cbb16f87120d0c3bf0cd2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-271a171e393b79c95418cb0645e75d72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ea2012f708d083bcf1f8357bfab292&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-677dd5182b714188875fefaff19c7680</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903915</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
