</node>
<node id="Stress Transformation">
  <data key="d0">Stress Transformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress Transformation is a fundamental concept and mathematical procedure in mechanics focused on analyzing and calculating stresses on differently oriented planes within a material. The process involves transforming stress components between different coordinate systems, typically achieved by rotating the coordinate system by a specific angle, such as φ. This allows for the determination of stresses on arbitrary cross-sections and is frequently analyzed within two-dimensional systems.

A primary objective of Stress Transformation is the mathematical derivation and calculation of principal stresses and principal angles. By decomposing arbitrary plane stress states into their principal components, engineers can isolate the critical maximum and minimum stresses necessary for safe design and structural analysis. This procedure relies on detailed mathematical formulas and is commonly discussed in technical mechanics as a foundational topic prior to evaluating equilibrium conditions.

The application and visualization of Stress Transformation are heavily supported by Mohr's Stress Circle methodology. This mechanical process is frequently illustrated through Mohr's Circle and technical engineering diagrams, providing a comprehensive graphical framework for understanding how stress components vary with plane orientation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3cdbbf7d68148fe88c3174f0ea8e4742&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1678f499f1d6db4c88e28c21ca3f7ac4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f7f241f746726b97e3dc567365e483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40562a8ce19d10e78736b201626c9b32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80068bee1bee4c2ec1a3ff3d5d6d4f71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ef0d1a935f39ab673c43461c36533f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a7d19da473048f7577c45363a2702e7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radius Of Mohr's Circle">
  <data key="d0">Radius Of Mohr's Circle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The squared radius ($r^2$) of Mohr's Circle, calculated using the equation $r^2 = \frac{1}{4} [(\sigma_x + \sigma_y)^2 - 4(\sigma_x \sigma_y - \tau_{xy}^2)]$.&lt;SEP&gt;The radius of Mohr's circle, calculated as approximately 46.1 MPa, representing the maximum shear stress magnitude for the given stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d6513413e0a80f005888472d94d385e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b625d3948531b3dcd9b53a158a3a5265</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Invariante">
  <data key="d0">Invariante</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Invariant values that remain constant regardless of the orientation of the coordinate system, which visually decompose the radius formula of Mohr's Circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d6513413e0a80f005888472d94d385e0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical framework used to observe the state of stress, which stress is proven to be independent of&lt;SEP&gt;A system of axes used to define the orientation of stresses; the invariant values remain constant regardless of its orientation.</data>
