<node id="Section 11.2.3">
  <data key="d0">Section 11.2.3</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A section of the document where the discussion transitions to Mohr's stress circle (Mohrscher Spannungskreis)&lt;SEP&gt;A section of a German engineering mechanics document covering Mohr's Stress Circle, including its construction methodology and applications for stress transformations and finding extreme values.&lt;SEP&gt;A section containing comparative analysis of stress states, in which Sonderfall 1 serves as a reference state&lt;SEP&gt;A section within the mechanics and engineering document specifically covering Mohrscher Spannungskreis (Mohr's Stress Circle).&lt;SEP&gt;A section covering Mohr's Circle for Stress, covered on pages 26-27 of the technical document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1678f499f1d6db4c88e28c21ca3f7ac4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f2990ab5263ad174ce6fec37fef1115&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd747436c8f7ae4c9f1ad317397dda30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfafff355a9da39ab811d6385cb83520</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coordinate System Rotation">
  <data key="d0">Coordinate System Rotation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The mechanical transformation under which the stress components are derived and calculated on page 13.&lt;SEP&gt;The rotation of the coordinate system by an angle φ, transforming stress components from the original (x, y) system to the rotated (ξ, η) system.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure involving the rotation of a coordinate system by an angle φ to determine stresses in arbitrary cross-sections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1678f499f1d6db4c88e28c21ca3f7ac4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b5475536a7fccb42b9f6f9e39d32b77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Arbitrary Cross-Sections">
  <data key="d0">Arbitrary Cross-Sections</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Sections within a material or disk for which stresses are determined using Mohr's Stress Circle and coordinate system rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1678f499f1d6db4c88e28c21ca3f7ac4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Angle 2φ">
  <data key="d0">Angle 2φ</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A geometric principle in Mohr's circle where physical angles of coordinate system rotation appear doubled (2φ) and with the opposite sign.&lt;SEP&gt;A factor of 60° arising from Mohr's circle geometry, where angles on the circle are doubled relative to physical space.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1678f499f1d6db4c88e28c21ca3f7ac4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1f8906173d951132fd77877e8868d53</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Transformation Equation">
  <data key="d0">Stress Transformation Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An equation derived from the equilibrium condition for an infinitesimal element, used to determine stress states on arbitrarily oriented planes.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical equation representing the coordinate transformation law for the normal stress component σ_ξ in a two-dimensional stress state when rotating from the (x, y) to the (ξ, η) coordinate system by angle φ&lt;SEP&gt;The general mathematical formula used to evaluate transformed normal stress at a specific angle.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard equations for transforming stresses in plane stress states, such as σ_ξ = (σ₁ + σ₂)/2 + (σ₁ - σ₂)/2 · cos(2φ), representing recognized continuum mechanics principles.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical formula σξ = (σx + σy)/2 + (σx - σy)/2·cos(2φ) + τxy·sin(2φ) used to compute the normal stress in the rotated ξ-direction.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation τ_{ξη} = -((σ_x - σ_y)/2)·sin(2φ) + τ_xy·cos(2φ) used to calculate shear stress components in a rotated coordinate system from original stress components and rotation angle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebe13321a6d5df3c82e760adc093a99c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a1ba292932d5ab22c0f62081869648c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1f8906173d951132fd77877e8868d53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93703d14a7c8d37c2c5e2aae854f6240</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sigma Xi (σ_ξ)">
  <data key="d0">Sigma Xi (σ_ξ)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Normal stress component in the rotated ξ-direction, calculated by the stress transformation equation&lt;SEP&gt;Normal stress acting on a plane whose normal is oriented at angle φ from the x-axis in the rotated coordinate system</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sigma X (σ_x)">
  <data key="d0">Sigma X (σ_x)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Normal stress component in the original x direction&lt;SEP&gt;Normal stress component in the original x-y coordinate system</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sigma Y (σ_y)">
  <data key="d0">Sigma Y (σ_y)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Normal stress component in the original y direction&lt;SEP&gt;Normal stress component in the original x-y coordinate system</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tau XY (τ_xy)">
  <data key="d0">Tau XY (τ_xy)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Shear stress component in the original coordinate system&lt;SEP&gt;Shear stress component in the original x-y coordinate system</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coordinate Transformation Law">
  <data key="d0">Coordinate Transformation Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Law governing how stress components transform under coordinate rotation in continuum mechanics</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensor Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Tensor Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical framework applied to mechanical stress states, enabling the complete characterization of internal force distributions in deformable bodies.&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical framework for analyzing second-rank tensors in 2D continuum mechanics</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second-rank Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Second-rank Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as Tensor 2. Stufe, a mathematical object representing physical quantities like stress that transform according to specific tensorial rules and are rotation-invariant.&lt;SEP&gt;Type of tensor that stress represents, transforming according to specific rules under coordinate rotation while representing the same physical stress state&lt;SEP&gt;Type of mathematical object (Tensor 2. Stufe) defined by the transformation behavior exhibited by stress components under coordinate rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb966affea49c8515afe80d893d4ba39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensor Transformation Rule">
  <data key="d0">Tensor Transformation Rule</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">General tensor transformation rule σ'_ij = a_ik · a_jl · σ_kl, where a_ij are direction cosines</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Direction Cosines">
  <data key="d0">Direction Cosines</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Coefficients a_ij used in the general tensor transformation rule</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Stress">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mean stress component (σ_x + σ_y)/2 that is invariant under rotation; when σ_x = σ_y and τ_xy = 0, normal stresses are equal in all directions and shear stresses vanish&lt;SEP&gt;Special case where σ_x = σ_y and τ_xy = 0, resulting in the equation reducing to σ_η = σ_x = σ_y, confirming rotation independence.&lt;SEP&gt;The mean stress component, represented by ½(σ_x + σ_y), which is invariant under coordinate rotation.&lt;SEP&gt;The arithmetic mean of normal stresses, σ_M = (σ_x + σ_y)/2, equal to the center of Mohr's Circle and the normal stress on the plane of maximum shear.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb966affea49c8515afe80d893d4ba39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcd5ad0575ad16303ca4601a26ea32f2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deviatoric Normal Stress">
  <data key="d0">Deviatoric Normal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress contribution (σ_x - σ_y)/2 · cos2φ that varies with rotation angle φ</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Analysis of maximum and minimum principal stresses, to which the stress tensor derivation is foundational.&lt;SEP&gt;Method for identifying maximum and minimum stresses and potential failure planes in materials and structures&lt;SEP&gt;An analytical process central to continuum mechanics and strength of materials, for which the normal stress transformation formula is foundational.&lt;SEP&gt;An analytical method focusing on two-dimensional stress states, serving as the broader topic of pages 18-20 in the textbook.&lt;SEP&gt;The analysis of principal stresses and principal stress directions σ1 and σ2, which is the subject of the surrounding context sections&lt;SEP&gt;The process of computing and analyzing principal stresses, which is fundamental to failure theories, structural design, and continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553477d678ccfb56c56a99c6a713a1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8735ecf72f07b4cd7b82e22f492ade68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6546507247b4802eb945e2a493cb040&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failure Criteria Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Failure Criteria Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Analysis method for determining material failure conditions using stress transformations&lt;SEP&gt;Analysis of material failure based on stress states, forming an application built upon principal stress and Mohr's circle theory</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-792f1aab7b01df3f4ae8fa499208a41a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903947</data>
