<node id="Failure Criteria">
  <data key="d0">Failure Criteria</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Subsequent analysis principles in material strength for which the plane stress state content on this page provides a foundation.&lt;SEP&gt;Criteria used in analyzing material failure, for which the stress transformation equations serve as a fundamental step.&lt;SEP&gt;Criteria analyzed in continuum mechanics that utilize the first stress invariant.&lt;SEP&gt;Material failure standards that are analyzed in different material orientations using the stress transformation equations.&lt;SEP&gt;General theoretical rules for predicting material failure, including von Mises and Tresca, whose theoretical basis is formed by stress analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Criteria such as von Mises and Tresca used in structural analysis and material strength assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Criteria (Versagen durch Rissbildung - failure through crack formation) used to assess material failure based on computed stress states from biaxial stress analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb966affea49c8515afe80d893d4ba39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1685984065ed0dcd54dea046d7d4f918&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b625d3948531b3dcd9b53a158a3a5265&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f852bec3a8e229cf7fc9b647545ae29</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903969</data>
