  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Pressure">
  <data key="d0">Internal Pressure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Internal pressure, commonly denoted as "p" and measured in Pascals [Pa], is a physical load condition applied internally against the inner walls of thin-walled pressure vessels. This loading condition is a fundamental parameter in the analysis of various vessel geometries, specifically both thin-walled cylindrical and spherical pressure vessels. Conceptually, the internal pressure acts uniformly in a radially outward direction against the interior surfaces of the vessel walls. In technical and mechanical diagrams, this outward force is frequently represented by a red symbol or red arrows pointing outward from the central axis of the structure.

From a mechanical and mathematical perspective, internal pressure acts upon the projected areas of the vessel to generate resultant forces. In cylindrical vessels, the pressure acts upon the internal circular cross-section with an area of πr². Similarly, in spherical vessels, it exerts pressure on the projected area of the spherical segment. This pressure distribution results in a total resultant force calculated as the internal pressure multiplied by the projected circular area (p·πr²). This resultant force functions as the critical driving force within the force equilibrium equations used to analyze the structural stability of the vessel.

Ultimately, internal pressure serves as a key variable in engineering mechanics and structural design. The outward force it generates dictates the calculation of localized stresses within the vessel walls. Specifically, the internal pressure "p" is utilized to determine the hoop, longitudinal, and tangential stresses experienced by the material. By analyzing these stress calculations, engineers can appropriately design and evaluate thin-walled vessels to safely withstand internal physical load conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-35913765c2d7101f6d284ecd039952ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb31546e781f11ba51b9575be2e9cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e80e31b80a7c1439506d701b5bbac342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ba563b25b03c792bf6e4d826fd0f062&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfafff355a9da39ab811d6385cb83520&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b026fcfc3bb4dcf15d90b692b7902eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e3cdc6151a0814d04d2cc632b0969f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bd1d5444d931cb0efa80cdff770977&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9c3492ddaae5a544709ab9626c48a75</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903990</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A state of stress where the stress vector is zero on a particular plane, relevant for analyzing infinitesimal triangular elements extracted from thin plates.&lt;SEP&gt;A two-dimensional state of stress in a continuum where stresses act only in a single plane&lt;SEP&gt;A two-dimensional stress condition under which the normal stress transformation formula applies.&lt;SEP&gt;A state of stress whose transformation is represented by Mohr's Circle.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress state (ebener Spannungszustand, ESZ) in continuum mechanics where stress in one dimension is zero, providing the framework within which Mohr's circle is typically applied.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-341774a38a3b4fd47c6656391af81dd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82d26e50b1a6b9a189224f9805e4217e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b625d3948531b3dcd9b53a158a3a5265</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903990</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
