<node id="Equation $\sigma_{\xi} + \sigma_{\eta} = \sigma_{x} + \sigma_{y}$">
  <data key="d0">Equation $\sigma_{\xi} + \sigma_{\eta} = \sigma_{x} + \sigma_{y}$</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematically represents the first invariant of the stress tensor under a two-dimensional coordinate rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903990</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="First Stress Invariant">
  <data key="d0">First Stress Invariant</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Often denoted as I_1, it defines the conservation of the sum of normal stresses, proving physical quantities like stress are tensors independent of rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903990</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trace Of A Second-Rank Tensor">
  <data key="d0">Trace Of A Second-Rank Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical property of tensors that is conserved under orthogonal transformations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903990</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Orthogonal Transformations">
  <data key="d0">Orthogonal Transformations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Transformations under which the trace of a second-rank tensor, such as the sum of normal stresses, remains conserved.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903990</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cauchy's Stress Transformation Rules">
  <data key="d0">Cauchy's Stress Transformation Rules</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Transformation equations that demonstrate how shear stress terms and trigonometric terms cancel out when rotated normal stresses are summed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903990</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cartesian Coordinate System (X, Y)">
  <data key="d0">Cartesian Coordinate System (X, Y)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The original coordinate system where the normal stress components $\sigma_{x}$ and $\sigma_{y}$ are defined.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Component Of Stress">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Component Of Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The average normal stress that acts as an intrinsic property of a material point, independent of the observer's perspective or coordinate axes orientation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Components $\sigma_{x}$ And $\sigma_{y}$">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Components $\sigma_{x}$ And $\sigma_{y}$</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress components in the original Cartesian coordinate system $(x, y)$.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Components $\sigma_{\xi}$ And $\sigma_{\eta}$">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Components $\sigma_{\xi}$ And $\sigma_{\eta}$</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress components in a new coordinate system $(\xi, \eta)$ that is rotated relative to the original system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New Coordinate System $(\xi, \eta)$">
  <data key="d0">New Coordinate System $(\xi, \eta)$</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A coordinate system that is rotated by an angle $\varphi$ relative to the original Cartesian system, containing the rotated normal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stress Terms ($\tau$)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress Terms ($\tau$)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Terms in the transformation equations that cancel each other out with trigonometric terms when the rotated normal stresses are summed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trigonometric Terms ($\cos 2\varphi, \sin 2\varphi$)">
  <data key="d0">Trigonometric Terms ($\cos 2\varphi, \sin 2\varphi$)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Terms in the transformation equations that cancel each other out with shear stress terms when the rotated normal stresses are summed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Point">
  <data key="d0">Material Point</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A point in a material for which the hydrostatic component of the stress state acts as an intrinsic property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30fd62b02c985c73748d5224a5c1f6aa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vessel Formula">
  <data key="d0">Vessel Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A fundamental formula σ_t = (1/2) · p · (r/t) in pressure vessel theory that relates tangential stress to internal pressure and vessel dimensions, derived from the force equilibrium condition for thin-walled pressure vessels</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-493acae899ca4eaaedf536afa97f481b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tangential Stress (σ_t)">
  <data key="d0">Tangential Stress (σ_t)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The tangential or meridional stress in a pressure vessel wall, derived as (1/2) · p · (r/t) from the equilibrium of tensile force and pressure force</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-493acae899ca4eaaedf536afa97f481b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hoop Stress (σ_φ)">
  <data key="d0">Hoop Stress (σ_φ)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The circumferential stress (Umfangsspannung) in a pressure vessel wall, equal to p·r/t, acting as the larger principal stress in the hoop direction&lt;SEP&gt;A stress component in cylindrical vessels equal to twice the tangential/longitudinal stress (σ_φ = 2·σ_x)</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-493acae899ca4eaaedf536afa97f481b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thin-walled Pressure Vessel">
  <data key="d0">Thin-walled Pressure Vessel</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Application context (dünnwandiger Kessel) discussed in the subsequent section where stress transformation analysis is particularly relevant.&lt;SEP&gt;A structural component type (dünnwandiger Kessel) discussed in section 11.2.4, where biaxial stress states govern the design and analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A pressure vessel characterized by wall thickness t much smaller than radius r (t &lt;&lt; r), where bending stresses in the thin wall are neglected under the membrane stress assumption&lt;SEP&gt;A type of vessel, also known as dünnwandiger Kessel, analyzed for internal pressure and resulting stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-493acae899ca4eaaedf536afa97f481b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e80e31b80a7c1439506d701b5bbac342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a1ba292932d5ab22c0f62081869648c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1f8906173d951132fd77877e8868d53</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cylindrical Vessel">
  <data key="d0">Cylindrical Vessel</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of pressure vessel mentioned for contrast, characterized by $\sigma_{\varphi} = 2 \cdot \sigma_x$, meaning its hoop stress is twice its longitudinal stress.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of thin-walled pressure vessel for which hoop stress σ_φ = p·r/t and longitudinal stress σ_x = p·r/(2t), yielding the relationship σ_φ = 2·σ_x.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of pressure vessel where the tangential/longitudinal stress equals half of the hoop stress (σ_φ = 2·σ_x)</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-493acae899ca4eaaedf536afa97f481b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bd1d5444d931cb0efa80cdff770977&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9c3492ddaae5a544709ab9626c48a75</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784903997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Membrane Stress Assumption">
