<node id="Maximum Shear Stress">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Shear Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Maximum Shear Stress is a critical mechanical concept and stress analysis metric representing the extremal value of shear stress at a given point within a material. Generally denoted as τ_max, it is mathematically defined as half the absolute difference between the principal stresses, expressed by the standard equation τ_max = |σ₁ - σ₂| / 2. 

This characteristic shear stress occurs at an angular orientation of 45 degrees (φ* ± 45°) relative to the principal directions or axes. It can be determined through plane stress analysis and stress transformation equations. Graphically, Maximum Shear Stress is represented on Mohr's Circle, corresponding to the highest and lowest points on the vertical τ-axis. At the specific angular orientation where maximum shear stress occurs, the normal stress on the element equals the hydrostatic stress, denoted as σ_M.

The specific value and behavior of Maximum Shear Stress vary depending on the stress state applied to the material. In the case of uniaxial tension, the maximum shear stress equals σ₀/2, a concept also referred to as "Hauptschubspannung," and occurs precisely at an angle of 45° (φ* = 45°) from the principal direction. In a pure shear stress state, it is identified at the uppermost point of Mohr's circle and equals τ₀. Conversely, in a hydrostatic stress state, the largest shear stress across any cutting plane equals zero, regardless of the orientation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-341774a38a3b4fd47c6656391af81dd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1626d6e3dffee746e5efebf5c0f44ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f7f241f746726b97e3dc567365e483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6db9821cc411ad75c459848cb9d08e4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74378ee7045b50cecf2c625c47685477&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80068bee1bee4c2ec1a3ff3d5d6d4f71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4ade0a9e41cfa09698117c999ef93d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d8ef0753f64ed72eb451c29b8108d08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada0944efa350d49f7642c228e06590&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93703d14a7c8d37c2c5e2aae854f6240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f29928f565c2e69cfd84b7a3af24ffe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcd5ad0575ad16303ca4601a26ea32f2</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904009</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pure Shear Stress State">
  <data key="d0">Pure Shear Stress State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A special case ('Sonderfall 2: Reiner Schubspannungszustand') of stress state applications covered beginning on Page 24, characterized by the stress matrix σ = [[0, τ₀], [τ₀, 0]].&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as 'Sonderfall 2: Reiner Schubspannungszustand,' a special loading condition where normal stresses are zero (σx = σy = 0) and only shear stresses of magnitude τ₀ act on planes perpendicular to the x and y axes.&lt;SEP&gt;Special case 2 (Sonderfall 2: Reiner Schubspannungszustand) where initial normal stresses are zero and the applied shear stress is τ0, resulting in Mohr's circle centered at the origin with radius equal to τ0&lt;SEP&gt;A stress state equivalent to equal tensile and compressive principal stresses acting on planes inclined at 45 degrees to the original shear planes.&lt;SEP&gt;A special case of stress state where only shear stresses are present, presented as a theoretical foundation before the worked example.&lt;SEP&gt;Sonderfall 2, a special case discussed within the Mohr's Stress Circle section.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a32ade397f323fb086e2f86bb63b1a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95ef86ae323cfba68764c2acb2d46785&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74378ee7045b50cecf2c625c47685477&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40562a8ce19d10e78736b201626c9b32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f2990ab5263ad174ce6fec37fef1115&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1daacc45ca5900ab841904f26a5061a7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904009</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
