</node>
<node id="Cross-Sectional Annular Area">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Sectional Annular Area</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The annular cross-section of the vessel wall with area 2πrt (circumference 2πr multiplied by wall thickness t), over which the hoop stress acts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904014</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wall Thickness">
  <data key="d0">Wall Thickness</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The thickness t of the vessel wall, which must be much smaller than the inner radius (t ≪ r) for the thin-wall approximation to apply.&lt;SEP&gt;The geometric parameter t representing the thickness of the vessel wall, shown in both transverse cross-sectional and longitudinal section views of the cylinder.&lt;SEP&gt;The thickness $t$ of the vessel wall, where $t \ll r$.&lt;SEP&gt;Denoted as t, it represents the thickness of the vessel wall.&lt;SEP&gt;The thickness (t) of the pressure vessel's wall, used as a parameter in boiler formulas.&lt;SEP&gt;The thickness t of the vessel wall, measured in meters [m], assumed to be much smaller than the sphere radius in thin-wall theory.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e80e31b80a7c1439506d701b5bbac342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b026fcfc3bb4dcf15d90b692b7902eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bd1d5444d931cb0efa80cdff770977&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904014</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sphere Radius">
  <data key="d0">Sphere Radius</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The radius r of the spherical vessel, measured in meters [m].</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904014</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Classical Boiler Formula">
  <data key="d0">Classical Boiler Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The formula σ_φ = (1/2)·p·r/t (Kesselformel) for spherical pressure vessels, obtained by solving the equilibrium equation for hoop stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904014</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cylindrical Case">
  <data key="d0">Cylindrical Case</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The case of a cylindrical pressure vessel, distinct from the spherical case, where the hoop stress σ_φ equals twice the longitudinal stress σ_x.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904014</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thin-Wall Theory">
  <data key="d0">Thin-Wall Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The assumption that wall thickness t is much smaller than radius r (t ≪ r) and that shear stresses are absent, under which the equilibrium equation is valid.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904014</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="External Pressure">
  <data key="d0">External Pressure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pressure applied externally to the vessel; the equilibrium equation is applicable to external pressure by reversing the sign of p.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904014</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pressure Vessel Design">
  <data key="d0">Pressure Vessel Design</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The specific engineering application to which the content transitions.&lt;SEP&gt;The engineering process for designing pressure vessels, which fundamentally relies on the boiler formula.&lt;SEP&gt;The engineering context in which stress analysis, Kesselformeln, Mohr's circle theory, and failure mechanics are applied&lt;SEP&gt;The engineering discipline or process where the 2:1 ratio between hoop and longitudinal stresses is fundamental.&lt;SEP&gt;An engineering discipline in which the spherical pressure vessel equilibrium equation and classical boiler formula are fundamental.&lt;SEP&gt;An application domain that utilizes the fundamental equilibrium condition for analyzing thin-walled spherical vessels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e80e31b80a7c1439506d701b5bbac342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7fa545fe4bb1d6483b658ddec935e5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfafff355a9da39ab811d6385cb83520&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
