<node id="Uniaxial Tension">
  <data key="d0">Uniaxial Tension</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Uniaxial Tension, also referred to in German as "einachsiger Zug" or designated as "Sonderfall 1" (Special Case 1), is a specific loading condition and fundamental stress state in continuum mechanics and the strength of materials. In this state, an applied normal tensile stress acts exclusively along a single axis. As a result, the principal stress in one direction is nonzero, while the principal stress in the perpendicular direction is zero.

Due to its fundamental nature, Uniaxial Tension serves as a crucial theoretical foundation and reference state used to demonstrate broader stress state concepts. It is frequently analyzed as a special case within the context of Mohr's Stress Circle. For example, in specific instructional contexts, this loading condition is introduced as a theoretical baseline before progressing to worked examples, such as on Page 24 of a referenced text. Within the framework of Mohr's Circle, Uniaxial Tension is particularly valuable for illustrating the depicted stress states and demonstrating its analytical relationship with pure shear.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a32ade397f323fb086e2f86bb63b1a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e0e1d2a168869746758dfc06077ca50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b44241248f288278ba7f5d8fa8160e3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b2a42c115ed62e72e715daa89e2370&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f7f241f746726b97e3dc567365e483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f2990ab5263ad174ce6fec37fef1115&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1daacc45ca5900ab841904f26a5061a7</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sign Conventions">
  <data key="d0">Sign Conventions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rules used in stress analysis for determining the positive or negative direction of stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;Conventions used for determining the positive or negative directions of stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;The rules establishing that positive stresses on positive faces point in positive coordinate directions.&lt;SEP&gt;Definitions and rules presented in the main text of the academic document that are authenticated by the source literature.&lt;SEP&gt;Mechanical conventions that define the positive and negative directions for stresses, summarized in the document's summary section.&lt;SEP&gt;Standardized rules used in plane stress analysis to determine the positive or negative direction of stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6db9821cc411ad75c459848cb9d08e4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4ade0a9e41cfa09698117c999ef93d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0f20e6bdef6f836f08f3407dd794183</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normalspannungen">
  <data key="d0">Normalspannungen</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The German language term for normal stresses used in the continuum mechanics content.&lt;SEP&gt;Normal stresses in the mechanics of materials. A key theoretical point noted in the surrounding material states that normal stresses generally do NOT vanish at sections of extremal shear stress ('In den Schnitten extremaler Schubspannungen verschwinden die Normalspannungen im allgemeinen nicht!').</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5a5bec24cc4d96b66479607814f58f26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada0944efa350d49f7642c228e06590</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sonderfall 1">
  <data key="d0">Sonderfall 1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A labeled special case in the Mohr's stress circle analysis framework corresponding specifically to the uniaxial tension stress state.&lt;SEP&gt;Special Case 1: Einachsiger Zug (Uniaxial Tension), a special case in Mohr's stress circle analysis representing a uniaxial stress state where only σ_x (or σ_1) is non-zero, reducing the stress tensor to σ = [[σ_1, 0], [0, 0]]&lt;SEP&gt;The designation for the uniaxial tension stress state within the Mohr's Circle analysis framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d8ef0753f64ed72eb451c29b8108d08</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ_0">
  <data key="d0">σ_0</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable representing the magnitude of the applied tensile stress in the uniaxial tension stress state, determining the center position (σ_0/2, 0) and radius (R = σ_0/2) of the corresponding Mohr's circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stresses determined via Mohr's circle construction and eigenvalue analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum and minimum normal stresses evaluated within the context of specific stress components in an engineering formula.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum and minimum normal stresses at a point, referred to as 'Hauptspannungen' in the surrounding text.&lt;SEP&gt;The eigenvalues resulting from stress transformation analysis, where σ_1 represents the maximum principal stress and σ_2 equals zero in the uniaxial tension case.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum and minimum normal stresses in a stress state; for uniaxial tension, the principal stresses are σ_1 = σ_0 and σ_2 = 0, with the x-y coordinate axes aligned with the principal stress directions.&lt;SEP&gt;The extremal values of normal stress (σ₁ and σ₂) occurring on planes where shear stress vanishes, oriented at the principal angle.&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental component of stress analysis that can be traced through primary literature</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1626d6e3dffee746e5efebf5c0f44ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6ea2012f708d083bcf1f8357bfab292&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0cc3665d01054e69899dc58796aab4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95309ae773a0c5fe9703868d35942eff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f29928f565c2e69cfd84b7a3af24ffe</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Testing">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A material testing method that commonly encounters the uniaxial tension stress state, used to measure material properties under applied tensile loads.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structural Member">
  <data key="d0">Structural Member</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical components or elements in structures that are commonly subjected to uniaxial loading.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Transformation Principles">
  <data key="d0">Stress Transformation Principles</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fundamental mechanics concepts used to analyze stresses in different directions, which are demonstrated using Mohr's circle construction and the simplest non-trivial stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress-Shear Stress Plane">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress-Shear Stress Plane</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A coordinate plane used in Mohr's circle analysis where the stress state circle is plotted, centered at (σ_0/2, 0) with a radius of σ_0/2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea737003be49b184f13ec15eda6c870b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904029</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress Directions">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress Directions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The principal axes obtained by setting τ_ξη = 0 in the shear stress transformation equation, where the stress state becomes purely normal with no shear component.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Hauptrichtungen, these are two perpendicular directions of extremal normal stresses produced by the condition tan(2φ*) = 2τ_xy/(σ_x - σ_y) due to the π-periodicity of the tangent function.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as Hauptrichtungen, the two perpendicular directions in which normal stresses attain their extremal values, determined by tan(2φ*) = 2τ_xy/(σ_x−σ_y).&lt;SEP&gt;The directions of principal stresses from which the maximum shear stress occurs at an angle of 45 degrees.&lt;SEP&gt;The orientations of the principal stresses, which are discussed in the surrounding context of the document alongside the Mohr's circle construction.&lt;SEP&gt;Hauptrichtungen. The orientations of principal stresses, serving as a basis for subsequent stress transformation calculations at arbitrary cutting angles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e8a589d6ecd831cc0e1afc0b83e4bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553477d678ccfb56c56a99c6a713a1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93703d14a7c8d37c2c5e2aae854f6240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transformation Equations for Plane Stress">
  <data key="d0">Transformation Equations for Plane Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The key equations transforming stresses σ_ξ, σ_η, and τ_ξη as functions of the section angle φ, using double-angle trigonometric identities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="2D Stress Tensor">
  <data key="d0">2D Stress Tensor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The stress tensor whose eigenvalues are the principal stresses, with invariants I₁ (trace) and I₂ (determinant) remaining constant under coordinate rotation.&lt;SEP&gt;A two-dimensional stress tensor whose eigenvalues yield the principal stresses σ₁ and σ₂</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fca9235e4f945947ddf250020a6f05e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Invariant I₁">
  <data key="d0">Invariant I₁</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The first invariant of the 2D stress tensor equal to σ_x + σ_y (trace), remaining constant under coordinate rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Invariant I₂">
  <data key="d0">Invariant I₂</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The second invariant of the 2D stress tensor equal to σ_x·σ_y - τ_xy² (determinant), remaining constant under coordinate rotation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Characteristic Equation">
  <data key="d0">Characteristic Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation σ² - I₁σ + I₂ = 0 that yields the principal stress values σ₁,₂ = I₁/2 ± √(I₁²/4 - I₂).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section Angle φ">
  <data key="d0">Section Angle φ</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The angle parameter used in the transformation equations for plane stress, defining the orientation of the stress section.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress σ_ξ">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress σ_ξ</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A normal stress component defined as (σ_x + σ_y)/2 + (σ_x - σ_y)/2 · cos(2φ) + τ_xy · sin(2φ).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress σ_η">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress σ_η</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A normal stress component defined as (σ_x + σ_y)/2 - (σ_x - σ_y)/2 · cos(2φ) - τ_xy · sin(2φ).&lt;SEP&gt;The transformed normal stress evaluated at the principal angle, which yields one of the principal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebe13321a6d5df3c82e760adc093a99c</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stress τ_ξη">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress τ_ξη</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A shear stress component defined as -(σ_x - σ_y)/2 · sin(2φ) + τ_xy · cos(2φ).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress σ_x">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress σ_x</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress acting on the vertical face of the triangular element, contributing to the equilibrium equation.&lt;SEP&gt;A normal stress component in the original coordinate system, used in the transformation equations and invariant definitions.&lt;SEP&gt;Normal stress component in x-direction acting on the faces of an infinitesimal element, with spatial gradient ∂σ_x/∂x describing its variation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae7aec8cf21235bd79c851b9cc6978f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Critical Angle φ*">
  <data key="d0">Critical Angle φ*</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The section angle at which extremal normal stresses occur, defined by the condition tan(2φ*) = 2τ_xy/(σ_x - σ_y), yielding two perpendicular principal directions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Double-Angle Trigonometric Identities">
  <data key="d0">Double-Angle Trigonometric Identities</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical identities used to simplify the transformed shear stress equation into its classical form.&lt;SEP&gt;Trigonometric identities that give rise to the 1/2 factors appearing in the plane stress transformation equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0658f7561c12b0a53196517bdee01443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae7aec8cf21235bd79c851b9cc6978f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
