<node id="Transformed Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">Transformed Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A coordinate system resulting from a coordinate transformation, whose positive orientation may be indicated by the blue arrow icon.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-011c3fc433230965d7a734e4052f7689</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sonderfall 2">
  <data key="d0">Sonderfall 2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A special case in Mohr's stress circle analysis representing pure shear, where the stress state is σ = [[0, τ_0], [τ_0, 0]]&lt;SEP&gt;A special case of Mohr's stress circle representing pure shear stress state (reiner Schubspannungszustand), covered on page 24 of Technische Mechanik 2.&lt;SEP&gt;The designation for the pure shear stress state within the Mohr's Circle analysis framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9d212905be253e446a02b9ce4b7ba85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d8ef0753f64ed72eb451c29b8108d08</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sonderfall 3">
  <data key="d0">Sonderfall 3</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A special case in Mohr's stress circle analysis representing hydrostatic stress, where σ_1 equals σ_2&lt;SEP&gt;A special case of Mohr's stress circle representing hydrostatic stress state (hydrostatischer Spannungszustand), covered on page 24 of Technische Mechanik 2.&lt;SEP&gt;The specific designation given to the hydrostatic stress state within the Mohr's Circle analysis framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9d212905be253e446a02b9ce4b7ba85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d8ef0753f64ed72eb451c29b8108d08</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ_2">
  <data key="d0">σ_2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The second principal stress derived in the document.&lt;SEP&gt;The minimum principal stress (Minor principal stress) obtained from the eigenvalue decomposition of the stress tensor</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ_1">
  <data key="d0">σ_1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The first principal stress derived in the document, defined such that σ_1 is greater than σ_2.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum principal stress in the stress tensor, which in uniaxial tension is the only non-zero principal stress</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-788996859590fcaca4ffb066d7148714</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="τ_max">
  <data key="d0">τ_max</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The symbol representing maximum shear stresses acting tangentially along the faces of the material element, arranged in opposing pairs on adjacent faces to satisfy static equilibrium.&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum shear stress, equal to σ_1/2 in the uniaxial tension case, occurring at 45° to the principal directions&lt;SEP&gt;The maximum shear stress value indicated at the highest point of the circle's circumference, corresponding to the radius of the circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b2a42c115ed62e72e715daa89e2370&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012dfb2f1f77750672c0c98cb5c5f782</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="τ_0">
  <data key="d0">τ_0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The shear stress parameter appearing in the pure shear stress state (Sonderfall 2), where the stress tensor is σ = [[0, τ_0], [τ_0, 0]]</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="τ-axis">
  <data key="d0">τ-axis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The shear stress axis on the Mohr's circle diagram, which the Mohr's circle for uniaxial tension is tangent to at the origin</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c03dbd78277681c5ab7e7802efbca7b6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Angle">
  <data key="d0">Principal Angle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The angle φ* that defines the principal stress directions ('Hauptrichtungen'), found using the trigonometric relation tan(2φ*) = 2τ_xy / (σ_x - σ_y).&lt;SEP&gt;The angle φ* determined by the tangent equation tan(2φ*) = 2τxy/(σx - σy), defining the orientation of principal directions&lt;SEP&gt;The angle 2φ* determined via a specific mathematical relation involving stress components, used in stress transformation equations.&lt;SEP&gt;An angle, conventionally denoted as $\varphi^*$ or $\varphi^{**}$, at which normal or shear stresses are maximized, found by setting the derivative to zero.&lt;SEP&gt;The physical angle (φ*) between the x-axis and the direction of maximum/minimum principal stress, identified through Mohr's circle analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0cc3665d01054e69899dc58796aab4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Planes">
  <data key="d0">Principal Planes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The orthogonal planes separated by 90 degrees on which shear stress becomes zero and normal stresses reach their extremal values.&lt;SEP&gt;The specific planes corresponding to principal directions where shear stress is zero (shear-free)&lt;SEP&gt;Planes that experience only normal stresses without shear, established when the shear stress τ_ξη vanishes.&lt;SEP&gt;Planes where shear stress vanishes, contrasting with planes of extremal shear stress where normal stresses generally do not vanish.&lt;SEP&gt;The specific planes in a stress state where the shear stress is zero and the extreme normal stresses (principal stresses) act</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edcede9e6d28ea1431d1d8fe530b301d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="2D Stress Transformation">
  <data key="d0">2D Stress Transformation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical procedure in continuum mechanics used to determine how stress components change with coordinate rotation, specifically used to find the principal angle.&lt;SEP&gt;A key analytical process in continuum mechanics where principal stresses, conventionally denoted as sigma_1 and sigma_2, are calculated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da8ea347d2fe041d08bb85c59209fc9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b47aa4fc45ad067947c357da574f854</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as ebener Spannungszustand, this is the state of stress applied to the plate from which the triangular element is extracted.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress state (ebener Spannungszustand or ESZ) where stress components act on an infinitesimal element within a two-dimensional body.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as ESZ - Ebener Spannungszustand, representing the state of an infinitesimal element extracted from a plate or disc.&lt;SEP&gt;A state of stress, also referred to as ESZ (ebener Spannungszustand), analyzed in the context of stress tensor transformation and Mohr's stress circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7fa545fe4bb1d6483b658ddec935e5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae7aec8cf21235bd79c851b9cc6978f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="2D Cartesian Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">2D Cartesian Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A coordinate system with a horizontal x-axis pointing right and a vertical y-axis pointing upward, used as the reference frame for the stress diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Equations Of Equilibrium">
  <data key="d0">Differential Equations Of Equilibrium</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Governing partial differential equations derived by summing forces in x and y directions to zero for static equilibrium: ∂σ_x/∂x + ∂τ_yx/∂y + f_x = 0 and ∂τ_xy/∂x + ∂σ_y/∂y + f_y = 0.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dy">
  <data key="d0">Dy</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The infinitesimal height of the rectangular mechanical element, measured in the vertical y-direction of the 2D Cartesian coordinate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb64a0edb671186691ad9f74cd07ae9d</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sign Convention">
  <data key="d0">Sign Convention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A convention defining tensile normal stresses and positive shear stresses as those pointing in positive coordinate directions on positive faces of the volume element&lt;SEP&gt;A convention (Vorzeichenkonvention) where positive normal stresses acting on positive cut faces in the positive coordinate direction indicate tensile loading, while negative values indicate compressive loading.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific rule applied in the diagram where positive stresses act in positive coordinate directions on positive faces (faces whose outward normals point in the positive x or y directions).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36eb7e02adf94546ad8999d1636f68fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d97fb385691a70a5c314b2a77a107e6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sigma_0">
  <data key="d0">Sigma_0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Greek letter sigma with a subscript zero, representing the magnitude of the applied tensile normal stress in the x-direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b44241248f288278ba7f5d8fa8160e3e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Normal Stress">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Normal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stresses acting horizontally outward on the differential material element, represented by prominent red arrows originating from the left and right vertical faces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b44241248f288278ba7f5d8fa8160e3e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rectangle Cross-Section">
  <data key="d0">Rectangle Cross-Section</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A large rectangle representing a two-dimensional cross-section of a solid body or block, serving as the central element of the schematic diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Height Arrow h">
  <data key="d0">Height Arrow h</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A double-headed vertical arrow labeled 'h' indicating the total height of the object, located to the right of the block.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compressive Load">
  <data key="d0">Compressive Load</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A uniformly distributed compressive load or pressure applied to the top surface of the block, indicated by several downward-pointing arrows of equal length along the top edge.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Downward-Pointing Arrows">
  <data key="d0">Downward-Pointing Arrows</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Several arrows of equal length lining the top edge of the rectangle, indicating a uniformly distributed compressive load applied to the surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Diagonal Hatching Lines">
  <data key="d0">Diagonal Hatching Lines</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Lines featured on the bottom edge of the rectangle, serving as standard engineering notation for a fixed, rigid support or boundary condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-da2a8ad04e451820174ec4e36448c695</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cutting Direction">
  <data key="d0">Cutting Direction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The orientation of the cutting plane through a point, referred to as Schnittrichtung in German, on which the stress state depends.&lt;SEP&gt;Represented by the angle φ, it is the direction on which normal stresses and shear stress depend.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-85ca1064506b38c8fe493e3ffc533c71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="X-Direction Equilibrium Equation">
  <data key="d0">X-Direction Equilibrium Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical equation representing the force balance (ΣFx = 0) in x-direction for an infinitesimal rectangular element in plane stress state, derived from Newton's first law applied to a differential element of dimensions dx × dy with thickness t</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stress τ_yx">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress τ_yx</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The shear stress acting on the horizontal face of the triangular element, which is resolved into the ξ-direction.&lt;SEP&gt;Shear stress component on the y-face acting in x-direction, with spatial gradient ∂σ_yx/∂y&lt;SEP&gt;A surface force arising from shear stresses acting on element boundaries in the y-direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7fa545fe4bb1d6483b658ddec935e5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae7aec8cf21235bd79c851b9cc6978f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Volume Force Density f_x">
  <data key="d0">Volume Force Density f_x</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Body force density per unit volume in x-direction, such as weight, acting on the element volume dx·dy·t</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cauchy Equilibrium Equations">
  <data key="d0">Cauchy Equilibrium Equations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Set of two differential equilibrium equations in 2D continuum mechanics that govern the stress field&lt;SEP&gt;Mathematical equations representing equilibrium requirements where the net forces must vanish (e.g., ∂σx/∂x + ∂τyx/∂y + fx = 0).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Theory of Elasticity">
  <data key="d0">Theory of Elasticity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Framework within continuum mechanics for studying stress and strain in elastic bodies, including equilibrium conditions</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Taylor Expansion">
  <data key="d0">Taylor Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">First-order approximation technique used to represent stress field variation across an infinitesimal element</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boundary Value Problems">
  <data key="d0">Boundary Value Problems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Problems in solid mechanics solved using equilibrium equations and constitutive relations</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constitutive Relations">
  <data key="d0">Constitutive Relations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress-strain laws that, together with equilibrium equations, form the basis for solving boundary value problems in solid mechanics</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Equilibrium Equation">
  <data key="d0">Differential Equilibrium Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Simplified governing equation ∂σ_x/∂x + ∂τ_yx/∂y + f_x = 0 obtained by algebraically simplifying the x-direction force balance</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Rectangular Element">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Rectangular Element</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Differential element of dimensions dx × dy with thickness t extracted from a two-dimensional plate or disk, used as the basis for deriving the x-direction equilibrium equation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spatial Gradients Of Stress">
  <data key="d0">Spatial Gradients Of Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Spatial derivatives ∂σ_x/∂x and ∂σ_yx/∂y describing how stress components vary across the infinitesimal element, enabling Taylor expansion approximation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Governing Differential Equations Of Elasticity">
  <data key="d0">Governing Differential Equations Of Elasticity</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Derived governing equations that, together with y-direction counterpart and constitutive relations, form the basis for solving boundary value problems in solid mechanics</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plate Thickness">
  <data key="d0">Plate Thickness</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Thickness t (Dicke) of the plate or disk from which the infinitesimal element is extracted</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Element Dimensions">
  <data key="d0">Element Dimensions</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Infinitesimal element dimensions dx and dy defining the differential rectangular element used in the equilibrium analysis</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scheibe">
  <data key="d0">Scheibe</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A planar structural element (disc/plate) with small thickness relative to its side lengths, loaded only in-plane, used as the geometric model for defining the plane stress state.&lt;SEP&gt;A thin, rectangular solid body, also referred to as a plate or disk, shown in an isometric technical drawing.&lt;SEP&gt;An infinitesimal triangular element extracted from a plate under plane stress conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A two-dimensional stress element from which an infinitesimal triangular element is cut for analysis of stress equilibrium.&lt;SEP&gt;A German term for disk or plate, used in the text to refer to the differential material element represented by the square.&lt;SEP&gt;German term for disk or plate, the two-dimensional structural element from which the infinitesimal rectangular element is extracted for stress analysis</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e48d7926a99342f43a9d78825059455&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95ef86ae323cfba68764c2acb2d46785&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae7aec8cf21235bd79c851b9cc6978f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49e2cb25fd70f2b5f3b6c22629fe461a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1685984065ed0dcd54dea046d7d4f918&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20d2489764cb6b004e3ff06ba7ebe0c1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ₂=0">
  <data key="d0">σ₂=0</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The left intersection point of the Mohr's Circle with the σ-axis, located at the origin (0,0), representing a principal normal stress of zero.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30b2a42c115ed62e72e715daa89e2370</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ₁=σ₀">
  <data key="d0">σ₁=σ₀</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The right intersection point of the Mohr's Circle with the σ-axis, representing the nonzero principal normal stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30b2a42c115ed62e72e715daa89e2370</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal And Shear Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Normal And Shear Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Types of stresses for which transformation equations are discussed in the lecture content.&lt;SEP&gt;Stresses whose extremal values are examined as functions of the cutting angle φ in Section 11.2.2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3186598e4d31ef04b0826dc0b3dad17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f44965262ad3a19924f66261ac5694c5</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cutting Angle">
  <data key="d0">Cutting Angle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An angle, denoted as φ, that determines the extremal values of normal and shear stresses, discussed in Section 11.2.2.&lt;SEP&gt;The angle $\varphi$ with respect to which the derivative of the normal stress is taken and set to zero&lt;SEP&gt;An arbitrary angle φ, such as 30°, at which stress transformation calculations are performed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3186598e4d31ef04b0826dc0b3dad17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3553477d678ccfb56c56a99c6a713a1b</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress State (ESZ)">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress State (ESZ)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A two-dimensional stress state condition (ebener Spannungszustand) analyzed under Mohr's circle methodology, fundamental for analyzing stress in structural components.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress state (ebener Spannungszustand) in which stresses occur only in a plane, applicable to the thin-walled vessel wall where no shear stresses occur&lt;SEP&gt;A two-dimensional stress state in solid mechanics for which the mathematical equation mirrors a 2D equilibrium differential equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1f8906173d951132fd77877e8868d53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="2D Equilibrium Differential Equation">
  <data key="d0">2D Equilibrium Differential Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A differential equation of the form ∂σx/∂x + ∂τyx/∂y + fx = 0 that describes static equilibrium in a plane stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="dünnwandiger Kessel">
  <data key="d0">dünnwandiger Kessel</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A thin-walled spherical vessel whose structural integrity can be analyzed using discretized differential equations in engineering problems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Difference Quotient">
  <data key="d0">Difference Quotient</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A numerical technique used in the equation where fractions such as 0.02/0.02 represent discretized spatial gradients of normal and shear stresses (Δσ/Δx and Δτ/Δy).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Continuous Partial Derivatives">
  <data key="d0">Continuous Partial Derivatives</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Theoretical derivatives that are discretized into difference quotients to solve practical engineering problems involving differential equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f30cb257f67e16a0ea99ccc999878b4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904035</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mean Normal Stress">
  <data key="d0">Mean Normal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress component identified by the label σ_M = σ_0 / 2 in the stress diagram.&lt;SEP&gt;The mean value of normal stress, denoted as sigma subscript M, which equals zero for a pure shear stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98f7f241f746726b97e3dc567365e483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6efac359e95ae176e5b41c44f20b3bbc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904038</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Applied Axial Stress">
  <data key="d0">Applied Axial Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The original uniaxial tension denoted as σ_0 applied to the axis of the material element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98f7f241f746726b97e3dc567365e483</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904038</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Baseline">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Baseline</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A reference line located at the bottom left of the diagram, relative to which the material element is rotated by 45 degrees.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98f7f241f746726b97e3dc567365e483</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904038</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="45-Degree Plane">
  <data key="d0">45-Degree Plane</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Planes inclined at 45 degrees to the axis of uniaxial tension, on which the magnitude of the resulting normal stress equals the maximum shear stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98f7f241f746726b97e3dc567365e483</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904038</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
