<node id="Normal Stress Transformation Law">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Transformation Law</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical equation representing how normal stress σ_ξ in a rotated coordinate system relates to stress components (σ_x, σ_y, τ_xy) in the original reference frame, using rotation angle φ</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Stress Component">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Stress Component</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The mean stress component ½(σ_x + σ_y) that is invariant under coordinate rotation, representing the isotropic portion of the stress state</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deviatoric Stress Contribution">
  <data key="d0">Deviatoric Stress Contribution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The contribution ½(σ_x - σ_y)cos2φ that varies harmonically with rotation angle, encoding the directional dependence of normal stress differences&lt;SEP&gt;The portion of the stress transformation equation that varies with orientation, consisting of the terms involving cos(2φ*) and sin(2φ*).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Von Mises Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Von Mises Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A failure analysis criterion dependent on the full stress state of a component.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion that uses principal stresses to assess material yielding; central to structural design.&lt;SEP&gt;A material failure theory where stress transformation is applied for predicting yielding behavior&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion in continuum mechanics used for structural design, for which the principal stress analysis is foundational.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion used in strength assessment that relies on principal stresses to determine critical loading directions</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tresca Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Tresca Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A failure analysis criterion dependent on the full stress state of a component.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion that uses principal stresses to assess material yielding; central to structural design.&lt;SEP&gt;A material failure theory where stress transformation is applied for predicting yielding behavior&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion in continuum mechanics used for structural design, for which the principal stress analysis is foundational.&lt;SEP&gt;A failure criterion used in strength assessment that relies on principal stresses to determine critical loading directions</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr-Coulomb Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Mohr-Coulomb Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material failure theory where stress transformation is applied for predicting fracture behavior</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sigma 1 (σ_1)">
  <data key="d0">Sigma 1 (σ_1)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the principal stresses (Hauptspannungen) derived by extremizing the normal stress transformation equation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sigma 2 (σ_2)">
  <data key="d0">Sigma 2 (σ_2)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the principal stresses (Hauptspannungen) derived by extremizing the normal stress transformation equation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shear Stress On Rotated Plane (τ_ξη)">
  <data key="d0">Shear Stress On Rotated Plane (τ_ξη)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The shear stress in the rotated coordinate system, which vanishes (τ_ξη = 0) at the principal directions</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Failure Theories">
  <data key="d0">Material Failure Theories</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Theoretical frameworks for predicting material yielding and fracture, which utilize stress transformation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3780797c1d45f39538f2b68c98e007f0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Membrane Stress Formula">
  <data key="d0">Membrane Stress Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The classical formula for membrane stress in a thin-walled spherical pressure vessel, expressed as $\sigma_{\varphi} = (p \cdot r)/t$.&lt;SEP&gt;The equation (σ_t = σ_φ = p·r/(2t)) used to calculate membrane stresses in a spherical shell, where p is internal pressure, r is radius, and t is wall thickness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bd1d5444d931cb0efa80cdff770977</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress Formula">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The equation (σ₁,₂ = (σx + σy)/2 ± √((σx - σy)/2)² + τxy²) used to calculate principal stresses from stress components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gleichgewicht">
  <data key="d0">Gleichgewicht</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The equilibrium conditions for the spherical vessel where the internal pressure force must balance the circumferential or tangential stress resultant.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7eb83c1d892c505e546c1fe2cd585375</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cauchy's Second Law">
  <data key="d0">Cauchy's Second Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A law establishing the symmetry of the stress tensor, which reduces the nine components of the stress matrix to six independent ones.&lt;SEP&gt;The physical principle of angular momentum conservation which states that the stress tensor is symmetric.&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental principle dictating the symmetry of the stress tensor, meaning the shear stress components are equal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95309ae773a0c5fe9703868d35942eff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moment Equilibrium Condition">
  <data key="d0">Moment Equilibrium Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A condition (τ_xy = τ_yx, etc.) that reduces the nine tensor components to six independent ones via the symmetry of the stress tensor.&lt;SEP&gt;An algebraic equation representing the moment equilibrium about an axis parallel to the x-axis passing through the center of an infinitesimal cubic element.&lt;SEP&gt;A rotational equilibrium condition about coordinate axes through a body's centroid that establishes the symmetry property of the stress tensor</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf36ff85412f3dea295a176bbfb87119</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Finite Element Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Finite Element Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A computational method widely used in strength of materials and structural mechanics where the simplified single-index notation for normal stresses is applied.&lt;SEP&gt;A computational technique used for structural mechanics calculations where stress transformation is required.&lt;SEP&gt;A computational method where constitutive equations rely on the symmetric stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95309ae773a0c5fe9703868d35942eff</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensile Loading">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Loading</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of loading (Zug) indicated by positive normal stresses acting on positive cut faces in the positive coordinate direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compressive Loading">
  <data key="d0">Compressive Loading</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of loading (Druck) indicated by negative normal stress values according to the sign convention.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deformable Body">
  <data key="d0">Deformable Body</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A continuous material body subjected to external loads and surface loads, whose internal force distribution is characterized by the stress tensor σ_ij at any point.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical solid in continuum mechanics that undergoes deformation and contains internal stress states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7876e6fbf1af5b17253567ec9af4cf91</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cutting Plane">
  <data key="d0">Cutting Plane</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A conceptual plane (Schnittfläche) used to analyze internal forces, characterized by an outward normal vector.&lt;SEP&gt;An arbitrarily oriented plane used for determining stresses and traction vectors.&lt;SEP&gt;A plane whose specific rotation angle causes shear stresses to vanish and normal stresses to reach extreme values.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8be23ae96862171a1f9cb78cb1cfcb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Outward Normal Vector">
  <data key="d0">Outward Normal Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A vector denoted by the first index of the stress tensor component, representing the direction normal to the cutting plane.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensor Notation">
  <data key="d0">Tensor Notation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A rigorous notation using all nine components to represent the stress tensor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Engineering Notation">
  <data key="d0">Engineering Notation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A conventional notation that simplifies the double-index tensor notation into a single-index notation for normal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Symmetric Stress Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Symmetric Stress Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A 3×3 matrix representation of the stress tensor whose diagonal elements represent normal stresses and contains six independent components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c2c59e0f6a23f968e67f68fc9498278</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tau_0">
  <data key="d0">Tau_0</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical symbol representing the magnitude of the shear stress acting on the element.&lt;SEP&gt;The applied shear stress magnitude in the pure shear stress state, which determines the radius of Mohr's circle and the absolute values of the principal and maximum shear stresses</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-95ef86ae323cfba68764c2acb2d46785&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74378ee7045b50cecf2c625c47685477</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stationarity Condition Equation">
  <data key="d0">Stationarity Condition Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The derivative dσ_ξ/dφ set to zero, representing the stationarity (extremum) condition for the normal stress σ_ξ as a function of the rotation angle φ in a plane stress state, given by dσ_ξ/dφ = -(σ_x - σ_y)·sin(2φ) + 2τ_xy·cos(2φ) = 0.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Sigma Xi">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Sigma Xi</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress acting on the arbitrarily inclined plane in the xi direction&lt;SEP&gt;The transformed normal stress component acting in the xi-direction, whose general stress transformation equation is substituted into to derive the equation for sigma eta.&lt;SEP&gt;The transformed normal stress σ_ξ(φ) obtained via Mohr's stress transformation, expressed as σ_ξ = (σ_x + σ_y)/2 + (σ_x - σ_y)/2·cos(2φ) + τ_xy·sin(2φ).&lt;SEP&gt;A normal stress tensor component that the term 'O B C' in the equation is likely an OCR corruption of.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82d26e50b1a6b9a189224f9805e4217e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f29928f565c2e69cfd84b7a3af24ffe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f852bec3a8e229cf7fc9b647545ae29</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sigma X">
  <data key="d0">Sigma X</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress component acting on the original, unrotated Cartesian x-plane&lt;SEP&gt;The Cartesian normal stress component σ_x in the x-direction at a material point in plane stress state.&lt;SEP&gt;A normal stress component of 50 MPa (tensile) in the x-direction of the plane stress state</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sigma Y">
  <data key="d0">Sigma Y</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The normal stress component acting on the original, unrotated Cartesian y-plane&lt;SEP&gt;The Cartesian normal stress component σ_y in the y-direction at a material point in plane stress state.&lt;SEP&gt;A normal stress component of -20 MPa (compressive) in the y-direction of the plane stress state</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tau XY">
  <data key="d0">Tau XY</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The shear stress component acting on the x plane in the y-direction, equal to τ_yx due to the conjugate shear stress theorem&lt;SEP&gt;The shear stress component τ_xy at a material point in plane stress state.&lt;SEP&gt;A shear stress component of 30 MPa in the given plane stress state</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea236c2c090948676eebcd4986bd6df7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phi">
  <data key="d0">Phi</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The angle of rotation between the original x,y coordinate system and the rotated η,ξ coordinate system&lt;SEP&gt;The angle φ of the section plane (Schnittrichtung) measured from the x-axis, representing the rotation angle in stress transformation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf5a136f98019f0bd369d2b6d44879f8</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr's Stress Transformation">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Stress Transformation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical transformation used to obtain the transformed normal stress σ_ξ as a function of the rotation angle φ from Cartesian stress components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-97ae2381cfa6e993a16166b7495765c3</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
