<node id="Equation $\Sigma M^{(M)} = 0$">
  <data key="d0">Equation $\Sigma M^{(M)} = 0$</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation representing the principle of moment equilibrium, stating that the algebraic sum of all moments acting on a body evaluated with respect to a specific reference point M must equal zero.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Point M">
  <data key="d0">Point M</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The center point of the infinitesimal quader where the state of stress is being analyzed, marked with a small circle in the diagram&lt;SEP&gt;The center point of the material element, where the origin of the local coordinate system coincides.&lt;SEP&gt;The geometric center of an infinitesimal volume element, used as the specific reference point for evaluating the moment equilibrium.&lt;SEP&gt;The center of Mohr's Circle located on the sigma-axis, representing the average normal stress.&lt;SEP&gt;The center point of Mohr's Circle located on the horizontal σ-axis, from which radii and angles are measured.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb64a0edb671186691ad9f74cd07ae9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb9e40b1a13627933f97ce043e59f768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ca440e5727deb504109d3cda9b3642d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b23912fb95dffaea41a466afd896e511</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infinitesimal Volume Element">
  <data key="d0">Infinitesimal Volume Element</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A light-grey cube representing an infinitesimal rectangular portion of a material with side lengths dx, dy, and dz, used to visualize stress components at a point within a solid body&lt;SEP&gt;A small mathematical object used to analyze mechanical states such as tension and compression caused by normal stresses.&lt;SEP&gt;A small material element about whose center point the moment equilibrium is formed.&lt;SEP&gt;An infinitesimally small geometric body (infinitesimaler Quader) used in continuum mechanics to analyze the equilibrium of opposing shear stresses on its parallel faces.&lt;SEP&gt;A small volume element used to derive moment equilibrium conditions by analyzing axes through its center point.&lt;SEP&gt;A small volume element used in continuum mechanics to apply the angular momentum balance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60e545266c1919041c313cfba39a94c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012508b359d5f14b7a687dd269f97c12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95309ae773a0c5fe9703868d35942eff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d97fb385691a70a5c314b2a77a107e6</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Static Rotational Equilibrium">
  <data key="d0">Static Rotational Equilibrium</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The physical state of a body where the sum of all acting moments is zero, preventing rotational acceleration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross Shear Stresses">
  <data key="d0">Cross Shear Stresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pairs of shear stresses, such as $\tau_{xy}$ and $\tau_{yx}$, that are proven to be equal as a direct consequence of the moment equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Classical Mechanics">
  <data key="d0">Classical Mechanics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A branch of mechanics within which the principle of moment equilibrium is specifically applied.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Symmetric Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Symmetric Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical matrix property where the matrix equals its transpose, a property enforced on the stress tensor by the fundamental equilibrium condition.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical representation used for the Cauchy stress tensor, exploiting its symmetry property to reduce independent components to six</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d90be9fb874d21dec7e04ccacce0f39e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd290e24d36ba227108f0e9b5c1965e</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trigonometric Relationships">
  <data key="d0">Trigonometric Relationships</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical relationships used for stress analysis and derivations of principal stresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-83ac1dafe6c2621a4fb3a412b3493553</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Schematic Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Schematic Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A technical schematic diagram labeled with a blue letter 'c', illustrating a two-dimensional state of stress on a differential material element rotated counter-clockwise by angle φ** relative to a horizontal reference line.&lt;SEP&gt;An image displaying a two-dimensional material element subjected to a specific state of stress, with red arrows representing stress vectors acting perpendicular to the element's faces, illustrating a hydrostatic stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98ff466f481e3ef48f03b583574686cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012dfb2f1f77750672c0c98cb5c5f782</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohr's Circle Of Stress">
  <data key="d0">Mohr's Circle Of Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The English translation of 'Mohrscher Spannungskreis', representing a graphical method for stress analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Known as "Mohrscher Spannungskreis" in German, a graphical method for analyzing stress states; in the hydrostatic case, the Mohr's circle degenerates into a single point on the normal stress axis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98ff466f481e3ef48f03b583574686cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dbabf4bbc38075b362efc1cf68c92ba</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sigma 0">
  <data key="d0">Sigma 0</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A red Greek symbol ($\\sigma_0$) labeling the stress vectors, representing the equal magnitude of normal stresses in all directions ($\\sigma_x = \\sigma_y = \\sigma_0$).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98ff466f481e3ef48f03b583574686cc</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Stress Transformation Formula">
  <data key="d0">Normal Stress Transformation Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical equation representing the normal stress transformation for plane stress conditions, evaluated at the principal angle φ* where extremal stress values occur. It expresses the normal stress σ_ξ acting on a plane oriented at angle φ* relative to the original x-axis coordinate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ₁">
  <data key="d0">σ₁</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The maximum principal stress, representing the maximum normal stress at a point, derived from the normal stress transformation formula with appropriate substitutions.&lt;SEP&gt;The larger extremal principal normal stress, with σ₁ greater than σ₂.&lt;SEP&gt;The first principal stress in the principal axis system used in two-dimensional stress analysis</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd747436c8f7ae4c9f1ad317397dda30</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ₂">
  <data key="d0">σ₂</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The minimum principal stress, representing the minimum normal stress at a point, derived from the normal stress transformation formula with appropriate substitutions.&lt;SEP&gt;The smaller extremal principal normal stress, with σ₁ greater than σ₂.&lt;SEP&gt;The second principal stress in the principal axis system used in two-dimensional stress analysis</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6643a9ec6dd9b81db5b4233bf1899ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd747436c8f7ae4c9f1ad317397dda30</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">Principal Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as the Hauptachsensystem, it is the coordinate system in which the stress tensor becomes diagonal and principal planes experience only normal stresses without shear.&lt;SEP&gt;Also known as the principal axis system, a coordinate system where the axes are orthogonal and aligned perfectly with the sides of the material element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8be23ae96862171a1f9cb78cb1cfcb0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Beams">
  <data key="d0">Beams</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical structural components analyzed using stress transformation equations and failure criteria within structural analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Engineering components for which determining critical stress states using the normal stress transformation equation is foundational.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shafts">
  <data key="d0">Shafts</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Engineering components for which determining critical stress states using the normal stress transformation equation is foundational.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pressure Vessels">
  <data key="d0">Pressure Vessels</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Engineering components for which determining critical stress states using the normal stress transformation equation is foundational.&lt;SEP&gt;Containers designed to hold gases or liquids at a pressure substantially different from the ambient pressure, whose design and analysis utilize the thin-walled cylinder stress formulas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e9d228101e892c4b9b284f5228fa25ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="1000 = 1000 Equation">
  <data key="d0">1000 = 1000 Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A trivial tautological identity that is mathematically self-evident and holds for all values, likely a typographical artifact, OCR misread, or placeholder in the context of continuum mechanics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cauchy Second Law Of Motion">
  <data key="d0">Cauchy Second Law Of Motion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as angular momentum conservation; derived from moment equilibrium conditions about axes through the center of an infinitesimal volume element, mandating that the stress tensor must be symmetric (σ_ij = σ_ji).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Symmetry Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Symmetry Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Conditions τ_xy = τ_yx, τ_yz = τ_zy, and τ_xz = τ_zx that reduce the nine stress components to six independent entries, uniquely defining the complete stress state at a point.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="OCR">
  <data key="d0">OCR</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Optical character recognition, which is suggested as a possible cause for the tautological '1000 = 1000' equation artifact.&lt;SEP&gt;Optical Character Recognition technology that produced a garbled scan of the fundamental trigonometric condition, misreading 2φ and π as '2' and '10'</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2839b8d3cca96c984fc98ff0599ca3a1</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moment Equilibrium Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Moment Equilibrium Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Conditions that establish relationships between shear stress components in a stress state.&lt;SEP&gt;Equilibrium conditions about coordinate axes through point P, used to derive the symmetry of shear stresses and establish the symmetry property of the Cauchy stress tensor.&lt;SEP&gt;Conditions derived from the moment equilibrium about axes parallel to the coordinate axes through the center point of an infinitesimal volume element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cabab253ce4ee75c98c51cfd7f1a27b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffd7059b21365543021b6e1a5ac8087</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coordinate Axes">
  <data key="d0">Coordinate Axes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference axes used to define directions for stresses, cutting planes, and moment equilibrium in the three-dimensional stress state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5e628a4266eeb0409d1d0001367be687</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sigma (σ)">
  <data key="d0">Sigma (σ)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Greek letter sigma used as the label for the horizontal axis in the stress diagram, denoting normal stress, with arrowheads pointing in the positive direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2662124ebaa998fdaa13ffecb3add380</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tau (τ)">
  <data key="d0">Tau (τ)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Greek letter tau used as the label for the vertical axis in the stress diagram, denoting shear stress, with arrowheads pointing in the positive direction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2662124ebaa998fdaa13ffecb3add380</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="σ₀">
  <data key="d0">σ₀</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific non-zero normal stress value plotted at coordinates (σ₀, 0) on the stress diagram, represented by a small open circle and labeled in red text for emphasis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2662124ebaa998fdaa13ffecb3add380</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Point (σ0, 0)">
  <data key="d0">Point (σ0, 0)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A single data point plotted on the positive side of the horizontal axis, represented by a small open circle and labeled σ0 in red text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2662124ebaa998fdaa13ffecb3add380</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sign Convention (Vorzeichenkonvention)">
  <data key="d0">Sign Convention (Vorzeichenkonvention)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A convention stating that positive stresses on positive (negative) cut faces point in the positive (negative) coordinate direction, where positive normal stresses cause tension and negative normal stresses cause compression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-60e545266c1919041c313cfba39a94c4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tension (Zug)">
  <data key="d0">Tension (Zug)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mechanical state caused by positive normal stresses on an infinitesimal volume element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-60e545266c1919041c313cfba39a94c4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compression (Druck)">
  <data key="d0">Compression (Druck)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mechanical state caused by negative normal stresses on an infinitesimal volume element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-60e545266c1919041c313cfba39a94c4</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
