<node id="Longitudinal Stress">
  <data key="d0">Longitudinal Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Longitudinal Stress, also referred to as axial stress or Längsspannung, is a fundamental principal stress component that acts upon the walls of a cylindrical pressure vessel. Denoted mathematically as σ_x and typically measured in megapascals (MPa), this stress acts parallel to the longitudinal axis of the cylinder. Its primary mechanical function is to resist the internal pressure that would otherwise cause the vessel's end caps to blow off.

In the context of thin-walled pressure vessel analysis, longitudinal stress exists within a biaxial (plane) stress state where no shear stress is present. According to established boiler formulas, the magnitude of the longitudinal stress in a cylindrical geometry is exactly half that of the hoop stress. This axial stress component is calculated using the formula σ_x = p·r/(2t), where 'p' represents the internal pressure, 'r' is the radius of the vessel, and 't' is the wall thickness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73adfabedf760785acfd7b799786f369&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e80e31b80a7c1439506d701b5bbac342&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b026fcfc3bb4dcf15d90b692b7902eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b625d3948531b3dcd9b53a158a3a5265&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bd1d5444d931cb0efa80cdff770977&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecddc3be14bd893c4f22755acfb1f21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9c3492ddaae5a544709ab9626c48a75</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tensor Rotation">
  <data key="d0">Tensor Rotation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical operation connecting the x,y-system to a rotated η,ξ-system to transform stress components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thin Plate">
  <data key="d0">Thin Plate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A structural element whose thickness is small compared to its lateral dimensions, also referred to as a 'Scheibe'.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical object, also referred to as a Scheibe, from which an infinitesimal triangular element is extracted for stress analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A thin plate, referred to as Scheibe in German, from which an infinitesimal triangular element is cut for stress transformation analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a2f298ccb33e1f76a031a29e81e1f9dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f056013d274101fd8fe012cf408b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d77ac531ca72db1dfb705ad40ac98c3f</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
