<node id="Rotated Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">Rotated Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A coordinate system rotated relative to the Cartesian system, labeled as ξ, η.&lt;SEP&gt;A secondary Cartesian coordinate system defined by the 'eta' axis (normal to the inclined hypotenuse) and the 'xi' axis (tangential to the hypotenuse).&lt;SEP&gt;A coordinate system, denoted as (ξ, η), that is rotated by a specific angle φ relative to the original (x, y) axes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6589bf554e5066ac0fa2960ba8d673c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c2cf3a337f73c88af5880c2708e9c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65f35adb2f34cbe81a9755002339ad40</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chain Rule">
  <data key="d0">Chain Rule</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical rule utilized during the derivation of the shear stress extremization equation to take the derivative with respect to the rotation angle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6589bf554e5066ac0fa2960ba8d673c0</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plane Stress States">
  <data key="d0">Plane Stress States</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Also known as "Ebener Spannungszustand," a technical mechanics concept discussed in the document describing a two-dimensional state of stress.&lt;SEP&gt;A topic covered within Chapter 11.2.1 'Koordinatentransformation', involving stress transformation equations and the analysis of stress components under rotation.&lt;SEP&gt;Referred to as "ebener Spannungszustand" in German; a stress analysis topic involving coordinate transformation, covered in the textbook and on page 12 of the source document.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific state of stress in continuum mechanics that the surrounding theoretical material analyzes using Mohr's circle construction.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress state condition analyzed using Mohr's circle construction and stress transformation equations.&lt;SEP&gt;A technical mechanics topic discussed in the instructional material, covering stress states in a plane, sourced from "Technische Mechanik 2".&lt;SEP&gt;A concept in mechanics describing a state of stress in two dimensions, which is analyzed using Mohr's stress circle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0139ebf1ce9f9e12ac7fedf1b939a2f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b5475536a7fccb42b9f6f9e39d32b77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165c1196600ad5f85ab89d1be16e9bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dead11f22d1654614fa266e394b63218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac4ea6191b5ef268551e8e517a153c48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93703d14a7c8d37c2c5e2aae854f6240</data>
