  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Force Equilibrium In X-direction">
  <data key="d0">Force Equilibrium In X-direction</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The method of establishing force equilibrium in the x-direction using the transverse cross-sectional view in panel c, involving the longitudinal stress σx.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7b026fcfc3bb4dcf15d90b692b7902eb</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coefficient Function a">
  <data key="d0">Coefficient Function a</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material or geometric coefficient defined on a two-dimensional domain, evaluated at specific coordinate points in stress analysis computations, mapping the origin to itself.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Origin (0,0)">
  <data key="d0">Origin (0,0)</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The origin of a two-dimensional coordinate system, serving as a reference point in the principal axis system where principal stress directions are defined, also functioning as the zero vector in scalar multiplication.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rational Coefficient 11/12">
  <data key="d0">Rational Coefficient 11/12</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific numerical value derived from intermediate calculations, possibly related to interpolation functions, shape functions, or stiffness matrix entries, used as a scalar multiplier in the equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress σ1">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress σ1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A principal stress direction aligned with the 1-axis of the principal axis system (Hauptachsensystem).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Stress σ2">
  <data key="d0">Principal Stress σ2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A principal stress direction aligned with the 2-axis of the principal axis system (Hauptachsensystem).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Finite Element Computation">
  <data key="d0">Finite Element Computation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A numerical computation method used in stress analysis for evaluating material or geometric coefficients on two-dimensional domains, from which the equation likely arises.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Two-Dimensional Coordinate System">
  <data key="d0">Two-Dimensional Coordinate System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A coordinate system having two dimensions, within which the origin $(0,0)$ is located and the function $a$ is defined.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zero Vector">
  <data key="d0">Zero Vector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The vector $(0,0)$ in a two-dimensional coordinate system, which yields the zero vector when multiplied by any scalar, reducing the equation to a trivial identity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interpolation Functions">
  <data key="d0">Interpolation Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical functions potentially related to the specific numerical value of the fraction $\frac{11}{12}$ derived from intermediate calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shape Functions">
  <data key="d0">Shape Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Functions used in finite element analysis that may be related to the derivation of the rational coefficient $\frac{11}{12}$.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stiffness Matrix Entries">
  <data key="d0">Stiffness Matrix Entries</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Components of a stiffness matrix in numerical computations, with which the specific numerical value $\frac{11}{12}$ might be associated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Transformation Framework">
  <data key="d0">Stress Transformation Framework</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A framework in which the coefficient evaluating to zero may indicate a vanishing contribution, a boundary condition, or a normalization requirement.&lt;SEP&gt;A conceptual and pedagogical framework utilizing general stress transformation equations to compute normal and shear stresses at specific angles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c4df6ac3e36adc464a8f9e47b557ec07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b625d3948531b3dcd9b53a158a3a5265</data>
  <data key="d4">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1784904052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Syllabus">
  <data key="d0">Syllabus</data>
