<edge source="Stress Design Criterion (|σ| ≤ σ_zul)" target="Normal Stress (σ)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The stress design criterion requires that the absolute value of normal stress σ does not exceed the allowable stress σ_zul.</data>
  <data key="d9">constraint,inequality</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897042</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Stress Design Criterion (|σ| ≤ σ_zul)" target="Allowable Stress (σ_zul)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The stress design criterion uses σ_zul as the upper bound limit for the absolute value of normal stress.</data>
  <data key="d9">constraint,limit</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897042</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Stress Design Criterion (|σ| ≤ σ_zul)" target="Tensile Stress">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The absolute value formulation ensures the stress design criterion applies to positive tensile stress.</data>
  <data key="d9">applies to,covers</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897042</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Stress Design Criterion (|σ| ≤ σ_zul)" target="Compressive Stress">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The absolute value formulation ensures the stress design criterion applies to negative compressive stress.</data>
  <data key="d9">applies to,covers</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897042</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Stress Design Criterion (|σ| ≤ σ_zul)" target="Cross-Section Dimensioning">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The stress design criterion serves as the governing constraint for cross-section dimensioning (Querschnittsbemessung).</data>
  <data key="d9">constraint,governs</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897042</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Stress Design Criterion (|σ| ≤ σ_zul)" target="Von Mises">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The stress design criterion is foundational to more complex failure theories such as von Mises.</data>
  <data key="d9">extends,foundation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897042</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Stress Design Criterion (|σ| ≤ σ_zul)" target="Tresca">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The stress design criterion is foundational to more complex failure theories such as Tresca.</data>
  <data key="d9">extends,foundation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897042</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Stress Design Criterion (|σ| ≤ σ_zul)" target="Mohr-Coulomb">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The stress design criterion is foundational to more complex failure theories such as Mohr-Coulomb.</data>
  <data key="d9">extends,foundation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897042</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress (σ)" target="Internal Normal Force (N(x))">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Normal stress σ is computed using the internal normal force N(x) via the formula σ(x) = N(x)/A(x).</data>
  <data key="d9">computation,formula</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897042</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress (σ)" target="Cross-Sectional Area (A(x))">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Normal stress σ is computed using the cross-sectional area A(x) via the formula σ(x) = N(x)/A(x).</data>
  <data key="d9">computation,formula</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897042</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress (σ)" target="Stress Concentrations">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Stress concentrations (Spannungsspitzen) caused by geometric discontinuities can locally exceed stress predictions from elementary bar theory.</data>
  <data key="d9">exceeds,localized</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897042</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress (σ)" target="Elementary Bar Theory">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Elementary bar theory is used to make stress predictions that stress concentrations can locally exceed.</data>
  <data key="d9">predicts,theory</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-0fcadd4252fc137457a877dc2c0a4044</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897042</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress (σ)" target="Stress Equation (σ = N/A)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The stress equation defines normal stress σ as the result of the calculation</data>
  <data key="d9">calculates,defines</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897132</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress (σ)" target="Proportional Limit (σ_p)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The normal stress value is compared against the proportional limit to verify stresses remain within the linear-elastic range (σ ≤ σ_p)</data>
  <data key="d9">compared against,verified</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897132</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress (σ)" target="Yield Stress (σ_F)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The normal stress value is compared against the yield stress as a characteristic material limit</data>
  <data key="d9">compared against,material limit</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897132</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress (σ)" target="Bar">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Normal stress acts on the cross-section of a bar, describing how internal force distributes over the cross-section</data>
  <data key="d9">acts on,distributes over</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897132</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress (σ)" target="Linear-elastic Range">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Normal stress is verified to remain within the linear-elastic range (σ ≤ σ_p)</data>
  <data key="d9">remains in,verified within</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2b12d2d988ba8fdb94bda0bb9243511d</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel10_Elastostatik_Zug_und_Druck_in_Stäben.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784897132</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress (σ)" target="Cartesian Coordinate System">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The horizontal axis of the Cartesian coordinate system represents normal stress (σ).</data>
  <data key="d9">horizontal axis,represents</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5ca440e5727deb504109d3cda9b3642d</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904239</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress (σ)" target="σ1">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">σ1 represents the maximum principal stress, which is a specific value of normal stress.</data>
  <data key="d9">maximum principal stress</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5ca440e5727deb504109d3cda9b3642d</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904239</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Normal Stress (σ)" target="σ2">
