  <data key="d12">1784904249</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Thin-Walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel" target="Longitudinal Cracking">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Thin-walled cylindrical pressure vessels typically fail by longitudinal cracking.</data>
  <data key="d9">fails by,failure mode</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-1ee3c8346d073a8fddaf35535eb1158c</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904249</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Thin-Walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel" target="Maximum Shear Stress (τ_max)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The maximum shear stress occurs in the wall of a thin-walled cylindrical pressure vessel under internal pressure</data>
  <data key="d9">physical context,stress state</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904260</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Thin-Walled Cylindrical Pressure Vessel" target="Plane Stress State (ESZ)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The plane stress state is applicable to the thin-walled vessel wall where no shear stresses occur, making σ_x and σ_φ the principal stresses</data>
  <data key="d9">applicability,stress state</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904260</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Internal Pressure (p)" target="Longitudinal Stress (σ_x)">
  <data key="d7">3.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Longitudinal stress equals p·r/(2t), directly depending on internal pressure, vessel radius, and wall thickness&lt;SEP&gt;The internal pressure induces the longitudinal stress along the axis of the cylinder.&lt;SEP&gt;Internal pressure acting on the end caps induces longitudinal stresses within the vessel wall to maintain force equilibrium.</data>
  <data key="d9">causes,dependency,formula,induces</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-887f545fd528c5ed1b7980ed83618c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-290ff749460960cc500499bfce8172f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904162</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Internal Pressure (p)" target="Circumferential Stress (σ_φ)">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The internal pressure induces the circumferential or hoop stress around the perimeter of the cylinder.&lt;SEP&gt;Internal pressure acting on the projected rectangular area of the half-cylinder induces circumferential stresses across the cut surfaces of the vessel wall.</data>
  <data key="d9">causes,induces</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-887f545fd528c5ed1b7980ed83618c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-290ff749460960cc500499bfce8172f5</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904162</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Internal Pressure (p)" target="End Caps">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The internal pressure exerts a force directly acting upon the end caps of the cylindrical vessel.</data>
  <data key="d9">acts on</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-290ff749460960cc500499bfce8172f5</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904163</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Internal Pressure (p)" target="Projected Rectangular Area">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A prominent red arrow labeled 'p' represents the resultant force of the internal pressure acting on the projected rectangular area of the half-cylinder.</data>
  <data key="d9">acts on</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-290ff749460960cc500499bfce8172f5</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904164</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Internal Pressure (p)" target="Projected Circular Area">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The internal pressure p acts on the projected circular area πr² to generate the resultant force</data>
  <data key="d9">acts on</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-493acae899ca4eaaedf536afa97f481b</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904201</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Internal Pressure (p)" target="Hoop Stress (σ_φ)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Hoop stress equals p·r/t, directly depending on internal pressure, vessel radius, and wall thickness</data>
  <data key="d9">dependency,formula</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904260</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Internal Pressure (p)" target="Maximum Shear Stress (τ_max)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Maximum shear stress is directly proportional to internal pressure: τ_max = (1/4)·p·r/t</data>
  <data key="d9">direct dependency,formula</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-14e4d70cf188ab298b97a6d1cf9e80c8</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904261</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Longitudinal Stress (σ_x)" target="Circumferential Stress (σ_φ)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The circumferential stress is twice as large as the longitudinal stress, expressed mathematically as σ_φ = 2 · σ_x.</data>
  <data key="d9">ratio,relationship</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-290ff749460960cc500499bfce8172f5</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904163</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Longitudinal Stress (σ_x)" target="End Caps">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Longitudinal stresses develop within the vessel wall specifically to resist the pressure acting on the end caps.</data>
