<edge source="Stress Transformation Equations" target="Mohr's Circle">
  <data key="d7">3.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Stress transformation equations form the mathematical basis for Mohr's circle&lt;SEP&gt;Stress transformation equations involving trigonometric functions of 2φ form the analytical basis for Mohr's circle construction.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohr's Circle serves as the graphical-analytical representation of the stress transformation equations.</data>
  <data key="d9">basis for,foundation,graphical representation of</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-82d26e50b1a6b9a189224f9805e4217e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b625d3948531b3dcd9b53a158a3a5265</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904227</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Stress Transformation Equations" target="Angle-Doubling Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The angle-doubling property arises because stress transformation equations involve trigonometric functions of 2φ.</data>
  <data key="d9">arises from,caused by</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3cae0fdb4f5af9a553af5e5ec7a633b8</data>
  <data key="d11">Kapitel11_Spannungszustand.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1784904235</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Stress Transformation Equations" target="Equilibrium Condition For Shear Forces">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The stress transformation equations are derived by combining the shear stress equilibrium equation with the analogous normal stress equilibrium.</data>
